WEBVTT

03:37.345 --> 03:41.099
Good morning, everyone, and welcome to our special council

03:41.599 --> 03:45.334
meeting on April 8th, 2026. The purpose of this meeting is

03:45.366 --> 03:49.280
to discuss water-related matters. I will now call on Tim Brubacher,

03:49.296 --> 03:52.361
regional clerk, to conduct the roll call. Thank you. Chair Redman?

03:52.570 --> 03:56.022
Present. Councillor Craig? Councillor Erb? Present.

03:56.440 --> 03:59.378
Councillor Foxton? Present. Councillor Gowing?

03:59.892 --> 04:04.067
Present. Councillor Harris? Present. Councillor Huinink? I'm here.

04:05.030 --> 04:08.161
All right. Councillor James? Present. Councillor Liggett?

04:08.964 --> 04:12.239
Councillor McCabe? Present. Uh, Councillor Nowak just stepped

04:12.739 --> 04:14.230
out but is present. Councilolr Rodrigues?

04:16.800 --> 04:18.723
He's online. Councillor Salonen.

04:20.919 --> 04:23.260
Councillor Schantz. Present.

04:23.564 --> 04:26.931
Councillor Vrbanovic. Present. Councillor Wolf.

04:27.476 --> 04:31.419
Present. Thank you, Chair. I'd like

04:31.919 --> 04:35.154
to start by acknowledging that the land on which we gather today is the traditional

04:35.170 --> 04:38.809
territory of the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabe, and Chonnonton

04:38.857 --> 04:42.528
peoples. In doing so, I want to reflect on what it means to be doing

04:42.640 --> 04:45.942
important community work like we are engaging in collectively today,

04:46.632 --> 04:49.630
and also in my own individual relationship relationship to this land.

04:50.560 --> 04:53.961
As a settler, one of the most important aspects of my individual journey

04:54.089 --> 04:56.640
towards truth and reconciliation has been to listen.

04:57.538 --> 05:01.308
I am continually grateful to a community who shares their stories and

05:01.324 --> 05:04.580
concerns with me. As well as Chair of the

05:04.596 --> 05:08.061
Region, I know that I carry a large responsibility to ensure that it

05:08.561 --> 05:11.318
is not just myself on an individual level listening and acting,

05:11.991 --> 05:15.264
but that as a government, we are listening and creating action in the

05:15.764 --> 05:20.191
community. And with the community. I'm offering this acknowledgement

05:20.691 --> 05:24.236
as one of the many actions I am personally, and the region largely, is taking

05:24.333 --> 05:26.773
towards truth and reconciliation.

05:27.639 --> 05:31.010
We acknowledge the enduring presence of the Indigenous people with whom we

05:31.510 --> 05:35.152
share this land today, their achievements and their contributions to

05:35.280 --> 05:39.053
our community. Are there any declarations of pecuniary interest?

05:41.460 --> 05:44.797
Seeing none, there are no presentation. We do

05:45.297 --> 05:48.610
have delegations. I'm going to proceed to them as listed on

05:48.658 --> 05:52.391
the agenda, but I would like to remind all delegations of some guidelines

05:52.408 --> 05:55.804
for participation. If you're attending in person,

05:55.868 --> 05:58.912
please proceed to the podium when you are called upon to speak.

05:59.698 --> 06:03.607
I will note that we have a new accessible podium that has the ability

06:03.703 --> 06:07.228
to be lowered and raised using the buttons on the right.

06:07.789 --> 06:11.730
If you're attending virtually, please turn your microphone on and camera when you

06:12.230 --> 06:15.437
are called upon. You will have 5 minutes to speak if you are

06:15.501 --> 06:19.403
registered before the deadline and 3 minutes if you are registered afterwards.

06:20.157 --> 06:23.786
When the light on the podium turns yellow, that means you have

06:23.834 --> 06:26.949
1 minute left to speak. When the light turns

06:27.046 --> 06:30.193
red, that means your time is up and you need to sum up.

06:31.076 --> 06:34.737
You may hear me give you a reminding— a reminder about the remaining

06:34.833 --> 06:37.755
time. Additionally, some general reminders.

06:38.317 --> 06:42.058
If you conduct yourself in any of the following manners, it will result

06:42.075 --> 06:45.896
in immediate removal from the meeting. Interrupting councillors,

06:45.928 --> 06:49.781
staff, or other delegations, derogatory statements

06:50.281 --> 06:54.261
about councillors, staff, or others, turning your camera

06:54.742 --> 06:59.093
on at inappropriate times, and hate speech or defamatory comments.

06:59.896 --> 07:03.412
One last reminder for delegations: please do not take offense

07:03.912 --> 07:06.977
if following your presentation there are no questions from council.

07:07.522 --> 07:11.665
This is council's time to really listen and reflect upon the opinions and ideas

07:12.018 --> 07:14.474
shared with us from our community members.

07:16.432 --> 07:19.465
The first delegation is Tom Chesnovick,

07:20.107 --> 07:23.156
Chair of Nith Valley EcoBoosters. Tom,

07:23.172 --> 07:26.863
proceed to the podium, and you have 5 minutes.

07:27.168 --> 07:27.954
The floor is yours.

07:35.866 --> 07:39.429
Okay, thank you. Good morning, Regional Chair Redman.

07:39.782 --> 07:42.682
Regional councillors and staff members of the public.

07:43.547 --> 07:45.871
My name is Tom Knezovich. You were close.

07:47.697 --> 07:51.335
And I am a member of the Nith Valley EcoBoosters, a local volunteer

07:51.399 --> 07:55.341
organization that has worked for over 10 years to achieve and support

07:55.841 --> 07:58.946
a long-term healthy environment in Wilmot and Wellesley townships

07:59.330 --> 08:01.926
through education, action, and collaboration.

08:02.679 --> 08:06.044
Today, I would like to speak to you about protecting the natural heritage

08:06.544 --> 08:09.906
of this region. And will be sharing aerial and ground-level

08:10.406 --> 08:14.392
photographic confirmation regarding the Shingletown wetland that

08:14.892 --> 08:18.927
is missing from the staff report that was presented at the February 25th council meeting.

08:20.065 --> 08:23.894
At that meeting, Mayor Shantz asked if the region's pumping caused

08:24.007 --> 08:25.865
the Shingletown wetland to dry up.

08:27.067 --> 08:30.656
Staff called it multi-factor— multifactorial.

08:31.201 --> 08:34.888
However, over 70 years of photo history shows that

08:35.388 --> 08:39.241
this wetland has survived every major drought since 1950

08:39.883 --> 08:43.465
until the region's experimental pumping began in 2019.

08:44.541 --> 08:47.657
This ecosystem has been remarkably resilient.

08:48.300 --> 08:51.994
It rebounded after significant water was taken to create

08:52.494 --> 08:56.668
cattle feedlot manure lagoons between 1965 and 1987,

08:57.294 --> 09:01.421
and it survived the severe drought years of 2012 2016.

09:02.448 --> 09:06.525
However, following the region's recent optimization and

09:06.605 --> 09:10.778
experimental pumping peaks, we are seeing a decline that

09:11.278 --> 09:13.972
over 70 years of history suggests is unprecedented.

09:14.967 --> 09:18.306
For the first time in decades, this wetland is failing to rebound.

09:19.381 --> 09:23.634
This isn't a climate cycle. It appears to be a direct response created

09:24.134 --> 09:27.712
by municipal extraction of water. May I have

09:28.212 --> 09:28.418
the first slide, please?

09:47.861 --> 09:51.136
And I know this is going to be difficult to see from— for some

09:51.233 --> 09:54.604
of you from where you are, but the councillors have

09:55.104 --> 09:57.203
a copy of this so they will be able to see this a little better.

09:58.470 --> 10:01.855
In this first slide, it is an aerial photo showing a

10:01.983 --> 10:05.881
healthy Shingletown wetland in 1955.

10:06.555 --> 10:09.442
And there's a red triangle, which I hope some of you can see,

10:09.779 --> 10:13.051
that highlights the wetland. And also the

10:13.388 --> 10:16.741
location of the Haldimand gravel pit is also highlighted in the photo.

10:17.976 --> 10:21.071
So I know it's kind of hard to see, but it's a healthy wetland that

10:21.571 --> 10:23.461
we're seeing in 1955. Next slide, please.

10:27.162 --> 10:30.560
The next slide is a 1977 photo, so we

10:30.624 --> 10:34.342
are 22 years later, and this

10:34.486 --> 10:38.893
photo is showing the effect of excessive pumping, which started in 1965

10:39.213 --> 10:42.482
to fill the manure lagoon and irrigate the cattle lands. So we're

10:42.982 --> 10:46.585
starting to see that negative effects on the wetland.

10:47.530 --> 10:51.190
Next slide, please. This slide is

10:51.206 --> 10:54.595
taken in the year 2000, and the wetland

10:55.095 --> 10:58.577
continues to show the negative effect of the excessive manure lagoon pumping,

10:59.075 --> 11:02.432
even though it ended in 1987. Next slide, please.

11:05.355 --> 11:07.716
This photo is taken in 2010,

11:08.551 --> 11:12.100
and this is a great indication of how— showing how the wetland survived

11:12.600 --> 11:15.441
and recovered from the excessive manure lagoon pumping.

11:15.553 --> 11:19.709
So even though there was excessive pumping done, the wetland

11:20.209 --> 11:21.857
rebounded at that time. Next slide, please.

11:23.813 --> 11:28.029
This is a 2017 photo that demonstrates again how that wetland rebounded

11:28.045 --> 11:31.716
from a very significant drought in 2016. So we're seeing

11:31.748 --> 11:35.868
that pattern of the wetland being able to rebound after considerable

11:36.333 --> 11:38.129
stresses. Next slide, please.

11:40.646 --> 11:44.797
This is a 2019 photo. The wetland

11:45.297 --> 11:48.723
looks healthy at this point, but it's at the point where the— this is the

11:49.223 --> 11:52.537
year when the Region's experimental pumping started. So we're okay here.

11:53.226 --> 11:56.879
But next slide, please, is showing 2 years later in 2021.

11:57.857 --> 12:01.799
And in 2021, we're starting to see the negative effects of the experimental

12:01.863 --> 12:04.603
pumping. And final slide— sorry, next slide.

12:05.116 --> 12:09.010
2022, we continue to see the negative effects of the Region's

12:09.510 --> 12:12.091
experimental pumping. On the wetland drying up.

12:13.006 --> 12:16.459
I must say that in 2022 there was also significant drought

12:16.959 --> 12:20.378
that year, so there was a double whammy that year. And final slide,

12:21.598 --> 12:25.163
uh, no, and still not final, 2025, uh, we're starting to

12:25.663 --> 12:29.194
see the wetland continuing to dry up. And final slide,

12:29.227 --> 12:32.519
2026, these were taken, um, just a

12:33.019 --> 12:36.887
few weeks ago on March the 30th, and we're continuing to see the wetland

12:37.096 --> 12:40.364
failing to rebound from the— for the first time in decades.

12:41.662 --> 12:45.348
The loss of the Shingletown Wetland will result in a loss

12:45.848 --> 12:48.536
of the benefits that it has provided to the community for many years.

12:49.450 --> 12:52.654
These benefits include providing a habitat for wildlife,

12:52.735 --> 12:57.141
including species at risk and migratory birds, filtering pollutants,

12:57.782 --> 13:01.980
buffering against flooding and drought, and absorbing and storing carbon,

13:02.253 --> 13:05.685
reducing the effects of climate change. In addition,

13:06.038 --> 13:10.164
a 2022 study completed by the Institute for Wetland and Waterfowl

13:10.357 --> 13:14.435
Research, Ducks Unlimited Canada, confirmed that restored

13:14.468 --> 13:18.610
wetlands can reduce phosphorus and nitrogen loads entering streams

13:18.963 --> 13:22.496
and rivers across the working landscape of southwestern Ontario.

13:23.700 --> 13:27.361
The 1980 Wilmot Water Agreement was put in place to

13:27.393 --> 13:30.892
prevent exactly what we are seeing today. The Nith

13:30.924 --> 13:34.418
Valley EcoBoosters ask that council reject the recommendation

13:34.931 --> 13:39.131
to rescind these protections. We need a watershed-first

13:39.195 --> 13:43.283
approach that respects the historical baseline of this wetland

13:43.652 --> 13:46.601
before we commit to higher pumping values— volumes.

13:47.675 --> 13:51.042
Thank you for the opportunity to present to you on this important topic.

13:52.725 --> 13:56.748
Thank you very much, Tom. I gave you a couple extra, a few extra seconds

13:57.248 --> 14:00.868
because it took a while to get your slides up. So thank you for finishing

14:00.916 --> 14:04.046
in a expeditious manner.

14:04.110 --> 14:07.659
We have some questions. Councillor Salonen. Uh, thank you

14:08.159 --> 14:11.881
very much, Tom, for coming and for representing, um, the Nith Valley

14:11.978 --> 14:15.414
EcoBoosters on this matter. I'm curious,

14:15.430 --> 14:19.010
and I really appreciate the images that you provided and

14:19.042 --> 14:22.880
the context too for when the manure lagoons were there. I think

14:23.380 --> 14:26.798
that's really important. I'm curious, in your read-through

14:27.215 --> 14:30.843
of the report that council got, and I to be fair, it was

14:30.859 --> 14:35.476
a lot of pages. I don't know if you got through it all, but it's

14:35.976 --> 14:39.291
claimed that it's not actually an impact from the pumping.

14:39.403 --> 14:43.122
I was just curious. I don't personally agree with it,

14:43.186 --> 14:46.906
but on if your group had any thoughts based off

14:46.970 --> 14:48.188
of the data that you've shown,

14:51.186 --> 14:54.745
um, I guess all I can say is that if

14:55.245 --> 14:59.184
we take a look at the the photographs and the rebounding

14:59.216 --> 15:02.645
that has happened over many years. The wetland was able

15:02.725 --> 15:06.042
to rebound from that excessive pumping for the cattle,

15:07.468 --> 15:10.833
the manure lagoons, and also from severe droughts.

15:11.938 --> 15:15.143
And so, and there may be a few people that are going to

15:15.643 --> 15:19.165
delegate after me today that can address that better because they

15:19.665 --> 15:23.091
live in that area more directly than I do, but I think

15:23.591 --> 15:27.307
I would just go on reflecting on what has happened in the past

15:28.204 --> 15:31.635
and every time it has rebounded. So something's different. So the only thing

15:31.731 --> 15:35.563
I can point to is different is the excessive experimental

15:35.595 --> 15:38.785
pumping. So I guess that, that's about all I could say about it. Thank you

15:39.285 --> 15:42.568
very much. You're welcome. Councillor Hunnicke. Thank you for coming today.

15:42.712 --> 15:45.838
Thank you for the historical review of what it used to be and what it

15:46.338 --> 15:49.958
is currently. Um, you mentioned something really interesting to

15:50.458 --> 15:54.014
me, which is a watershed-first approach. Can you explain more what

15:54.514 --> 15:57.948
you mean by that? Does that mean no pumping at

15:58.448 --> 16:00.999
all? Does that— yeah, parameters of that.

16:01.722 --> 16:05.577
Yeah, I think when you take a look at the benefits, and I only

16:06.077 --> 16:09.608
mentioned very briefly some of the benefits that were shown from the study that

16:10.108 --> 16:14.073
was done by Ducks Unlimited in terms of reductions in phosphorus

16:14.186 --> 16:17.334
and nitrogen, nitrates, etc. So I

16:17.398 --> 16:22.872
think watershed first to me means The

16:22.936 --> 16:26.752
people that live in that area are seeing—

16:27.249 --> 16:31.482
have received the benefits of that watershed

16:31.498 --> 16:34.865
for many, many years. For example, just filtering of pollutants,

16:34.897 --> 16:38.168
et cetera, but also in terms of the water that

16:38.668 --> 16:42.465
they are getting and have always gotten for many, many years.

16:42.962 --> 16:46.666
And so that people in that area that live in that area, the first

16:47.166 --> 16:50.467
thing that they're concerned about is making sure that they have access to water.

16:51.157 --> 16:54.863
They're not going to worry about their wells drying up, etc. And so

16:54.927 --> 16:58.586
the watershed-first approach means take care of the people that

16:58.602 --> 17:01.393
live in the area, make sure that they get water,

17:01.891 --> 17:05.902
and then secondly, make sure you're maintaining a natural wetland

17:05.918 --> 17:09.689
that is doing all these wonderful things to reduce the effects of climate change

17:10.189 --> 17:15.786
and to reduce pollutants, etc. So that's how I look at the— does

17:16.286 --> 17:19.458
that mean no pumping at all? Again, I think

17:19.958 --> 17:22.569
it depends on what effect the pumping is having on the people that live in

17:22.601 --> 17:26.658
the area and the loss of the ability

17:26.690 --> 17:30.058
to filter pollutants and affect climate change, et cetera.

17:30.763 --> 17:33.409
Okay. So it depends on how much pumping. Thank you.

17:33.906 --> 17:37.001
You're welcome. Thank you, Tom. I have no further questions.

17:37.354 --> 17:40.385
Thank you. The next delegation is Laverne Forwell,

17:40.401 --> 17:44.137
Citizens for a Safe Groundwater. Laverne, I believe you're here in person.

17:44.780 --> 17:46.706
You have the floor and you have 5 minutes. Welcome.

18:01.989 --> 18:05.071
To the chair and councillors, my name is Laverne Forwell.

18:06.548 --> 18:10.224
I have lived as a farming resident on Whitmer Road

18:10.273 --> 18:13.660
for 35 years. At this time,

18:15.554 --> 18:19.488
I have seen significant changes, including several gravel pits,

18:20.371 --> 18:24.063
one being my neighbor now. I understand

18:24.563 --> 18:27.402
growth, but I also understand poor decisions,

18:28.301 --> 18:29.682
and that's why I'm here today.

18:34.964 --> 18:38.319
I'm having to speak about water, but more importantly,

18:39.796 --> 18:42.333
about trust and accountability.

18:44.533 --> 18:48.852
Because what we're facing today is not just water issues, it's a breach

18:48.948 --> 18:52.561
of a longstanding commitment to our community.

18:54.729 --> 18:59.562
To understand why this matters, we need to go back to

19:00.062 --> 19:02.805
1970. Wilmot Township was in crisis.

19:05.727 --> 19:09.289
Council records show that pumping had already dropped the

19:09.337 --> 19:12.707
water level table by 11 feet.

19:13.173 --> 19:17.088
Residents were facing real hardship for drinking

19:17.104 --> 19:18.485
water and for livestock.

19:21.052 --> 19:24.069
At this point, I would like you to look at the screen,

19:25.690 --> 19:29.092
and, uh, you can't read it, but if

19:29.140 --> 19:32.447
you can scroll through the various Pages like that.

19:33.731 --> 19:37.424
These are all council meetings going back

19:37.924 --> 19:42.064
from 1970. It was a lot of time

19:44.488 --> 19:48.518
and a lot of stressful delegation to

19:48.566 --> 19:51.697
create this 1980 water

19:52.197 --> 19:56.016
policy. This policy became a commitment

19:56.369 --> 19:59.678
to protect Wilmot Township for the long term.

20:01.751 --> 20:04.450
And that, for decades, that promise held.

20:05.285 --> 20:07.759
Water was taken and it was capped.

20:09.848 --> 20:13.238
Beginning in 2019, the region began

20:13.738 --> 20:16.917
exceeding these limits through what they described

20:17.417 --> 20:19.391
as experimental pumping.

20:21.271 --> 20:25.255
Another word comes to mind when I think about someone taking from

20:25.303 --> 20:27.743
a neighbor without proper notification.

20:29.381 --> 20:33.283
There was no communication with the township or

20:33.379 --> 20:37.169
the residents. Now in 2026,

20:37.650 --> 20:40.508
we are seeing the consequences.

20:43.110 --> 20:47.220
What residents are reporting water issues such

20:47.720 --> 20:51.331
as sediment Well interference

20:52.005 --> 20:54.301
and shortage and ponds going dry.

20:57.464 --> 21:00.740
For 43 years, I, as an outdoorsman,

21:01.478 --> 21:04.625
had access with permission to

21:05.125 --> 21:07.965
the Wilmot Wetlands or Shingletown Wetlands.

21:10.085 --> 21:13.232
The water average over those years were

21:13.681 --> 21:17.326
about 4 feet. Now the

21:17.826 --> 21:22.063
several— last several years I have seen it slowly decreasing to

21:22.111 --> 21:25.611
what you see in the slide there.

21:27.891 --> 21:29.385
I believe that's not the right one.

21:34.732 --> 21:36.273
It's a sort of a grassland.

21:38.665 --> 21:39.115
Keep going.

21:49.166 --> 21:49.648
There you go.

21:52.699 --> 21:56.231
That was a week ago Sunday. That was the

21:56.731 --> 21:59.057
extent of the water, and it had rained for a day and a half.

22:00.245 --> 22:03.200
I was walking in the middle of it with my running shoes on.

22:10.529 --> 22:13.830
This is no— this is a long-term risk

22:14.455 --> 22:17.099
taking us back to the 1970 crisis,

22:18.220 --> 22:21.874
and now there's a decision about to remove

22:21.986 --> 22:25.560
the policy that was designed to prevent this.

22:26.617 --> 22:30.127
So the question becomes, after a decade of work to

22:30.159 --> 22:33.973
prevent another crisis, Is the solution

22:34.005 --> 22:35.977
really to remove the safeguard?

22:39.937 --> 22:43.784
This issue extends beyond Wilmot.

22:43.944 --> 22:47.631
It affects residents, farmers, and businesses, and wildlife,

22:49.298 --> 22:52.617
and the growth of the region, because water

22:53.117 --> 22:56.720
is not optional infrastructure.

22:57.249 --> 23:00.957
It is fundamental. So today I'm asking

23:01.021 --> 23:03.397
for 3 things. First, transparency.

23:04.376 --> 23:07.941
Clear public reporting of water levels

23:08.246 --> 23:09.900
since 2019.

23:11.152 --> 23:15.150
Accountability. A clear acknowledgement to

23:15.650 --> 23:19.774
where limits were exceeded and the correction action

23:19.870 --> 23:23.531
that will be taken. Third, do not weaken or

23:23.563 --> 23:26.882
remove the protection of the 1980 agreement

23:27.828 --> 23:30.761
without equal or stronger safeguards.

23:31.899 --> 23:35.088
Because once trust is broken, it is not easily

23:35.184 --> 23:37.412
rebuilt. So, councillors,

23:38.647 --> 23:42.109
some point in all of your lives you will need trust.

23:43.679 --> 23:47.190
And right now, the people of Wilmot Township are asking.

23:48.985 --> 23:52.142
It's longstanding. So, Laverne, you're out of time. If you want to sum

23:52.190 --> 23:56.234
up, please. If longstanding

23:56.298 --> 24:00.037
agreements cannot be exceeded without

24:00.537 --> 24:04.274
clear transparency, if the 1980 agreement cannot be trusted,

24:05.574 --> 24:09.490
what protection do we have to

24:09.667 --> 24:13.904
honour the agreement and the commitment that

24:13.920 --> 24:17.547
were made and to ensure the

24:18.047 --> 24:21.719
decisions made today do not create the crisis of tomorrow.

24:23.563 --> 24:26.451
Thank you. Thank you, Laverne. We do have questions.

24:26.515 --> 24:29.772
Councillor Salonen. Thank you very much, Laverne, for coming in this

24:29.884 --> 24:33.590
morning and for sharing all the

24:34.090 --> 24:37.744
information that you found. I'm curious if you think

24:38.244 --> 24:41.771
that the original motion— and I appreciate the historic context

24:42.271 --> 24:46.327
you found, I also stumbled across that in our archives— But

24:46.827 --> 24:50.407
I'm curious if you think that that policy of 1980 was put in

24:50.439 --> 24:53.620
place much like the other thinking of the region

24:54.214 --> 24:59.033
that's forward-thinking, like putting in the Countryside

24:59.533 --> 25:02.598
Line, for example. So putting in our own additional

25:03.691 --> 25:07.353
backstops to ensure good protection of our resources.

25:07.626 --> 25:11.208
Is that your interpretation of this policy? Yes, I'm sure it was

25:11.320 --> 25:14.692
because people then seen what we're seeing today.

25:15.639 --> 25:19.300
And, uh, and what we're seeing today

25:19.429 --> 25:23.571
is— in some of those other pictures were springs

25:23.828 --> 25:27.907
that are not running anymore. It's, it's very

25:27.923 --> 25:31.423
obvious to anybody that looks

25:31.696 --> 25:35.261
at the water levels and the ponds that

25:35.309 --> 25:39.193
we have a problem. Thank you very much again for coming in today.

25:39.562 --> 25:43.797
Councillor Hunnicutt. Thank you for coming today. I think what you and the previous delegate

25:44.070 --> 25:47.727
have successfully shown is that the experimental pumping that we've done

25:47.760 --> 25:51.690
for 7 years is actually producing negative impacts

25:52.190 --> 25:55.989
for the population that lives there, and I don't want to make those impacts permanent.

25:56.038 --> 25:59.551
So, um, thank you for demonstrating that for us

26:00.051 --> 26:03.273
today. Councillor Schantz.

26:05.566 --> 26:08.901
Thank you. Thanks for presenting to all the presenters

26:08.917 --> 26:12.349
who have and are going to present. I'm trying

26:12.381 --> 26:16.390
to understand the depth of the pond. I think

26:16.454 --> 26:20.030
I heard you say it was about 4 feet. Was that—

26:20.944 --> 26:24.664
and I think I heard that it was at its largest

26:25.164 --> 26:27.102
about 7 acres in size.

26:28.817 --> 26:32.731
Would it have been about 4 feet through that whole 7

26:33.231 --> 26:36.485
acres, or like, can you just give me some, some sense of,

26:36.822 --> 26:39.453
of what the extent of it was?

26:41.715 --> 26:45.051
When I originally went out there, I had a

26:45.551 --> 26:50.233
duck blind, probably the distance of the diameter

26:50.733 --> 26:54.404
of this facility here, and you had

26:54.904 --> 26:57.452
to walk through 4 feet of water to get to it.

26:59.442 --> 27:03.213
There probably was deeper areas, but— and

27:03.713 --> 27:08.171
then it tapered to the edges. But on average, I would have assumed

27:08.187 --> 27:11.011
that that's what the depth would have been, 4 to 5 feet.

27:14.060 --> 27:17.558
Okay, if I could just follow up to that, um,

27:18.617 --> 27:22.533
is there any sense of, uh, of silt

27:22.709 --> 27:26.192
migrating to that area And filling

27:26.256 --> 27:30.495
it in, or can you speak to that at all?

27:32.663 --> 27:36.308
No, I moved out there 35 years ago. I'm right next to it.

27:36.597 --> 27:40.515
Like I say, I'm next to the gravel pit, which part of it is on

27:41.510 --> 27:44.208
a small corner, but it is on the gravel pit property,

27:44.481 --> 27:48.398
and the rest is on people

27:48.898 --> 27:53.054
behind me. And no,

27:53.230 --> 27:56.745
I got to go over there on a pretty regular basis just to check out

27:56.825 --> 28:00.549
the wildlife, and I didn't

28:01.049 --> 28:03.406
see any silting coming into it.

28:06.134 --> 28:09.488
Thank you. Thank you, Laverne. I see no further

28:09.521 --> 28:12.939
questions, so thank you for your delegation. The next delegation is

28:13.035 --> 28:15.908
Greg Caster from Wellmet. Greg, are you here in person?

28:18.184 --> 28:21.325
Welcome. Uh, the floor is yours and you have 5 minutes.

28:31.629 --> 28:34.097
Madam Chairman Redmond, mayors,

28:34.594 --> 28:37.654
councillors, staff, I'm here to give a,

28:37.959 --> 28:41.580
a historical perspective from a community that went

28:42.080 --> 28:45.636
through this over 40 years ago. I've been a resident

28:45.748 --> 28:47.512
in a little town called Rosebank.

28:48.233 --> 28:50.606
Rosebank exists on Huron Road,

28:51.359 --> 28:54.469
which is near Dundee Country Club. Uh,

28:54.565 --> 28:56.778
we have a major regional well,

28:56.970 --> 29:00.272
K-26, located on Mannheim Road

29:00.898 --> 29:05.354
around the corner from us. And this is a historical

29:05.386 --> 29:09.698
perspective that I hope will help the Shingletown

29:10.198 --> 29:13.448
people realize what they're likely going to be up against.

29:14.185 --> 29:18.127
You can imagine waking up one morning, turning the tap

29:18.627 --> 29:22.293
on, and nothing happens. Your response

29:22.793 --> 29:25.994
would be, 

29:26.058 --> 29:28.270
pumps, you check wiring,

29:29.263 --> 29:32.740
and then as a last thought you think, 

29:33.253 --> 29:36.654
and you open up the lid and there's absolutely nothing there.

29:38.003 --> 29:41.230
I want to give a historical perspective of what a

29:41.730 --> 29:45.967
neighbor and I went through. His name is Jim Sterling. He lives about 100

29:46.497 --> 29:48.954
feet from us in an old schoolhouse.

29:50.126 --> 29:53.546
We had a 10-foot hand-dug well with a 3-foot

29:53.578 --> 29:57.769
diameter that used to supply 2 households

29:58.475 --> 30:03.319
and 20 head of cattle. And then in 1987,

30:04.169 --> 30:07.936
it started to go dry. And one day it did. So I

30:08.436 --> 30:11.447
had to ask the tenant with the cattle that he had to move them out

30:11.848 --> 30:15.567
because we were short of water. A next-door neighbor had drilled their

30:15.615 --> 30:18.934
own well, so they were no longer associated with our

30:19.062 --> 30:23.407
well. And so with one household of myself,

30:23.487 --> 30:26.805
my wife, and three children, our well

30:27.237 --> 30:29.945
was continually at a very low level.

30:30.923 --> 30:34.624
Mr. Sterling's well, who was 42 feet deep,

30:34.688 --> 30:36.403
hand-dug well, uh,

30:37.412 --> 30:40.169
also was going through similar situations.

30:41.258 --> 30:45.040
Uh, we went to the Water Liaison

30:45.540 --> 30:48.853
Committee at the urging of 3 of our councillors in

30:48.966 --> 30:52.859
Wilmot Township, and I'd just like to— this is a historical

30:53.277 --> 30:57.757
Maybe some of you folks won't know these names. There was a fellow, Lynn Myers,

30:57.869 --> 31:02.799
who was the mayor. There was Councillor Ralph Schantz

31:03.474 --> 31:06.862
and Councillor Bill Weichel, who encouraged us to go to this water

31:07.362 --> 31:10.395
liaison committee and put a claim in.

31:11.342 --> 31:15.148
And we thought, okay, that's a good way to go. We went to that

31:15.357 --> 31:18.842
and it was very enlightening to

31:19.342 --> 31:22.584
see how things happen. And I thought, 40 years ago,

31:22.664 --> 31:26.872
I'm going to keep— these books were what was generated

31:27.660 --> 31:31.547
by a consulting company to tell us that we

31:31.595 --> 31:34.213
were not being affected by water pumping.

31:35.835 --> 31:39.883
I finally, after a couple more meetings

31:40.895 --> 31:44.477
and Mr. Sterling's 42-foot well going completely dry

31:44.686 --> 31:48.600
when they were gone for a month, The region decided that

31:49.100 --> 31:52.463
it would perhaps be appropriate that they drilled

31:52.963 --> 31:56.775
a well for us, which they did. We are now located at 142

31:57.275 --> 32:00.397
feet. Mr. Sterling is at 190 feet,

32:00.413 --> 32:04.164
so a little different elevations. We both

32:04.664 --> 32:08.475
have water that needs to be treated for iron. His needs to be treated

32:08.636 --> 32:11.024
for carbon,

32:11.521 --> 32:14.984
and he's also got a reverse osmosis system

32:15.065 --> 32:18.405
put on his, which he's had to pick up the cost of.

32:19.545 --> 32:23.703
Well, here is my concern. My concern is that

32:24.940 --> 32:26.754
when this research was done,

32:28.456 --> 32:32.053
the consultants came in and used that as their baseline.

32:32.968 --> 32:36.420
So their comment was, your well isn't producing enough

32:36.920 --> 32:41.525
water, which I already knew. So historically,

32:42.905 --> 32:46.371
I've lived there 70 years, I've seen all these changes take

32:46.871 --> 32:49.517
place. So, uh,

32:51.491 --> 32:55.487
is the process still happening? I recently talked to two more neighbors

32:56.064 --> 32:59.675
who have had to drill wells too. They had hand-dug wells and

32:59.771 --> 33:03.591
they have gone completely dry. They have put out one— in one

33:03.703 --> 33:06.951
case, a single person in one house has

33:06.999 --> 33:10.218
had to put out $55,000 to

33:10.234 --> 33:13.291
get a water system that she can operate off of.

33:14.617 --> 33:17.834
That's a lot of money for one person

33:18.616 --> 33:20.963
to have to deal with.

33:22.620 --> 33:25.971
So here's another issue. How much time do I have,

33:26.035 --> 33:29.354
Chair?

33:29.613 --> 33:33.346
5 seconds remaining. 5 seconds. Time goes

33:33.379 --> 33:37.312
fast. Okay. The other issue I was gonna bring up was Alder Creek.

33:37.456 --> 33:40.981
Alder Creek is a stream that runs through our

33:41.481 --> 33:44.651
farm. It has 3 tributaries. It has one that comes from a

33:45.151 --> 33:48.080
trout farm, one that comes from Dundee Country Club,

33:48.497 --> 33:52.247
and one that comes from Mannheim, where it used to

33:52.279 --> 33:56.013
run the sawmill back in the '50s. That stream that comes

33:56.109 --> 33:59.201
from Mannheim is almost non-existent now,

33:59.907 --> 34:03.786
so Water levels have been affected. There have been

34:03.818 --> 34:07.248
many streams, springs along Alder

34:07.748 --> 34:12.106
Creek that have completely dried up. So I think we're into an issue where

34:13.500 --> 34:16.947
we have some major concerns. Could I, at the chair's

34:17.447 --> 34:20.810
discretion, just read my final comments? Yes, if you would sum up,

34:20.874 --> 34:25.026
that would be great. Okay, here's what I would like to ask. There's monitoring stations

34:25.844 --> 34:29.613
in the township that I would like to see the information be

34:30.113 --> 34:33.223
released to the community. I would

34:33.723 --> 34:37.105
like to also— like, there's a monitoring station at the

34:38.052 --> 34:41.565
bridge on Alder Creek that I'd like to have that

34:42.065 --> 34:47.244
information released. I'd like to see the 6-month plan

34:47.357 --> 34:50.886
put in place that no further water is taken from

34:51.386 --> 34:52.875
Shingletown. I feel for you folks.

34:55.216 --> 34:58.791
There needs to be another solution for water,

34:58.999 --> 35:02.990
and I hope we are doing some thinking in terms of what else could

35:03.490 --> 35:07.158
we possibly do rather than just taking more water from Wilmot

35:07.222 --> 35:11.086
Township. That will be an issue if we continue

35:11.586 --> 35:14.692
to do that. Thank you. Uh,

35:14.773 --> 35:18.331
Greg, don't go away. Uh, Councillor Salonen. Thank you.

35:18.443 --> 35:21.249
Um, and Greg, you actually got to it, but I was going to ask you,

35:21.569 --> 35:24.727
um, because I know Aldercreek runs near, uh, where you

35:24.791 --> 35:28.051
are are the impacts, but I think you've already kind of outlined

35:28.147 --> 35:31.150
them, so that was going to be my question. But thank you very much for

35:31.198 --> 35:34.682
coming in today. I appreciate it. Thank you. I see

35:35.182 --> 35:38.841
no further questions. Thank you for your delegation. The next delegate is Samantha

35:39.341 --> 35:42.711
Lernout, uh, President Citizens

35:42.759 --> 35:44.107
for Safe Groundwater.

35:47.608 --> 35:49.374
Samantha, are you in person or online?

35:53.110 --> 35:56.459
Apparently that delegate has withdrawn,

35:58.110 --> 36:01.876
so the next delegate is Ernie Dreher

36:02.036 --> 36:03.029
from Wilmont.

36:07.372 --> 36:09.872
Ernie, welcome to the podium, and you have 5 minutes.

36:14.263 --> 36:17.872
Good morning. I've lived at Sand

36:18.372 --> 36:22.288
Hills Road for 68 years. I never had

36:22.788 --> 36:26.286
any issues— cattle, pigs, everything. And in

36:26.398 --> 36:30.140
2020, I started getting sand

36:30.252 --> 36:33.656
in all my taps, showers, and everything.

36:35.230 --> 36:39.115
Then I put in a filtering system in August

36:39.163 --> 36:42.559
'21, and it helped some. But it didn't stop.

36:42.881 --> 36:46.169
The problems continued. I sold the

36:46.669 --> 36:51.135
farm to my son in 2022,

36:52.236 --> 36:55.281
and on March 23rd, he said,

36:55.314 --> 37:00.234
37:00.734 --> 37:03.913

we had to bail water for over a week till I got somebody in,

37:04.574 --> 37:08.046
and I had to drill a new well. And I

37:08.094 --> 37:12.154
installed it on March 23rd, or yeah, March 23rd,

37:12.988 --> 37:16.052
and there's been no problem since. But though,

37:16.838 --> 37:20.496
you know, I've lived there that long and had livestock and no problem,

37:21.074 --> 37:23.898
and now I'm short of water, no water, you know.

37:25.486 --> 37:29.353
And you know, it's the future of the kids and stuff and the

37:29.853 --> 37:32.481
township water. That's all I have to say.

37:34.471 --> 37:38.388
Thank you. Thank you, Ernie, for coming in. I, um, hang on. Councillor Salonen.

37:39.287 --> 37:42.353
Thank you very much, Ernie. I'm curious, um, I know

37:42.369 --> 37:46.478
that you already drilled a new well, but have you reported that well

37:46.542 --> 37:49.062
interference through the Region?

37:50.603 --> 37:54.231
Did I record it? Did you report the well interference to the

37:54.295 --> 37:57.971
Region? Just, just lately since I heard this,

37:58.227 --> 38:01.518
yes. Okay, great. So you have added in? Yes. Claim for that.

38:01.614 --> 38:04.954
Okay, thank you very much for coming today. Thank you,

38:04.986 --> 38:09.514
Ernie. The next delegation is Michael Friend, Associate Hydrogeologist

38:09.562 --> 38:13.127
and Groundwater Modulator, Friend Consulting

38:13.897 --> 38:17.751
Associates. I believe that you're virtual,

38:17.767 --> 38:18.473
is that correct?

38:21.123 --> 38:24.318
Michael, welcome. Can you hear me? Can you hear me? We can

38:24.818 --> 38:26.357
hear you, and you have 5 minutes. Please go ahead.

38:27.721 --> 38:32.407
Okay, what I wanted to speak about is

38:32.519 --> 38:36.756
the issue that the— focusing more on the study that

38:37.256 --> 38:40.238
has come through from— done by SLR.

38:41.570 --> 38:45.566
The concern I have with that study is that it doesn't address the— it basically

38:45.582 --> 38:48.615
cherry-picks the datasets to be from what the region has,

38:49.529 --> 38:53.108
but it doesn't give insights into the wells from

38:53.220 --> 38:54.953
the residents themselves.

38:56.093 --> 38:59.704
And when you look at a set of data,

39:00.266 --> 39:04.182
like every well, including a well that serves

39:04.214 --> 39:07.536
as a residential well, by default it

39:07.584 --> 39:11.372
can also serve as a monitoring well. You can have a well that,

39:11.998 --> 39:14.502
if you just stop pumping for a little while, obviously it will show you a

39:15.002 --> 39:18.338
static water level. And so you can measure that with a dip tape.

39:18.883 --> 39:22.494
And The Citizens for Safe

39:22.994 --> 39:27.003
Groundwater, Samantha was unable to attend, but as she

39:27.067 --> 39:31.143
documented, there's a whole group of residents there who have issues with their wells.

39:31.255 --> 39:34.529
I think the count is 18 now, residents with dried

39:35.029 --> 39:37.401
out wells. We heard from a few residents in the area this morning, the previous

39:37.497 --> 39:40.643
speakers who spoke before me, and there's

39:41.143 --> 39:44.333
many more, and they've had issues with wells drying out, sand in wells, and so

39:44.413 --> 39:48.329
on. And they all occurred, you know, like all within a close

39:48.829 --> 39:52.099
period of time. So that cannot be explained by a mechanical issue with the well.

39:53.302 --> 39:56.399
This is only explainable by a low water level issue.

39:56.928 --> 40:00.297
And the problem is there's not enough

40:00.797 --> 40:04.934
discussion on geologic uncertainty. I could go into it and,

40:05.496 --> 40:08.191
you know, teach a whole course on this, but there's not enough time.

40:08.785 --> 40:11.592
But when you look at, for example, the Burns site,

40:13.293 --> 40:16.745
cross sections in the Burnside Report, you see a cross section

40:17.245 --> 40:20.085
that shows as a simple layer cake. But in reality,

40:20.663 --> 40:24.453
geology in 3D in a morainic environment like the Waterloo

40:24.953 --> 40:28.916
Moraine has, you tend to get pinch-outs and windowing and aquitards

40:29.350 --> 40:33.268
and all sorts of interesting things where you can get places where

40:33.768 --> 40:37.025
you pump from one well in one aquifer like the AFB2,

40:37.057 --> 40:39.979
which is what we have in Mannheim, And everything seems fine, uh,

40:40.284 --> 40:43.750
according to the monitoring wells that you have around that, but you

40:44.250 --> 40:47.522
just get problems elsewhere, and the problems elsewhere can take time to appear.

40:48.115 --> 40:52.192
And we've heard from previous speakers this morning how problems can

40:52.224 --> 40:55.514
take time to occur, and then it turns out, oh, well, the region's

40:56.014 --> 41:00.088
been quietly doing experimental pumping since 2019 at an elevated

41:00.588 --> 41:04.630
rate, far above the 87 liters per second agreed from

41:04.662 --> 41:08.589
the Wellmont Centre wells in the 1980

41:09.089 --> 41:12.934
agreement. And it took a while for problems to appear, but now a whole slew

41:13.434 --> 41:16.477
of problems is occurring. And that's the nature of aquifer systems.

41:16.525 --> 41:19.250
They're complex, especially morainic, and they're in 3D.

41:19.955 --> 41:23.803
So in one cross-section you might get what looks like

41:23.979 --> 41:28.115
a layer cake, but in reality a layer cake cross-section is drawn

41:28.308 --> 41:32.283
by drawing lines between your boreholes, which is the only places

41:32.315 --> 41:35.297
where you actually know where the geology is. So you see sand, clay,

41:36.150 --> 41:39.063
sand, clay, and sand, clay, sand, clay. Well, how do you know those layers of

41:39.096 --> 41:42.462
clay and sand are all continuous between those boreholes, which,

41:42.721 --> 41:45.958
you know, are wells that could be, you know, a kilometre or half a kilometre,

41:45.975 --> 41:49.616
whatever, apart? So the 3D structure of the aquifer

41:50.116 --> 41:53.596
of the Waterloo Moraine, even though it's been extensively studied, starting in

41:53.742 --> 41:57.782
the 1980s by Paul Martin, who actually did his

41:57.814 --> 42:01.213
master's under my father, you know, I mean,

42:01.229 --> 42:03.561
there's been a lot of studying done since then, but there's still a lot that's

42:03.577 --> 42:06.821
unknown. And the more that we know about the Waterloo Moraine, the more we realize

42:06.853 --> 42:10.190
we don't know. So when you get a study done by a consulting

42:10.287 --> 42:13.479
firm like SLR, which did a fair bit of work for that study,

42:14.298 --> 42:19.271
what bothers me is what they left out. You can't cherry-pick and lemon-drop data.

42:19.688 --> 42:22.672
You have to do a study that shows, that explains the observations,

42:22.881 --> 42:25.833
all of them. You can't just say, well, we're gonna use the region's monitoring wells

42:26.587 --> 42:28.961
and leave out the wells that are the private wells.

42:30.742 --> 42:34.272
So those are the concerns that I have. And the concern I have for the

42:34.772 --> 42:38.280
region is getting closer to— to doing the same mistake that it did at

42:38.780 --> 42:40.396
Mannheim here out here in Wilmoth Center.

42:42.865 --> 42:46.600
So yeah, I'd like, I'd like those concerns addressed in

42:46.648 --> 42:49.806
some way. And I think basically, certainly we need more studying.

42:49.903 --> 42:53.574
And I think the only safe approach is to stick with the not exceeding

42:53.606 --> 42:56.283
the 1987 level that, uh,

42:56.956 --> 43:00.066
um, the 87 liters per second that was

43:00.098 --> 43:03.692
mentioned in 19— the 1980 agreement. That agreement should be

43:04.192 --> 43:07.819
used as a cap for sure. And also, the other thing is that bothers

43:07.884 --> 43:11.497
me with the SLR report is that they, they try to key their arguments

43:11.997 --> 43:16.026
to the average of precipitation and the average rainfall,

43:16.074 --> 43:19.687
and it really should be keyed to the minimum years, the drought years, because climate

43:20.187 --> 43:22.883
change is occurring and that's going to make things more extreme in the future.

43:23.461 --> 43:26.849
Thank you. Thank you, Michael. And don't go away, we have questions.

43:26.913 --> 43:29.049
Councillor Downey, we have questions.

43:31.072 --> 43:33.977
Thanks, Chair. Thanks, Michael, for,

43:34.442 --> 43:38.054
for coming in virtually today. The region model suggests

43:38.554 --> 43:42.709
that these private wells and the Shingletown wetland are not connected or

43:43.209 --> 43:46.513
outside of the zone of influence. In your professional opinion, when there

43:46.721 --> 43:50.333
is a systemic well failure and the wetland dries

43:50.833 --> 43:54.602
up exactly when pumping increases, does that suggest the data

43:55.102 --> 43:57.411
is wrong or is the region using the wrong model entirely?

43:59.017 --> 44:01.939
Well, there's obviously seems to be some kind of a connection there. If you're doing

44:02.051 --> 44:05.536
pumping and there's—

44:05.825 --> 44:09.839
there's some other noise. Okay. All right. There was some interference

44:10.339 --> 44:14.238
from someone else. What you're getting there is— when you're getting it, there's obviously

44:14.270 --> 44:18.156
some kind of a connection there. When you're pumping from the region's wells and

44:18.188 --> 44:21.223
the residential wells are drying out, that does point out there's some kind of

44:21.239 --> 44:24.736
connection. So there could be like windowing in an aquitard.

44:24.784 --> 44:28.437
So, the modeling the region has

44:29.574 --> 44:32.955
is fundamentally, with any kind of groundwater modeling, there's geologic uncertainty.

44:33.051 --> 44:37.297
So, the modeling's hydrostratigraphy, that's the geologic

44:37.841 --> 44:40.901
layering, is only really understood at the places where you

44:41.401 --> 44:44.442
have boreholes. So, places where you've drilled, and the drilling process,

44:44.570 --> 44:48.736
the person at the drilling is supposed to observe, okay, I found sand down

44:49.236 --> 44:51.956
to 5 feet, and then I found 2 feet of clay, and then 20 feet

44:52.456 --> 44:55.434
of sand, and so on. You're supposed to log all that. So, you correlate that

44:55.466 --> 44:58.704
between the boreholes, but the problem is you don't really know what's between those boreholes.

44:58.753 --> 45:02.039
So, if you're finding observations in the field that,

45:02.664 --> 45:05.485
you know, you've got a well here and, you know, half a kilometer away,

45:05.565 --> 45:09.140
a region, a residential well's drying out after you've done extensive heavy-duty

45:09.172 --> 45:11.913
pumping, and the region's pumps, I've seen them, they're huge pumps,

45:12.939 --> 45:17.091
pumping from elsewhere, and the model says they're not connected, but observations show

45:17.591 --> 45:20.765
that there's something happening here at the same time. Then you need to

45:21.439 --> 45:24.876
do some more investigation because the modeling is fundamentally limited

45:25.376 --> 45:27.591
by what you know from the field, and you can't know everything from the field.

45:28.105 --> 45:31.541
So when you have observations that point to something the model structure

45:31.558 --> 45:35.316
doesn't, doesn't have already in it, then the model needs to be updated.

45:38.849 --> 45:43.041
Councillor Gowing. So what

45:43.541 --> 45:47.136
would you suggest in response to the way that

45:47.636 --> 45:51.087
the modeling is— is like, would you add to it, or what would you

45:51.587 --> 45:55.295
suggest that we would be looking at? Um,

45:56.612 --> 45:59.439
well, that, that's again what you need to do is do more field investigations.

45:59.487 --> 46:01.977
There's multiple ways of doing that. Um,

46:02.876 --> 46:06.249
one of them is to, uh, the geophysical methods is one

46:06.749 --> 46:09.991
example. Uh, that was done actually a number of years ago in Wilmot Line in

46:10.087 --> 46:13.894
Waterloo, where the Ontario Geological Survey was involved. You use what are called

46:14.394 --> 46:18.021
seismic methods, in that case vibroseis. Um, you basically vibrate the ground

46:18.599 --> 46:21.667
and you generate sound waves that bounce off the different layers. You have to ground

46:22.167 --> 46:25.361
truth that with actual boreholes and drilling, although, um, but they

46:25.861 --> 46:28.734
see different layers behave differently in terms of how they reflect sound waves in the

46:29.234 --> 46:32.411
ground up to, up to the surface, to geophones as they're called, microphones that measure

46:32.911 --> 46:36.073
ground vibration. And that can give you insight into,

46:36.426 --> 46:39.847
okay, you know, could there be windowing? So we thought that the

46:40.200 --> 46:43.749
residential wells are in this aquifer and the regional wells are

46:44.249 --> 46:48.004
deeper down here. We thought the two were disconnected, but oh, now we see

46:48.504 --> 46:51.119
that there's some windowing in the aquitards. There's some kind of connections in there,

46:51.151 --> 46:54.667
some pinch-outs in something. So you need to get more data,

46:55.213 --> 46:58.537
and that hasn't been done. I mean, geophysical fieldwork is expensive,

46:59.308 --> 47:02.086
and so it hasn't been done yet. So that needs to be done.

47:03.739 --> 47:05.312
Thanks, Michael. Councillor Schantz.

47:10.399 --> 47:13.930
Thank you. Thanks, Michael. I'm not

47:14.430 --> 47:18.407
sure if you know this answer or not, but I'm trying to get a

47:18.907 --> 47:22.724
visual 3D picture in my mind of what's happening.

47:22.772 --> 47:26.350
And so I heard the one resident talk

47:26.850 --> 47:30.138
about a 42-foot deep well that now he

47:30.638 --> 47:34.602
has to go down 140 feet. 42 feet, which is clearly

47:34.635 --> 47:38.121
in a different aquifer. I guess,

47:38.474 --> 47:42.154
Abe, can you speak to the difference between

47:42.587 --> 47:45.640
the depth that the region pulls from and

47:46.140 --> 47:49.865
the depth that residents pull from? And you just alluded to that

47:50.365 --> 47:51.825
a little bit in your answer to Joe.

47:53.271 --> 47:56.485
Can you speak to that a little more? Yeah, I have to look at the

47:56.985 --> 48:00.017
different depth. That was in the depth the regional wells is given there. They are

48:00.517 --> 48:03.917
deep, uh, and that the 3 K50, K51, and K52

48:03.933 --> 48:07.079
wells are— they're in the SLR report. I can pull up that number. They are

48:07.579 --> 48:10.770
deeper than the residential wells. I don't have all the numbers memorized. I can pull

48:11.270 --> 48:14.365
them up. Um, so the depths are different, but also the thing you got to

48:14.865 --> 48:18.602
remember with depth, um, you have to also consider the ground surface elevation.

48:19.292 --> 48:23.288
So, you know, like if you're measuring depth below ground surface or from the

48:23.788 --> 48:27.203
top of casing, often some it says TOC. You're measuring the depth that, you know,

48:27.364 --> 48:30.717
the well goes down to the well screen. And then,

48:30.733 --> 48:34.247
you know, the pump itself usually is elevated above the bottom of the well screen

48:34.343 --> 48:37.632
too. So there's a spot, you know, where the sediments can settle

48:38.132 --> 48:41.338
out and so on. So what happens is, you know,

48:41.483 --> 48:44.948
the regional wells are deep and they are deeper, but the issue isn't just the

48:45.448 --> 48:49.103
depth. The issue isn't even just the depth versus the ground surface elevation

48:49.603 --> 48:53.598
that you're measuring from. It's also the issue of hydraulic connectivity, how the

48:54.098 --> 48:57.418
two different— how the formations are connected. And the Waterloo Moraine is known

48:57.868 --> 49:01.271
to have different layers, but the different layers aren't completely separated.

49:01.319 --> 49:04.931
There are spots where, you know, an aquitard could pinch out,

49:04.947 --> 49:09.201
for example, and it turns out two layers that we thought were separate

49:09.731 --> 49:13.519
actually are connected, but in a roundabout way, out of the

49:14.019 --> 49:17.275
page of the cross-sections, like the cross-section you see in the Burnside Report.

49:17.836 --> 49:21.395
Um, so that's why, you know, the region's pumping can take

49:21.895 --> 49:25.195
a while. It can take, you know, a year or longer, several years to suddenly

49:25.227 --> 49:28.962
show up as a drying, as a water level drop in the residential

49:29.058 --> 49:32.329
wells. And then on top of that, you add the effects of like reduced recharge

49:32.938 --> 49:36.673
from drought periods, like it was mentioned, 2022. And then the other

49:37.173 --> 49:40.777
aspect is also to consider with climate change, we're having a situation now where we're

49:41.277 --> 49:45.110
getting more rainfall events even during wetter periods that come

49:45.610 --> 49:48.902
as an intense cloudburst, a really brutal cloudburst that is

49:48.951 --> 49:52.935
a lot of rain really fast. That tends to give a greater proportion

49:53.048 --> 49:55.651
of your incipient rainfall as runoff,

49:56.277 --> 49:59.989
as opposed to gentle drizzle that might be intermittent,

50:00.439 --> 50:03.556
that might give you those foggy rainy days that, you know, extend for a long

50:03.652 --> 50:06.866
period and lots of drizzle. But that kind of rainfall,

50:06.946 --> 50:10.256
like if you get, say, 30 centimeters of rain over, you know,

50:10.272 --> 50:13.613
2 weeks as a drizzle, that gives you much better recharge than, say,

50:13.629 --> 50:17.405
30 centimeters of rain all in 24 hours as intense cloudbursts.

50:18.031 --> 50:20.714
So that also needs to be considered. So there's so many factors to consider.

50:20.762 --> 50:23.091
You can't just say, well, this well is this deep and this well is that

50:23.591 --> 50:27.381
deep, and therefore they're, they're not connected. Well, yeah, they could

50:27.429 --> 50:31.268
well be connected, especially if that— those mega big pumps that the region has

50:31.766 --> 50:34.722
are all pumping full tilt at, you know, 120,

50:34.835 --> 50:38.428
130, 140, or even more that the SLR report

50:38.928 --> 50:42.354
mentioned, because that's the PTDW amount that the region has. If they max it out,

50:42.370 --> 50:45.783
sure, then you won't see something at the residential wells immediately,

50:46.360 --> 50:49.820
but you might see it, you know, 6 months later or 2 months later or

50:50.320 --> 50:52.705
whatever. And that's the kind of thing, you know, you get pumping tests done that

50:53.205 --> 50:55.428
are done for a month or 2 at a high rate, they might not show

50:55.928 --> 50:58.905
that, but you need that extra time to show that. It's like, oh my goodness,

50:58.954 --> 51:02.318
we got a big problem in the regional— in the residential wells. Now what do

51:02.818 --> 51:05.605
we do? Yeah, it's gonna take a long time for those to recover. Yeah,

51:05.766 --> 51:09.202
thank you. Councillor James. Thank you. Through you,

51:09.282 --> 51:13.232
Chair, uh, thank you, uh, Mr. Friend, for, for coming and delegating. I just

51:13.732 --> 51:17.584
have a question, um, regarding the 1980 policy.

51:17.632 --> 51:20.827
Have you reviewed the evidence used to develop the 1980

51:20.859 --> 51:24.408
policy, and, um, do you think it— there's been progress in

51:24.456 --> 51:27.924
science and technology or for hydrology in the

51:28.424 --> 51:28.855
past 50 years?

51:33.464 --> 51:38.187
There has been progress in terms of the information

51:38.687 --> 51:42.700
we've learned about it, the fundamentals of it. In terms of like field observations,

51:42.764 --> 51:46.426
we know more now. Seismic methods, for example, we can

51:46.635 --> 51:50.040
do more geophysics now. But the problem with fieldwork is it's

51:50.168 --> 51:53.204
more expensive to do more fieldwork.

51:54.103 --> 51:57.717
So the state of knowledge has grown. The big boost

51:58.217 --> 52:02.117
in knowledge of the Water Moraine has been in the 1980s, which was Paul

52:02.617 --> 52:06.484
Martin's master's thesis. You can download it freely. And that—

52:06.789 --> 52:10.563
what that was based on is doing— basically

52:10.643 --> 52:14.272
correlating all the existing water well datasets. So, as every time

52:14.772 --> 52:17.868
a well is drilled in Ontario, you gain more information to the geology

52:18.093 --> 52:21.240
at that point. So, the answer of, yeah,

52:21.545 --> 52:25.383
do we know— is the state of knowledge has increased? Yes, it has. The 1980

52:26.122 --> 52:29.832
agreement was based on the knowledge available at that time. So we know

52:30.332 --> 52:34.056
more now, but we're still not at the point of knowing everything. And we can—

52:34.329 --> 52:37.429
really, you can never be at the state of knowledge of knowing absolutely everything because

52:37.943 --> 52:41.075
you can't drill a borehole every 10 centimeters in a— or

52:41.575 --> 52:44.432
every meter in an aquifer. I mean, the region would be pocked with boreholes all

52:44.932 --> 52:47.821
over the place. It wouldn't be possible. So you do have to sort of balance

52:48.321 --> 52:51.579
the actual data you get from boreholes i.e., every time you drill a

52:52.079 --> 52:55.465
well, and that gets reported in the Ontario MOE WIS, Water Well Information System.

52:55.834 --> 52:59.286
That's publicly available. You can download the logs of every well. All that information's

52:59.786 --> 53:02.658
public. The only thing that's blocked out is the, you know, the personal name of

53:03.158 --> 53:06.319
the landowner. But that's all public, and every well driller by law has to

53:06.367 --> 53:09.482
fill that out and put that all in. And so you can see, oh,

53:09.498 --> 53:12.710
we found, you know, sand 5 feet, and— or it could be

53:12.790 --> 53:15.536
meters— and clay down to, you know, this deep, and so on, all the way

53:16.036 --> 53:18.925
down. and where the static water level was found when the drilling was done,

53:19.198 --> 53:22.042
and drillers should have that information, and that should all be in there.

53:22.106 --> 53:24.677
So, we do know more now. That's great.

53:26.187 --> 53:29.417
My concern is that we also know more things, you know, we can't

53:29.917 --> 53:32.710
just say, well, we know more now, therefore we can suddenly just pump more,

53:33.192 --> 53:36.711
because we also know more about the effects. We know things, climate change is another

53:37.211 --> 53:40.968
one, I touched on that earlier and explained the concerns with climate change that mean

53:40.984 --> 53:43.874
we have to be more careful of our water supplies. The other aspect that I

53:44.374 --> 53:47.449
didn't mention actually is the issue of drought periods,

53:47.801 --> 53:50.783
meaning a more greater demand for irrigation. And that means everything,

53:50.847 --> 53:54.710
like farmers might be needing more water for irrigating

53:55.210 --> 53:58.510
their farm fields versus before they didn't. They might need more water for their chickens.

53:58.606 --> 54:01.956
There's a trout farm there too. They might need more

54:01.972 --> 54:04.778
water for— and then even the city, the region itself,

54:05.259 --> 54:07.888
you know, you want to take the water for the integrated urban system. Well,

54:08.000 --> 54:11.050
there's going to be more people for example, watering gardens and lawns. So that also

54:11.550 --> 54:14.502
makes more demand on the— that's also a form of irrigation. So,

54:15.787 --> 54:18.758
you know, we can say, yeah, sure, we know more than we did back in

54:19.258 --> 54:22.869
the '70s, but we also know from the 1970s, it was a seminal moment that

54:23.013 --> 54:26.691
pumping at a high rate, the original PTTW amount, permit to take water from the

54:27.191 --> 54:29.324
MOE, that time the Ontario Water Resources Commission,

54:30.224 --> 54:32.938
um, back, you know, when the region started with its wells,

54:32.970 --> 54:37.996
1979-'72, pumping at full tilt, whatever it

54:38.496 --> 54:40.419
somewhere closer to 200 liters per second was just way too much. And that led

54:40.919 --> 54:44.768
to that decade of, you know, a lot of agony for the residents who

54:45.268 --> 54:48.909
at that time, sure, they— I'm sure they had shallower wells. And as we heard

54:48.925 --> 54:51.316
from previous speakers, they had to deepen their wells.

54:52.712 --> 54:55.681
And that is often a common solution. Okay. You know, really shallow wells, not good

54:56.181 --> 54:58.827
enough. We got to deepen it. Okay, that's fine. But you still got the issue

54:58.891 --> 55:02.197
where you're only getting so much recharge. Your recharge from a deeper well tends

55:02.261 --> 55:05.648
to come from a larger area, so that can help. And understanding

55:05.664 --> 55:09.384
the flow system is definitely valuable. But you also got to take

55:09.884 --> 55:13.441
into account all the information when you got residents today who now have largely

55:13.473 --> 55:16.838
deeper wells, and when they're reporting problems and the

55:17.338 --> 55:20.110
Region has been pumping at a higher rate since 2019 and reporting problems now,

55:20.561 --> 55:23.885
that is information that needs to be taken into account. So that needs to be

55:24.385 --> 55:27.898
sort of added in there. Councillor James. Okay,

55:27.964 --> 55:31.318
thank you. Just one follow-up. So, um, Are private

55:31.818 --> 55:35.635
wells regulated the same way that, say, a regional well would be? And I'm thinking

55:35.988 --> 55:40.049
there was comments earlier about hand wells.

55:41.830 --> 55:45.858
Hand dug? Hand dug wells, yes. Same regulations

55:45.922 --> 55:46.821
follow or different?

55:49.373 --> 55:52.502
Can you answer that, Michael? Can you just run

55:52.583 --> 55:55.744
that by me again? I just need to be sure I get everything. I'm just

55:55.873 --> 55:59.725
confirming the difference between between— so, you know, you've got your hand-dug wells and

55:59.741 --> 56:03.449
then you've got, say, the regional well. And in terms of the

56:03.577 --> 56:07.285
regulations or requirements and all of that, would it follow the same thing? Or if

56:07.911 --> 56:11.458
I'm somebody, I can just— on my property, I can hand-dig

56:11.958 --> 56:15.358
a well and I don't have to follow— is there regulations around that?

56:15.471 --> 56:19.371
That's what I'm asking. No, no, no. By law, any well in Ontario

56:19.387 --> 56:22.822
has to be dug by a well technician. We're not back in the

56:23.322 --> 56:26.674
1930s where you can just start digging a well at your itself. There are some

56:26.770 --> 56:28.760
cases where some historic hand-dug wells,

56:30.076 --> 56:33.030
which later failed. I get that.

56:33.286 --> 56:36.512
I mean, a well that's that shallow, you also get serious problems with water quality.

56:37.202 --> 56:40.589
You get serious issues with, you know, cow manure and all

56:41.089 --> 56:43.735
that kind of stuff. So, really, you know, drinking from a well that's just hand-dug

56:44.235 --> 56:47.330
at 10 feet is not really, not really a good idea. That's a bad idea.

56:47.394 --> 56:51.310
In any case, just for water quality, and those wells are very vulnerable to fluctuations

56:51.810 --> 56:55.509
and water levels and, you know, and rainfall and drought anyway. But the issue

56:56.009 --> 56:58.603
is when you, you know, the region, the residents now have much deeper wells,

56:59.340 --> 57:02.947
uh, by and large, and so they're having problems. That's, that's the issue

57:02.963 --> 57:06.762
there. Um, so, but yeah, you can't just start

57:06.794 --> 57:10.080
digging your own well. That's not that kosher. I mean, that's not allowed. That's,

57:10.320 --> 57:13.430
that's, you have to have a well technician, somebody certified, has to be done properly,

57:13.446 --> 57:17.197
has to be reported. There's a whole procedure behind that because groundwater is

57:17.229 --> 57:20.293
a shared public good. Aquifers have a

57:20.793 --> 57:24.580
shared public nature to them. So you can't just have people randomly

57:24.612 --> 57:27.695
doing, you know, whatever they want to. You know,

57:27.743 --> 57:30.955
that's not kosher. Thank you, Michael. Thank you for the questions, Chair.

57:31.179 --> 57:34.648
Thank you, Michael. I have no further questions. Thank you for delegating. The next

57:35.148 --> 57:38.999
delegate is joining us virtually, Larry Macio, Waterloo Region Home

57:39.063 --> 57:42.403
Builders Association. Welcome, Larry. You have

57:42.419 --> 57:44.009
the floor and you have 5 minutes.

57:50.849 --> 57:52.021
We're not hearing you yet.

57:56.966 --> 57:58.845
Can you hear me now? Yes, we can.

58:00.804 --> 58:05.830
Okay, great, because I'm getting a terrible feedback on my end, but I'll

58:05.942 --> 58:09.089
get going here. Good morning, Chair Redden and members of

58:09.589 --> 58:12.907
council. Thank you. I'm here this morning on behalf of

58:12.923 --> 58:16.563
Waterloo Region Homeowners Association in support of item

58:16.611 --> 58:20.332
6.3, being the deferred motion relating to

58:20.380 --> 58:23.812
the temporary suspension of the Region's 1980

58:23.844 --> 58:27.613
water take— well, not water take permit. While the motion

58:28.113 --> 58:31.526
speaks to a temporary suspension of the policy, I just want to know,

58:31.606 --> 58:35.263
we would also support a permanent suspension of that policy as well.

58:35.552 --> 58:38.954
Larry, your, your, your audio's coming in and out.

58:39.885 --> 58:43.448
What you're using as a microphone, maybe lean closer

58:43.833 --> 58:47.461
to the source. Yes, I'm having a

58:48.071 --> 58:51.634
heck of a time trying to— I've checked all my systems.

58:51.682 --> 58:55.421
There doesn't seem to be anything on my end that I can find. Maybe turn

58:55.438 --> 58:59.273
off your video. Chair Redmond, you're coming

58:59.370 --> 59:00.943
through that way as well.

59:04.318 --> 59:07.891
Sorry, I didn't hear that at all. I'm sorry, Larry, try turning off

59:08.391 --> 59:11.464
your video and just seeing if that— okay,

59:11.545 --> 59:13.312
I'll, I'll do that. Um,

59:14.947 --> 59:19.657
just so—

59:20.304 --> 59:23.652
can you hear me now? It's better.

59:24.022 --> 59:27.349
Okay, well, I'll carry on.

59:27.703 --> 59:31.471
Just stop me if you need to. Um, decisions on,

59:31.568 --> 59:35.287
on this matter must be grounded in independent

59:35.787 --> 59:39.119
analysis and verified data, particularly given both

59:39.280 --> 59:43.258
the documented water capacity constraints within the Mannheim service

59:43.758 --> 59:47.366
area and the growing economic impacts now being felt

59:47.866 --> 59:51.603
across the region as a result of the current

59:51.635 --> 59:55.308
development freeze, as clearly documented in

59:55.356 --> 59:58.572
Staff Report 26-2022. -003.

59:59.342 --> 01:00:02.984
Regional staff have undertaken a detailed hydrogeological

01:00:03.484 --> 01:00:07.348
review and system analysis, including a third-party

01:00:07.365 --> 01:00:11.039
review by SLR Consulting Canada Limited.

01:00:11.552 --> 01:00:15.018
This work confirms that sufficient groundwater capacity

01:00:15.518 --> 01:00:18.901
exists to support the temporary policy suspension

01:00:19.318 --> 01:00:23.153
contemplated by the motion. The conclusion is

01:00:23.249 --> 01:00:27.884
not theoretical, it's based on modeling current system monitoring,

01:00:28.317 --> 01:00:31.683
alignment with the province's permit to take water

01:00:32.183 --> 01:00:35.579
framework, and the region's own source water protection planning.

01:00:36.717 --> 01:00:39.827
At the same time, staff have acknowledged that

01:00:39.923 --> 01:00:43.723
the Mannheim service area is already operating under

01:00:43.787 --> 01:00:47.153
real and increasing water capacity constraints.

01:00:48.099 --> 01:00:52.284
Maintaining the 1980 policy in its current form despite

01:00:52.784 --> 01:00:56.202
confirmed availability capacity, does not

01:00:56.702 --> 01:01:00.649
preserve water resources. Instead, it artificially restricts

01:01:01.131 --> 01:01:04.695
system flexibility and limits the region's ability

01:01:05.032 --> 01:01:08.661
to responsibly and efficiently allocate existing supply

01:01:09.206 --> 01:01:12.000
where it is needed most. In addition,

01:01:12.337 --> 01:01:16.142
there is some question as to the legal validity of the

01:01:16.642 --> 01:01:20.108
1980 policy and the ability to restrict an

01:01:20.204 --> 01:01:23.239
entity from using groundwater for its own purposes,

01:01:24.074 --> 01:01:26.723
provided it conforms with all other applicable law.

01:01:28.313 --> 01:01:32.311
As noted in Staff Report 26-007,

01:01:33.130 --> 01:01:37.931
failure to suspend and ultimately review this decades-old policy

01:01:38.525 --> 01:01:41.720
will exacerbate servicing constraints within the

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:46.442
Mannheim servicing area. Prevent the use of demonstrated capacity,

01:01:47.599 --> 01:01:51.342
increase pressure on already constrained parts of the system,

01:01:52.097 --> 01:01:54.314
and complicate capital planning,

01:01:55.246 --> 01:01:57.125
delaying necessary solutions.

01:01:58.137 --> 01:02:02.025
Supporting this recommendation will not weaken environmental protection.

01:02:02.603 --> 01:02:06.041
Groundwater takings remain subject to provincial permitting,

01:02:06.459 --> 01:02:10.539
ongoing monitoring, wellhead re— and, and source water protection policies,

01:02:11.357 --> 01:02:14.901
and region-wide oversight. The recommendation before

01:02:15.401 --> 01:02:19.342
you will ensure that decisions reflect 2026

01:02:19.470 --> 01:02:22.805
conditions, not merely assumptions from 1980.

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:28.177
This is also a matter of good governance and economic responsibility.

01:02:29.010 --> 01:02:32.939
When professional staff and independent consultants both confirm

01:02:33.404 --> 01:02:36.835
that capacity exists and that continuing

01:02:36.867 --> 01:02:40.766
to rely on a legacy policy will worsen an already

01:02:40.798 --> 01:02:44.411
constrained system, Council should act on that evidence.

01:02:45.117 --> 01:02:48.955
Not doing so risks worsening and extending

01:02:49.067 --> 01:02:51.957
the economic impacts of the current crisis.

01:02:53.081 --> 01:02:57.288
And in that context, we also respectfully request

01:02:58.139 --> 01:03:02.314
the Council undertake a comprehensive economic assessment

01:03:02.635 --> 01:03:06.151
of the current development freeze including impacts

01:03:06.651 --> 01:03:10.388
on housing supply, employment, municipal revenues,

01:03:10.821 --> 01:03:14.914
and long-term affordability. Understanding these effects

01:03:14.994 --> 01:03:18.942
is essential to making balanced, informed decisions.

01:03:19.809 --> 01:03:23.837
So in closing, we urge council to support item 6.3

01:03:24.222 --> 01:03:26.999
in context of a full economic analysis.

01:03:27.737 --> 01:03:30.369
Thank you for your time and your consideration this morning.

01:03:32.390 --> 01:03:36.175
Larry, thank you very much. I have no questions, but thank you for— Thank

01:03:36.675 --> 01:03:39.382
you very much. Thank you.

01:03:43.135 --> 01:03:46.775
I was getting feedback, so I was waiting for a minute. The next delegation's in

01:03:46.919 --> 01:03:50.303
person, I believe. David Enakurin from

01:03:50.832 --> 01:03:54.521
Wilmot. You can correct my pronunciation when you get to the podium.

01:03:55.162 --> 01:03:56.557
Welcome, and you have 5 minutes.

01:03:56.942 --> 01:04:00.403
That was Dave Nkoren. Yeah,

01:04:00.611 --> 01:04:03.335
close. Hello,

01:04:03.351 --> 01:04:06.588
Council and Regional Chair. My name

01:04:06.636 --> 01:04:10.322
is David Nkoren and I live at 2428 Bleems Road in the

01:04:10.822 --> 01:04:13.623
township of Wilmot, west of K-50 and 51.

01:04:14.552 --> 01:04:17.789
My wife and I moved to this location in 1995.

01:04:18.430 --> 01:04:22.372
It was formerly owned by Mr. Clyde Gasmire, and it has been

01:04:22.484 --> 01:04:26.512
a focal point with the local residents because of its location to

01:04:27.012 --> 01:04:30.992
K-50, 51. Our property, which dates

01:04:31.008 --> 01:04:35.119
back to 1845 or older, has had

01:04:35.619 --> 01:04:39.181
a cider mill, had a wheat mill run by watermill powered by

01:04:39.681 --> 01:04:42.858
Huntsberger Creek. Residents remember coming to this property

01:04:44.110 --> 01:04:48.060
for boat rides and on the ponds and school trips

01:04:48.189 --> 01:04:52.020
to catch frogs. Kwoks, and I have a map of our

01:04:52.520 --> 01:04:53.959
property. I don't know if it was up or not,

01:04:56.603 --> 01:05:00.176
but the— yeah, that's it there. The black areas

01:05:00.353 --> 01:05:04.487
are— the biggest one is our pond, our area that

01:05:04.519 --> 01:05:08.557
used to be there. I'm here today to ask

01:05:08.605 --> 01:05:12.467
the Regional Waterloo to continue as stated in the May

01:05:12.611 --> 01:05:15.655
18th, 1972 water taking permit.

01:05:16.377 --> 01:05:19.841
And this is how it reads. In order to comply with the special

01:05:20.341 --> 01:05:23.962
conditions 3, it will be necessary for the Kitchener-Waterloo Commission

01:05:24.074 --> 01:05:28.083
to install, operate, maintain works whereby water

01:05:28.583 --> 01:05:32.012
can be directed from K-5051 into the upper pond of Mr.

01:05:32.092 --> 01:05:35.604
Gasmire's property and to direct a portion of

01:05:35.716 --> 01:05:39.180
the outflow by means of pipe to his lower pond.

01:05:39.790 --> 01:05:43.543
The outflow will then pass through Mr. Haymakers on

01:05:43.576 --> 01:05:47.220
stream pond via the existing stream channel before

01:05:47.268 --> 01:05:50.897
reaching the gauging site of H5, which I believe is still there,

01:05:51.668 --> 01:05:55.056
the point at which a minimum stream flow of 350

01:05:55.216 --> 01:05:58.957
imperial gallons, which is 1,591 liters,

01:05:59.471 --> 01:06:02.650
must be maintained. At present,

01:06:03.325 --> 01:06:07.226
the Region of Waterloo provides 6 liters per second

01:06:07.419 --> 01:06:11.607
into our pond. The region provided

01:06:12.107 --> 01:06:14.142
me with a letter on June 15,

01:06:14.382 --> 01:06:17.928
2009, stating that the region of Waterloo will— no,

01:06:18.153 --> 01:06:22.180
will continue the flow even though the MOE did not include

01:06:22.680 --> 01:06:26.191
the clause addressing the flow to the pond in the

01:06:26.691 --> 01:06:30.394
2006 permit, as it was stated in the 1972 permit.

01:06:31.245 --> 01:06:34.936
And I have attached pages of that for council.

01:06:35.884 --> 01:06:39.805
I'm asking the— let's see,

01:06:39.821 --> 01:06:43.532
I'm asking the region of council to ask the MOE

01:06:43.934 --> 01:06:47.260
to put back in the water taking permit the clause about the flow to

01:06:47.292 --> 01:06:51.116
our pond which feeds Hunsberger Creek. This would not

01:06:51.245 --> 01:06:54.844
make it a promise, but a policy. This would

01:06:54.924 --> 01:06:59.086
also provide written clarification for understanding

01:06:59.343 --> 01:07:02.245
should things change at the regional level in the future.

01:07:03.543 --> 01:07:07.020
Since purchasing this property, I've gained a lot of information

01:07:07.373 --> 01:07:10.802
and documentation, and this is just part of it. I've got

01:07:11.302 --> 01:07:15.065
a lot. People have given me information left, right, and center since

01:07:15.565 --> 01:07:18.013
I've lived there, from the last owner,

01:07:18.606 --> 01:07:22.004
Mr. Gasmire, Water Advisory Committee, the Township of

01:07:22.100 --> 01:07:26.395
Wilmot, Regional Waterloo, and the Wilmot Centre Monitoring Program

01:07:26.508 --> 01:07:30.584
Liaison Committee, in which in 1997

01:07:31.564 --> 01:07:34.918
I was on the Wilmot Centre Monitoring Program

01:07:35.271 --> 01:07:39.123
Liaison Committee for the Region of Waterloo until it was stopped

01:07:39.623 --> 01:07:43.601
in 2013. I learned a lot about aquifer groundwater

01:07:44.101 --> 01:07:47.838
levels, groundwater trigger levels, streambed water levels, stream flows,

01:07:47.935 --> 01:07:50.342
and brown trout levels in Huntsburg Creek.

01:07:51.963 --> 01:07:55.458
Should the region continue to increase pumping rates, I advise

01:07:55.554 --> 01:08:00.317
them to restart the Wilmot Centre Program Public

01:08:00.365 --> 01:08:03.395
Liaison Committee. This will get all hands on

01:08:03.427 --> 01:08:07.067
deck to make sure that there's no impact to the Wilmot

01:08:07.757 --> 01:08:11.252
Centre wellfield and Huntsburg Creek watershed.

01:08:11.846 --> 01:08:12.728
Thank you for your time.

01:08:15.373 --> 01:08:19.029
Thank you very much. Don't go away. We have questions. Councillor Hunnicutt.

01:08:19.301 --> 01:08:22.715
Thank you, Chair. Sorry, you said a lot of really good things really calmly

01:08:23.068 --> 01:08:26.659
to the point where I didn't quite catch your recommendation or

01:08:27.159 --> 01:08:30.602
your ask. Can you just repeat that one more time for everyone?

01:08:30.907 --> 01:08:34.770
Certainly. I have a letter from the region and

01:08:35.270 --> 01:08:39.099
what I'm looking for is it to be more put into the MOE

01:08:39.419 --> 01:08:42.786
water taking permit that this water that's fed to my

01:08:43.286 --> 01:08:46.924
pond, because the pond dried up back in '72

01:08:46.956 --> 01:08:49.892
when K50, 51 were on— was put online.

01:08:51.351 --> 01:08:54.720
The previous owner, Mr. Gasmire, his well dried up, and I

01:08:55.220 --> 01:08:59.100
have a letter from 1973 from the township of Wilmot indicating that

01:08:59.533 --> 01:09:02.276
his well dried up, and then he had to hook on to the regional water

01:09:02.517 --> 01:09:05.693
or City of Kitchener, who owned it at the time.

01:09:06.222 --> 01:09:09.736
So my ask is that the region put that

01:09:10.236 --> 01:09:13.889
clause back into the water taking permit as it goes to the MOE,

01:09:14.691 --> 01:09:18.187
and also to start the Water Advisory Committee again,

01:09:18.636 --> 01:09:22.741
because a lot of the data I have reports—

01:09:22.870 --> 01:09:26.222
there was a group on there that were fantastic. There's got to

01:09:26.238 --> 01:09:30.504
be about 15 members on there from the community to— there's

01:09:31.004 --> 01:09:35.379
a whole group that was on there, and it was, it was closed in 2013

01:09:35.539 --> 01:09:39.166
because they said that they had enough data that

01:09:39.666 --> 01:09:42.376
they didn't need to have us running it anymore or going to it anymore.

01:09:42.922 --> 01:09:46.244
But now that time has changed, and I think it needs to be,

01:09:46.950 --> 01:09:50.401
you know, brought back online. So, Chair Rubin, just a follow-up

01:09:50.901 --> 01:09:54.317
by me, uh, do regional staff have a

01:09:54.413 --> 01:09:57.928
copy of the letters that you have? And, um, I did send

01:09:58.428 --> 01:10:02.165
it in, um, to the— so it's part of your package?

01:10:02.422 --> 01:10:05.828
Yes, it was. Okay. But if there's any other information that

01:10:05.876 --> 01:10:08.591
you require, by all means, I've got lots.

01:10:09.250 --> 01:10:13.010
I'm not sure if staff are receptive to that, but if we can

01:10:13.428 --> 01:10:16.657
talk about that during the presentation, that would be super helpful.

01:10:18.329 --> 01:10:21.574
Thank you very much. Councillor Salonen. Thank you. Through you, Chair,

01:10:22.121 --> 01:10:25.222
thank you very much for coming today, David, and sharing,

01:10:25.559 --> 01:10:29.078
um, your experience as well as your ask to have

01:10:29.110 --> 01:10:32.925
that put back into the water take permit. I can see that being

01:10:33.021 --> 01:10:37.107
something of a priority. I am curious though that

01:10:37.607 --> 01:10:40.905
you noted you were on the Wilmot Centre Public Liaison Committee and you also said

01:10:40.953 --> 01:10:44.334
that it wrapped up in 2013 due to

01:10:44.834 --> 01:10:48.341
enough data being collected. But the report that council got for

01:10:48.405 --> 01:10:53.148
this committee today on page 47 said that the

01:10:53.648 --> 01:10:57.238
region actually communicated with the Wilmot Centre

01:10:57.738 --> 01:11:01.140
Public Liaison community in a presentation in 2017.

01:11:01.878 --> 01:11:05.170
To your knowledge, were you or any of your neighbors or any of the folks

01:11:05.670 --> 01:11:10.228
who were a part of the committee involved in that said

01:11:10.728 --> 01:11:13.343
presentation in 2017? Not that I can recall,

01:11:13.696 --> 01:11:17.164
unless as a member, I didn't get any

01:11:17.293 --> 01:11:20.151
kind of notice that that meeting was there.

01:11:21.178 --> 01:11:24.903
I most likely would have not attended. Okay. All I have is

01:11:24.935 --> 01:11:27.376
a letter from the region stating that the,

01:11:28.468 --> 01:11:31.888
uh, that it was no longer viable to have that committee to

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:35.758
be run in 2013. Okay, thank you

01:11:36.258 --> 01:11:38.905
very much. You're quite welcome. Thank you very much, David. Thank you.

01:11:39.948 --> 01:11:45.151
The next delegation, I believe, is virtual, and it's Adiat Linde-Lapel

01:11:45.167 --> 01:11:46.082
from Wilmot.

01:11:54.879 --> 01:11:56.950
Welcome. You have the floor and you have 5 minutes.

01:12:02.183 --> 01:12:05.843
Thank you for— yeah, thank you for letting

01:12:05.859 --> 01:12:09.936
me share my thoughts this morning with you as we both face similar challenges,

01:12:10.916 --> 01:12:13.789
providing humans with their most important needs.

01:12:14.768 --> 01:12:18.764
You are seeking a solution to provide enough water to a growing population,

01:12:19.037 --> 01:12:22.456
and I, or we farmers, are challenged to

01:12:22.488 --> 01:12:25.297
feed them all with less and less land available.

01:12:26.083 --> 01:12:26.436
Next.

01:12:29.453 --> 01:12:32.968
The province appears to have overlooked errors in issued

01:12:33.065 --> 01:12:36.981
permits. Two expired permits for K50 and 51

01:12:37.334 --> 01:12:40.094
list the wrong address, Lot 3,

01:12:40.303 --> 01:12:43.962
north of Bleems Road, which is near Mannheim. Rather than the

01:12:43.978 --> 01:12:46.499
actual location lot 11.

01:12:47.783 --> 01:12:50.994
Secondly, the amount permitted to be taken from each well,

01:12:52.005 --> 01:12:54.767
9,500 liters per minute,

01:12:54.895 --> 01:12:58.170
exceeds by far the recommended pump rate for each well

01:12:58.828 --> 01:13:01.573
of 3,900 liters per minute.

01:13:02.617 --> 01:13:07.256
To visualize the amount taken daily from K-50 wells at,

01:13:07.738 --> 01:13:11.182
let's say, 120 liters a second, One would need to line

01:13:11.311 --> 01:13:15.119
up 518 milk tanker trucks along Bleems

01:13:15.248 --> 01:13:17.810
Road carrying 20 cubic each.

01:13:18.405 --> 01:13:22.232
Next. The presented studies

01:13:23.469 --> 01:13:27.645
state a sustainable flow rate of 158

01:13:28.145 --> 01:13:31.283
liters for the K-50 well field, a number picked

01:13:31.783 --> 01:13:35.676
from the April 1970 well drilling and testing report

01:13:36.176 --> 01:13:39.606
of K-50. Done before the well got even registered

01:13:40.106 --> 01:13:43.948
in June 1970. This wasn't an operational test,

01:13:44.092 --> 01:13:46.533
but just a 100-hour well-driller's test,

01:13:47.180 --> 01:13:50.839
and even then water levels dropped in observation

01:13:50.888 --> 01:13:56.910
wells 1 kilometer around, notably 1067.

01:13:57.768 --> 01:14:01.474
Next. Observation or monitoring

01:14:01.587 --> 01:14:05.268
wells first drilled in 1967 at strategic

01:14:05.768 --> 01:14:09.870
locations, but to save money, additional monitoring wells were mainly located

01:14:09.983 --> 01:14:11.891
along roads and farmers' lanes.

01:14:14.088 --> 01:14:17.311
Observation Well 1067, located on

01:14:17.359 --> 01:14:20.726
the northwest corner of what is today the Hallman Pit,

01:14:21.063 --> 01:14:24.415
was part of the water level and pump test program since the

01:14:24.915 --> 01:14:28.568
beginning, and including the 3-month 2018

01:14:28.680 --> 01:14:31.741
pump test, but decommissioned right after.

01:14:33.167 --> 01:14:36.740
Next. Lack of easy access appears to be the only

01:14:36.772 --> 01:14:40.473
reason why the Shingletown wetlands have never been monitored.

01:14:41.114 --> 01:14:44.511
Thanks to hydrological studies done for the rezoning of

01:14:44.607 --> 01:14:47.924
Lot 10 south of Bleem's, data from this

01:14:48.052 --> 01:14:49.927
area become available. Next.

01:14:52.539 --> 01:14:57.009
The studies included detailed monitoring for multiple

01:14:57.362 --> 01:15:01.582
test wells around the perimeter of Lot 10, including the wetlands.

01:15:02.320 --> 01:15:06.156
This needed to be done to determine the future gravel pit floor level,

01:15:06.653 --> 01:15:10.906
as the operator has to stay 1.5 meters above the water table.

01:15:11.628 --> 01:15:15.897
Even water table trigger levels in the wetlands were established

01:15:16.397 --> 01:15:20.358
back then. Next. The hydrographs

01:15:20.471 --> 01:15:24.049
around and in the Lauenheisen gravel pit clearly

01:15:24.082 --> 01:15:27.259
responded to the pump test in 2018.

01:15:28.324 --> 01:15:29.902
Seen between the black lines,

01:15:31.286 --> 01:15:35.164
and increased pumping at various levels between 2020

01:15:35.664 --> 01:15:38.756
and 2023, and again in 2025.

01:15:39.743 --> 01:15:43.802
This 2018 pump test concluded 120

01:15:44.302 --> 01:15:48.128
liters a sec results— results in a measurable

01:15:48.161 --> 01:15:51.887
water table decline up to 2 kilometers off the K-50

01:15:51.954 --> 01:15:56.077
wells. Next. It puzzles

01:15:56.126 --> 01:16:00.249
me how a 2025 reliable operating flow

01:16:00.425 --> 01:16:03.891
of 158 liters could have been established,

01:16:04.372 --> 01:16:07.389
a number dating back to the 1970 drilling.

01:16:10.132 --> 01:16:13.822
Next. For 22 years shown

01:16:13.903 --> 01:16:17.529
here up to 2020, 110 liters a second or

01:16:18.029 --> 01:16:21.347
less have been pumped while the water table responded to seasonal rain

01:16:21.379 --> 01:16:24.188
and snowfall and always recovered,

01:16:24.349 --> 01:16:26.918
but no longer due to higher pump rates.

01:16:28.347 --> 01:16:31.735
Please respect the 1980 agreement limiting the flow

01:16:31.799 --> 01:16:35.556
to Kitchener. When adding current New Hamburg invading needs

01:16:35.669 --> 01:16:39.506
to the 1980 agreed flow to Kitchener, we will stay

01:16:39.522 --> 01:16:42.814
in a healthy pump bridge scene and hopefully stop

01:16:43.314 --> 01:16:46.186
the continued decline of the water table in our area.

01:16:46.828 --> 01:16:50.582
Next. Staff reports show as

01:16:50.775 --> 01:16:54.976
wells in Mannheim started to fail, Shingletown wells were simply

01:16:55.089 --> 01:16:57.574
ramped up. Next,

01:16:59.194 --> 01:17:02.594
to combine these well fields and claim there is unused

01:17:02.626 --> 01:17:05.802
flow in Wilmot is a paper exercise

01:17:06.058 --> 01:17:09.891
that doesn't hold water. Please include

01:17:09.987 --> 01:17:13.419
the Shingletown wetlands in future hydrological studies

01:17:13.853 --> 01:17:17.932
and bring it into the source water protection area as it

01:17:17.949 --> 01:17:21.900
is a kettle lake and represents the exposed

01:17:22.028 --> 01:17:25.514
aquifer. We all need to have

01:17:26.014 --> 01:17:29.240
it restored and protected to survive. Thank you.

01:17:29.979 --> 01:17:33.609
Thank you very much. I see no questions, so thank you very much for

01:17:34.109 --> 01:17:37.431
delegating. The next delegation is Megan McDermott,

01:17:37.768 --> 01:17:41.381
lawyer, Davies Howe LLP. P, speaking on behalf of Kitchener

01:17:41.638 --> 01:17:45.570
Trustler Holdings Inc. Um, Megan, I think you're joining us

01:17:46.070 --> 01:17:49.951
virtually. You have 5 minutes and the podium is yours virtually.

01:17:51.733 --> 01:17:55.360
Thank you. Good morning, uh, Madam Chair, councillors. My name is Megan

01:17:55.860 --> 01:17:59.292
McDermott. I'm a lawyer with Davies Howe LLP, and I'm counsel

01:17:59.792 --> 01:18:03.400
to Kitchener Trustler Holdings Inc. and Kitchener Trustler Holdings 3 Inc.

01:18:04.010 --> 01:18:07.461
And some of you may remember me from when I spoke at

01:18:07.961 --> 01:18:11.617
the Sustainability, Infrastructure and Development Committee on February 10th on

01:18:11.649 --> 01:18:15.116
this issue. I'm appearing today to

01:18:15.164 --> 01:18:19.417
provide input that we would like to see incorporated into the forthcoming interim

01:18:19.433 --> 01:18:23.205
risk management framework and water allocation framework,

01:18:23.606 --> 01:18:27.377
as well as express support for the proposal to rescind the Wilmot

01:18:27.877 --> 01:18:32.096
Water Taking Policy. My client's site is located at the northeast

01:18:32.596 --> 01:18:35.257
corner of Ottawa Street and Tressler Road in Kitchener,

01:18:35.723 --> 01:18:39.017
right at the boundary of Wilmot Township and also adjacent to the

01:18:39.517 --> 01:18:42.713
Mannheim Water Treatment Plant. The lands received

01:18:43.213 --> 01:18:46.955
draft approval in June 2024, and since then my client

01:18:47.455 --> 01:18:51.069
has been actively working to clear draft plan conditions and prepare for development.

01:18:52.515 --> 01:18:56.098
This includes advancing $1.2 million of funding

01:18:56.195 --> 01:19:00.694
for the installation of new water mains and sanitary sewers within regional

01:19:01.194 --> 01:19:04.948
rights of way. This work is being completed by the region with the

01:19:05.029 --> 01:19:08.255
water mains installed late last summer and the

01:19:08.755 --> 01:19:11.209
sanitary sewer work scheduled for this spring summer.

01:19:12.252 --> 01:19:15.993
My client has also commenced pre-grading work and completed

01:19:16.041 --> 01:19:20.616
tree removals on the site. The work also includes the negotiation

01:19:21.116 --> 01:19:25.127
of development and servicing agreements with regional staff. Requests for

01:19:25.627 --> 01:19:29.412
these agreements were first made in March 2025. We received the

01:19:29.444 --> 01:19:32.970
drafts in June 2025, and we were negotiating

01:19:33.051 --> 01:19:36.545
the agreements over the course of the early fall prior to the discovery of

01:19:36.593 --> 01:19:40.168
the capacity issue when progress stopped. Had my

01:19:40.668 --> 01:19:43.727
client been able to finalize and sign the servicing agreement over the summer,

01:19:44.272 --> 01:19:48.103
they would be one of the developments that could proceed to registration unaffected

01:19:48.603 --> 01:19:52.351
by the capacity issue. The frameworks that are being prepared

01:19:52.851 --> 01:19:56.929
now, in particular the allocation framework, need to recognize and prioritize

01:19:57.041 --> 01:20:00.946
projects like my client's where legitimate expectations

01:20:00.978 --> 01:20:04.224
of capacity were already in place before the constraints were

01:20:04.256 --> 01:20:08.025
announced. It is also critical that the frameworks properly

01:20:08.121 --> 01:20:11.423
recognize and support development timeframes and

01:20:11.923 --> 01:20:15.280
the time lag between when capacity commitments are required

01:20:15.782 --> 01:20:18.127
versus when the physical capacity is required.

01:20:19.113 --> 01:20:22.622
And to help illustrate this time lag, I'd like to give you an overview of

01:20:22.720 --> 01:20:24.848
my client's anticipated development timeline.

01:20:25.859 --> 01:20:29.713
As I've already noted, tree removal is complete. External sewer

01:20:30.213 --> 01:20:34.128
construction on Trussler Road will be commencing this spring/summer.

01:20:35.075 --> 01:20:39.731
In Q3 of this year, site grading would start. In Q2

01:20:39.747 --> 01:20:43.279
of next year would be site servicing, and it's at this point

01:20:43.344 --> 01:20:46.635
that they will need the Form 1 sign-off to proceed with water

01:20:47.135 --> 01:20:50.520
main construction. In Q4 of 2027,

01:20:50.585 --> 01:20:53.989
building permits would be applied for. At this time, water will

01:20:54.037 --> 01:20:57.617
be needed for firefighting purposes only. And finally,

01:20:57.794 --> 01:21:01.230
occupancy would commence in Q2 of 2028.

01:21:01.969 --> 01:21:05.405
It would not be until that point that domestic water supply would be required.

01:21:06.545 --> 01:21:10.093
The development timeline needs to be carefully orchestrated to

01:21:10.593 --> 01:21:14.557
ensure that all of the puzzle pieces come together when they need to, from financing

01:21:14.669 --> 01:21:17.945
to approvals and permitting to entering into construction contracts.

01:21:18.666 --> 01:21:22.401
Without the region's commitment to future capacity now, this entire

01:21:22.465 --> 01:21:26.103
timeline is jeopardized. In our view, the solution

01:21:26.199 --> 01:21:29.260
here is to enter into forward-looking servicing agreements.

01:21:29.821 --> 01:21:33.635
This means that the agreements would be executed now, committing the capacity

01:21:33.667 --> 01:21:37.962
and allowing a project to proceed through the initial steps, but they would contain

01:21:38.058 --> 01:21:41.359
a time in the future when that capacity will be available.

01:21:42.081 --> 01:21:45.895
This approach allows the developer to work backwards from that timeframe

01:21:46.007 --> 01:21:49.757
to plan out and align its own development timeline. And it keeps

01:21:50.257 --> 01:21:54.789
things moving while the Region works towards restoring water capacity without jeopardizing

01:21:54.821 --> 01:21:58.955
the supply in the interim. In my client's case, the anticipated

01:21:58.987 --> 01:22:02.721
2028 timing for requiring supply coincides

01:22:02.817 --> 01:22:05.348
well with the forecasted timing for restoring it.

01:22:06.662 --> 01:22:10.284
And we have already provided staff with proposed wording to be included in my

01:22:10.784 --> 01:22:14.306
client's servicing agreement to reflect this forward-looking reservation

01:22:14.806 --> 01:22:18.857
of future capacity. My client also supports the proposal

01:22:19.357 --> 01:22:23.007
to transfer excess water capacity from the Wilmot Centre wells to help address

01:22:23.507 --> 01:22:26.885
the Mannheim capacity issues. I know you're all very well

01:22:26.933 --> 01:22:30.490
aware of the significant impacts that the capacity issue

01:22:30.555 --> 01:22:34.128
has and will continue to cause until solutions

01:22:34.160 --> 01:22:37.910
are found and implemented. In our view, the Region needs to use

01:22:37.926 --> 01:22:41.820
every available option and particularly the available short-term options

01:22:42.012 --> 01:22:45.385
to help alleviate those impacts. My client

01:22:45.885 --> 01:22:50.108
encourages Regional Council to follow the technical peer-reviewed data and support

01:22:50.608 --> 01:22:53.658
this necessary solution. Thank you very much for your time today.

01:22:54.445 --> 01:22:56.806
Thank you, Megan. We have questions. Councillor Salonen.

01:22:58.059 --> 01:23:00.998
Thank you. Through you, Chair, uh, thank you very much, Megan. Um,

01:23:01.930 --> 01:23:05.544
I'm curious on two things. One, is your client

01:23:06.044 --> 01:23:10.249
supportive of the risk assessment that the region is currently undertaking

01:23:10.362 --> 01:23:14.424
in terms of how to proceed with development

01:23:14.761 --> 01:23:17.041
as we find new solutions and water capacity?

01:23:19.514 --> 01:23:22.019
Thank you. Through the chair, yes, Councillor,

01:23:22.404 --> 01:23:25.744
my, my client supports the, the work that's underway.

01:23:27.205 --> 01:23:30.898
We do, as I mentioned, we do want to ensure that the

01:23:31.398 --> 01:23:34.829
frameworks that come out of that work Firstly, that the

01:23:34.845 --> 01:23:38.356
work is undertaken quickly, which I know that staff are really working hard

01:23:38.856 --> 01:23:43.341
to do, and to ensure that projects

01:23:43.841 --> 01:23:47.622
like my client's are basically put at the front

01:23:47.750 --> 01:23:50.443
in terms of getting that capacity.

01:23:51.982 --> 01:23:55.733
As I mentioned, had we signed those— had been able to sign those agreements

01:23:55.765 --> 01:23:58.779
last summer, I wouldn't even be here today. So,

01:23:59.377 --> 01:24:03.339
Uh, we're certainly supportive of making sure that everything's

01:24:03.839 --> 01:24:07.236
done correctly, but also that it's done quickly and that

01:24:07.365 --> 01:24:09.898
the projects that are deserving get to go ahead.

01:24:10.915 --> 01:24:13.176
Thank you, Councillor Schantz.

01:24:14.856 --> 01:24:18.079
Thank you. Thank you, Megan. Um,

01:24:18.176 --> 01:24:21.909
we also have some developers that are in similar situations.

01:24:21.973 --> 01:24:23.686
I know that's region-wide.

01:24:25.705 --> 01:24:29.156
My question is, as a— I guess as a

01:24:29.656 --> 01:24:33.730
lawyer from a legal perspective, is there a way,

01:24:34.051 --> 01:24:37.469
a willingness, or possibility of

01:24:38.304 --> 01:24:41.530
if we were to grant water

01:24:42.076 --> 01:24:45.302
today for a year from now,

01:24:46.169 --> 01:24:49.700
that if that water is not available in a year from

01:24:50.200 --> 01:24:54.242
now, would your client be willing to accept that liability

01:24:54.354 --> 01:24:57.484
and that risk? That, um, okay,

01:24:57.885 --> 01:25:00.902
we say you can go ahead, carry on,

01:25:01.175 --> 01:25:04.658
we, you know, we like your development, we want you to go ahead, but if

01:25:04.690 --> 01:25:08.751
something happens in this process— and we hope it doesn't—

01:25:09.104 --> 01:25:12.458
but if it does, that the water is not available a year from

01:25:12.507 --> 01:25:16.557
now when you need it, uh, that you would accept

01:25:16.606 --> 01:25:20.091
that risk? I mean, certainly that's

01:25:20.591 --> 01:25:24.851
not the ideal situation. My client will be putting significant—

01:25:25.351 --> 01:25:29.192
making financial investments, putting resources into their

01:25:29.692 --> 01:25:33.261
projects in order to proceed. Based on the

01:25:33.487 --> 01:25:36.784
forecasting work that's being done thus far, it does

01:25:37.284 --> 01:25:40.679
appear that the water will be available in the year

01:25:40.728 --> 01:25:44.210
and a half when it's needed. And I think,

01:25:44.370 --> 01:25:49.602
you know, you would need to account for the eventualities

01:25:50.102 --> 01:25:53.823
in the agreements, and you would also need to make sure that there is

01:25:53.855 --> 01:25:57.996
very consistent updating. So if there are things

01:25:58.012 --> 01:26:03.228
that happen that is going to delay the timeline, those need to be communicated immediately

01:26:03.324 --> 01:26:06.550
so that developers can make decisions about what

01:26:06.598 --> 01:26:09.231
they need to do to respond to those those things.

01:26:10.854 --> 01:26:14.132
But, you know, that's certainly not the ideal situation, and it would cause

01:26:14.164 --> 01:26:18.020
a lot of problems. Yeah, of course it's not ideal,

01:26:18.165 --> 01:26:21.363
and, you know, we hope we can come to a better resolution,

01:26:21.475 --> 01:26:24.641
but I'm just curious if that would be something that

01:26:24.753 --> 01:26:28.658
would, you know, allow the area municipalities to say,

01:26:29.574 --> 01:26:33.655
uh, we like this project, we want it to go ahead,

01:26:33.848 --> 01:26:37.074
but you know, not, I guess,

01:26:37.154 --> 01:26:39.705
in the end get sued because we can't provide water.

01:26:41.068 --> 01:26:44.838
And certainly with development, there's always an aspect of managing risks.

01:26:44.918 --> 01:26:48.817
There's always risks part of development,

01:26:48.913 --> 01:26:52.586
and so I think through the drafting of

01:26:52.619 --> 01:26:56.549
the agreements, there would need to be the

01:26:56.613 --> 01:26:59.789
provisions that are put into how those risks get managed,

01:26:59.998 --> 01:27:04.007
and then it will be up to the individual developers as to what, what amount

01:27:04.507 --> 01:27:06.974
of risk they're prepared to take on. Thank you,

01:27:08.562 --> 01:27:09.364
Councillor Vovanovic.

01:27:14.030 --> 01:27:17.927
Thank you very much, Chair Redmond, and thank you, Miss McDermott, for, for coming

01:27:17.959 --> 01:27:21.183
