for years and we've seen how this wetland works. It isn't

01:38:02.271 --> 01:38:05.832
just a swamp or a pretty view. It's a vital part of our

01:38:05.865 --> 01:38:09.415
region's water supply. It acts as a natural filter,

01:38:10.150 --> 01:38:14.128
cleaning nitrates out of the groundwater and keeping our environment healthy.

01:38:15.014 --> 01:38:18.106
But lately, the water levels are dropping in ways that

01:38:18.235 --> 01:38:21.895
we haven't seen in decades. And it's happening right alongside

01:38:21.944 --> 01:38:24.884
the region's experimental pumping hikes.

01:38:26.012 --> 01:38:29.860
We've seen the Region's slides claiming that the wetland isn't connected

01:38:30.360 --> 01:38:34.060
to their pumping. They are basing that on sensors located at

01:38:34.560 --> 01:38:38.216
a nearby gravel pit, but we see a massive flaw in that logic.

01:38:39.020 --> 01:38:42.448
Those sensors were never meant to track the Region's pumps.

01:38:43.159 --> 01:38:46.054
They are located on the opposite side of the wetland,

01:38:46.814 --> 01:38:51.636
designed specifically to monitor the gravel pit's

01:38:52.136 --> 01:38:54.190
impact, not the vacuum created by the K-50 and 51 wells.

01:38:55.425 --> 01:38:59.001
We aren't just here to complain. We are here to offer a solution.

01:38:59.835 --> 01:39:03.651
We recently brought in Philip Horst of Ducks Unlimited,

01:39:04.148 --> 01:39:08.462
a well-known wetland restoration expert,

01:39:09.136 --> 01:39:13.353
to walk on our land, and he designed the rehabilitation

01:39:13.770 --> 01:39:18.167
of the Mike Schout Wetland in New Hamburg. After inspecting

01:39:18.199 --> 01:39:21.539
the site, Philip is confident that the Shingletown

01:39:21.635 --> 01:39:24.991
Wetland can be rehabilitated. He sees

01:39:25.023 --> 01:39:28.379
the potential to bring this system back to health, but he can't

01:39:28.475 --> 01:39:31.992
do it alone while the Region is pulling the rug out from under

01:39:32.492 --> 01:39:36.327
the water table. We are calling on the Region today to stop

01:39:36.827 --> 01:39:40.197
the experimental pumping and collaborate with us and

01:39:40.261 --> 01:39:43.713
Philip Holst. Let's use

01:39:44.213 --> 01:39:48.803
his expertise to preserve this vital part of our groundwater infrastructure.

01:39:49.493 --> 01:39:53.267
This wetland does the hard work of cleaning our water for

01:39:53.283 --> 01:39:57.265
free. If we let it die, the taxpayers will eventually

01:39:57.281 --> 01:40:00.717
have to pay for expensive mechanical filters to do

01:40:00.749 --> 01:40:04.570
the same job. Please don't ignore the reality on

01:40:04.603 --> 01:40:07.990
the ground for our sensor at a gravel pit. Work with us

01:40:08.230 --> 01:40:11.600
to save the Shingletown Wetland. Thank you.

01:40:13.413 --> 01:40:17.648
As a side note, I'd like to mention that the 17 families that

01:40:17.680 --> 01:40:21.290
we know of having well issues due to shrinking water

01:40:21.790 --> 01:40:24.868
tables get the support they deserve from the region.

01:40:26.103 --> 01:40:29.280
Again, thank you. Thank you very much,

01:40:29.392 --> 01:40:32.008
David. Councillor Salonen, you have a question?

01:40:33.066 --> 01:40:36.175
Um, thank you, David. And out of curiosity,

01:40:36.592 --> 01:40:40.518
your neighbors that you noted, the 17, and I'm aware of

01:40:40.998 --> 01:40:44.107
18, so I'm not certain if that's not including yourselves, um,

01:40:44.444 --> 01:40:48.017
has that information since been reported to the Region

01:40:48.450 --> 01:40:51.559
since February that you're aware of, of the well interferences?

01:40:53.594 --> 01:40:57.264
I'm sorry, can you say that again? Yeah, um, so the well interferences that you

01:40:57.764 --> 01:41:00.296
just noted at the very end of the 17 neighbors.

01:41:01.035 --> 01:41:04.567
Um, do you know if any of them have reported to the

01:41:04.583 --> 01:41:08.292
Region their well interference issues? I don't know

01:41:08.516 --> 01:41:12.097
which ones, but I do believe there has been some, and I'm— hopefully

01:41:12.354 --> 01:41:15.324
all of them have, but I don't know that answer.

01:41:15.886 --> 01:41:17.186
Okay, thank you very much.

01:41:19.354 --> 01:41:22.484
Thank you, David and Sherry. Thank you for, uh, coming to support.

01:41:23.351 --> 01:41:27.028
Um, Martha Bricker from Wilmot. Martha, I believe you're in person

01:41:27.124 --> 01:41:30.523
and you have 3 minutes as you registered after the deadline.

01:41:30.587 --> 01:41:33.681
So welcome to the podium, the floor is yours.

01:41:34.515 --> 01:41:37.881
Thank you. Good morning, Chair and members of council. My name

01:41:38.381 --> 01:41:42.306
is Martha Bricker. I delegated on February the 25th regarding our well

01:41:42.514 --> 01:41:46.185
failure. Mayor Natasha Salonen said I need to report this

01:41:46.217 --> 01:41:49.503
to the Region. Natasha had Amy Shaw email me

01:41:50.003 --> 01:41:53.751
the number at the Region's service first call center. I called the Region's call center

01:41:54.251 --> 01:41:57.350
and spoke to Supriya Singh and reported our well failure.

01:41:57.985 --> 01:42:00.264
Our reference claim number is L02-40/Bricker.

01:42:03.035 --> 01:42:06.208
A licensed technician, Wayne Jancy, from Whitesoul Pump

01:42:06.708 --> 01:42:10.139
and Water Treatment, came out on March 5th to inspect our well and found no

01:42:10.639 --> 01:42:14.503
mechanical issues with the pumping infrastructure. The static water

01:42:14.568 --> 01:42:18.195
level was 11.7 meters below the top casing, which is

01:42:18.292 --> 01:42:22.765
2.34 meters lower than the 2019 when

01:42:22.814 --> 01:42:26.362
Hardin Environmental did a well survey before the Holman gravel pit

01:42:26.862 --> 01:42:31.034
started up. We received a letter in response to our well interference

01:42:31.227 --> 01:42:35.273
complaint from Commissioner Kenneth Brothers referencing Section

01:42:35.322 --> 01:42:38.517
4 of the Region of Waterloo policy and procedures.

01:42:39.127 --> 01:42:42.595
Section 4 refers to the historic compensation area, stating that

01:42:42.643 --> 01:42:45.918
the region would pay $4,000 towards the cost of a new well.

01:42:46.817 --> 01:42:50.509
Then we would have to sign a release waiver to basically go

01:42:51.009 --> 01:42:54.521
away and don't bother us again. We have appealed this response and

01:42:55.021 --> 01:42:59.400
have received a letter back stating that the appeal is justified under the policy where

01:42:59.900 --> 01:43:03.187
compensation provided did not cover the entire cost of the new supply.

01:43:04.150 --> 01:43:07.440
We are to meet with Commissioner Kenneth Brothers later this, this month.

01:43:08.467 --> 01:43:12.383
A Microsoft Teams meeting was sent to us by a very disappointed that Commissioner Brothers

01:43:12.415 --> 01:43:16.026
would not take the time to meet us— with us in person. I have asked

01:43:16.526 --> 01:43:20.022
him to do so. Meanwhile, I'm still trying to manage our water

01:43:20.522 --> 01:43:24.339
use by limiting the amount of laundry I do, not flushing the toilet every time,

01:43:24.371 --> 01:43:27.420
not turning on the dishwasher same day as we shower, etc.

01:43:28.126 --> 01:43:31.913
Every time I turn on the tap, I'm cognizant of the water— the

01:43:32.413 --> 01:43:36.406
amount of water I'm using, hoping to have enough. I couldn't have Easter dinner

01:43:36.906 --> 01:43:40.259
at home for fear of running out of water. My daughter and

01:43:40.759 --> 01:43:44.080
her family with her husband, 2 adult kids, and 2 teenage kids

01:43:44.578 --> 01:43:48.351
who live in Newfoundland are coming next month for a family event. They are not

01:43:48.851 --> 01:43:52.526
able to stay at our home because we will run out of water. How will

01:43:53.026 --> 01:43:56.299
we manage this summer if it's a hot, dry summer? How do I water the

01:43:56.799 --> 01:44:00.586
garden? I'm asking Region not to rescind the 1980

01:44:00.651 --> 01:44:04.649
water taking permit. No further increase should be considered until

01:44:04.681 --> 01:44:08.245
every active well interference claim is resolved to our satisfaction.

01:44:09.000 --> 01:44:12.147
Thank you for our time— for your time. Thank you very

01:44:12.647 --> 01:44:15.391
much, Martha. Councillor Salonen has a question. Um,

01:44:15.647 --> 01:44:19.116
thank you very much, Martha. I— it's technically not

01:44:19.616 --> 01:44:22.793
a question, um, so if you will indulge me, Chair, that's my

01:44:23.293 --> 01:44:26.646
question. Um, I'm sorry that that's been your experience. I really

01:44:27.146 --> 01:44:30.612
appreciate that you're continuing to persevere and I'll continue to

01:44:30.660 --> 01:44:33.904
follow up because that's not adding up to me. So,

01:44:34.594 --> 01:44:37.950
um, but thank you very much for continuing to share with council what you're going

01:44:38.046 --> 01:44:41.595
through. Okay, thank you. Thank you, Martha. I have no further questions.

01:44:41.739 --> 01:44:45.609
Thank you for delegating. Uh, the next delegation is Linda Crest

01:44:46.109 --> 01:44:49.880
from Wilmot. Linda, you have 3 minutes. You're here in person. The podium

01:44:50.380 --> 01:44:53.557
is yours. And this is on.

01:44:54.424 --> 01:44:58.344
Great. Good morning, Chair and members of council and audience.

01:44:58.939 --> 01:45:02.232
My name is Linda Kress. I have lived in Wilmot Township

01:45:02.732 --> 01:45:05.895
for most of my life. I live with my husband in a home built in

01:45:06.395 --> 01:45:10.185
1886 by my great-grandparents. It is where my parents

01:45:10.685 --> 01:45:14.764
raised me and my siblings. My connection to this land and to its

01:45:14.828 --> 01:45:18.523
water spans generations. In the early '70s,

01:45:18.941 --> 01:45:22.329
1970s, my father raised conditions concerns

01:45:22.938 --> 01:45:26.367
with the City of Kitchener when municipal wells were being drilled

01:45:26.800 --> 01:45:30.662
in the Shingletown area, 0.3 kilometers up the road

01:45:31.162 --> 01:45:34.299
from us. Dad was told clearly, these wells

01:45:34.572 --> 01:45:38.786
will not affect yours, but they did. Shortly afterward,

01:45:38.899 --> 01:45:42.344
our well, along with surrounding wells, some ponds and

01:45:42.844 --> 01:45:46.510
streams, dried up. Since that time, we have had to purchase our water

01:45:47.010 --> 01:45:50.438
from the city. And now the region. I am sharing this not

01:45:50.454 --> 01:45:54.734
as a personal story, but as part of a documented historical pattern.

01:45:55.648 --> 01:45:59.127
To ensure it is not lost, the following information has been

01:45:59.255 --> 01:46:03.231
organized by Anne Leffler, a fellow Wilmot resident who

01:46:03.247 --> 01:46:06.357
unfortunately could not be with us this morning.

01:46:07.110 --> 01:46:10.589
In 1970, a local farmer, Stuart Shantz,

01:46:11.022 --> 01:46:14.293
and other residents were experiencing serious water problems.

01:46:14.790 --> 01:46:18.704
Wells were running dry, ponds were dropping, and water quality

01:46:18.816 --> 01:46:22.264
was declining. At the same time, municipal wells were

01:46:22.764 --> 01:46:26.403
being developed to supply water to Kitchener, drawing from the same

01:46:26.659 --> 01:46:30.509
aquifer systems. Residents organized. They formed

01:46:31.009 --> 01:46:33.203
the Wilmot Water Resources Association,

01:46:33.733 --> 01:46:37.470
documented the impacts, and petitioned government agencies.

01:46:38.143 --> 01:46:42.634
Importantly, correspondence from the Ontario Water Our Water Resources

01:46:43.134 --> 01:46:47.236
Commission acknowledged that municipal pumping would likely reduce

01:46:47.669 --> 01:46:51.036
flows. The risks were known, even if

01:46:51.536 --> 01:46:56.232
the science was still developing. Permits were issued with monitoring conditions,

01:46:56.649 --> 01:47:00.305
yet despite this, residents continued to report dry

01:47:00.433 --> 01:47:04.121
wells, reduced stream flows, and dropping pond

01:47:04.621 --> 01:47:08.498
levels. By 1980, these concerns led to what became known

01:47:08.675 --> 01:47:12.368
as the Wilmot Agreement, described by the township

01:47:12.868 --> 01:47:15.917
staff as a gentleman's agreement. It was informal,

01:47:16.318 --> 01:47:20.076
not legally binding, but it matters. It shows that

01:47:20.576 --> 01:47:24.155
the Region acknowledged groundwater impacts in Wilmot and that mitigation

01:47:24.428 --> 01:47:27.817
and flow management were necessary. Even into the

01:47:28.317 --> 01:47:32.346
1990s, monitoring shows these aquifers remained sensitive

01:47:32.538 --> 01:47:35.943
to overpumping. So why does this matter today?

01:47:36.656 --> 01:47:40.750
Because it's not speculation, it's history. Wilmot has

01:47:40.863 --> 01:47:44.390
already experienced water scarcity linked to municipal pumping.

01:47:45.579 --> 01:47:49.510
Residents raised concerns, governments acknowledged risks, and impacts

01:47:49.980 --> 01:47:53.572
occurred anyway. My concern and the concern

01:47:53.701 --> 01:47:57.482
of many residents is simple: we do not want history

01:47:57.920 --> 01:48:02.311
to repeat itself. We ask that council respect

01:48:02.584 --> 01:48:06.214
and take this historical record seriously. Ensure it

01:48:06.714 --> 01:48:10.053
is part of your decision-making and proceed with caution when it

01:48:10.553 --> 01:48:13.731
comes to any actions that could affect our groundwater.

01:48:14.309 --> 01:48:17.586
Thank you for your time. Linda, thank you so much for coming. We have a

01:48:18.086 --> 01:48:20.862
question. Councillor Hunnicke. Thank you for delegating today. Um,

01:48:21.119 --> 01:48:24.412
I think just an insight on my part, whatever the cause of the

01:48:24.912 --> 01:48:28.684
current water shortages being experienced by many, well, residents.

01:48:29.182 --> 01:48:32.297
It doesn't make sense to me to take from residents

01:48:32.602 --> 01:48:36.263
who are already struggling to provide for the development

01:48:36.648 --> 01:48:40.646
industry, to provide for future residents. So thank

01:48:41.146 --> 01:48:43.665
you so much for sharing your experience so well.

01:48:44.339 --> 01:48:46.828
Thank you for sharing your opinion. We appreciate it.

01:48:47.856 --> 01:48:51.019
Thank you very much, Linda. That is the end of our delegation,

01:48:51.083 --> 01:48:53.347
so I'm going to suggest we have a 10-minute bio break.

01:48:56.141 --> 01:48:57.715
Councillor Wolff, did you want to intervene?

01:49:02.548 --> 01:49:04.458
My question to the delegate was,

01:49:05.438 --> 01:49:09.612
um, what happened to those neighbors? Did they all— did

01:49:10.112 --> 01:49:13.916
their wells come back by digging, you know, deeper, or did they,

01:49:14.189 --> 01:49:17.368
uh, do what your family did, which is become

01:49:17.416 --> 01:49:20.900
part of the, uh, —water system for their

01:49:20.932 --> 01:49:24.625
kitchen. Linda, do you know the answer to that? It's about your neighbors. Did they

01:49:24.641 --> 01:49:28.591
end up connecting? Can you come back to the podium so people viewing online can

01:49:32.670 --> 01:49:36.507
Okay. I know that some did indeed go

01:49:36.523 --> 01:49:39.911
on city water. I actually, I think quite a few. I think

01:49:40.040 --> 01:49:43.155
that most of the people in Shingletown Village

01:49:43.283 --> 01:49:46.960
itself which is the village that the

01:49:47.265 --> 01:49:50.557
new gravel pit abuts to, I think most of those people

01:49:50.653 --> 01:49:53.881
are on wells now. But they would not have been back in that day.

01:49:55.310 --> 01:49:58.697
They're on wells now? I'm sorry. They're on city water.

01:49:58.842 --> 01:50:02.904
They're on city— Like my family is, yes. Okay. So the people do

01:50:02.920 --> 01:50:07.207
have water? We do have water. Right. It's coming from

01:50:07.336 --> 01:50:10.611
where our wells were originally and now we purchase it from the region.

01:50:10.949 --> 01:50:14.465
Yes. Yeah, okay, thank you. Thank you, Linda, for coming back.

01:50:14.561 --> 01:50:18.431
And sorry, Councillor Wolf, um, for some reason I have a very tired eScribe

01:50:18.931 --> 01:50:22.044
today and it's not showing, uh, people. So we will take a 10-minute,

01:50:22.269 --> 01:50:23.104
uh, bio break.

02:02:56.407 --> 02:03:00.148
So colleagues, I'm going to ask you all to resume your, um, seats and we

02:03:00.648 --> 02:03:04.706
will continue. I want to thank all the delegations.

02:03:04.754 --> 02:03:07.708
Some of them are still here, some of them have gone on with their day.

02:03:08.430 --> 02:03:12.009
And I want to really underscore that this is a complex issue

02:03:12.041 --> 02:03:14.416
that's affecting real people and communities.

02:03:15.315 --> 02:03:18.958
We take it seriously. Our responsibility is to manage

02:03:19.458 --> 02:03:23.324
it carefully and transparently, using the best available data,

02:03:23.436 --> 02:03:26.936
listening closely to residents' concerns and taking action

02:03:27.193 --> 02:03:30.872
where needed while continuing to support sustainable growth

02:03:30.968 --> 02:03:34.727
across the region. We know that this is urgent

02:03:35.065 --> 02:03:38.679
and we're treating it that way. I want to thank all of our staff and

02:03:39.179 --> 02:03:41.795
the experts in the water science, engineering, planning,

02:03:42.229 --> 02:03:45.570
and communications who are at the table every

02:03:45.666 --> 02:03:49.907
day, and I'm looking at a lot of them over here working

02:03:50.084 --> 02:03:53.345
through the challenges and helping us move forward with

02:03:53.845 --> 02:03:57.296
care and urgency. Our job is to protect safe drinking

02:03:57.392 --> 02:04:00.363
water, and our drinking water is safe.

02:04:01.503 --> 02:04:05.663
While moving forward on housing and jobs, we're focused

02:04:06.163 --> 02:04:09.373
on making real progress, not just on talking about

02:04:09.873 --> 02:04:13.757
it. So the conversations that we have had, the ongoing conversations

02:04:13.790 --> 02:04:18.527
we will have, and the further conversations that we will have today are

02:04:19.027 --> 02:04:22.670
very important. I'm now going to call on Ken

02:04:22.702 --> 02:04:26.379
Brothers, Interim Commissioner of Water and Wastewater, to speak about the

02:04:26.879 --> 02:04:29.815
report followed by a presentation. So,

02:04:30.072 --> 02:04:33.364
uh, Ken, I will turn the floor over to you, uh, to talk about the

02:04:33.864 --> 02:04:37.314
Mannheim service area water capacity constraint,

02:04:37.635 --> 02:04:39.482
the update, and the work that is underway.

02:04:41.007 --> 02:04:44.427
Thank you, Chair. You can bring up the presentation, please.

02:04:47.173 --> 02:04:49.935
So before we start off, I thought I'd just offer a comment, uh,

02:04:50.321 --> 02:04:53.388
by one of the delegates, uh, here. I've been away for about

02:04:53.888 --> 02:04:56.487
a week and a half, and upon my return, I was looking at the cascade

02:04:56.987 --> 02:05:01.289
of my emails. Um, I will be meeting with all of the appealed,

02:05:01.787 --> 02:05:05.127
um, uh, applications on the water interference,

02:05:05.722 --> 02:05:09.431
uh, on an appeal process, uh, that has not been communicated

02:05:09.931 --> 02:05:13.860
to them as yet, but we will, and we'll schedule our to ensure

02:05:14.360 --> 02:05:17.772
that all staff and we can meet with everyone's agenda. So I

02:05:18.272 --> 02:05:21.891
just want to clarify that. So moving

02:05:22.391 --> 02:05:26.139
ahead, we'll start today by providing an update on the work underway across

02:05:26.251 --> 02:05:30.691
the organization to address the Mannheim service area water capacity constraint.

02:05:31.348 --> 02:05:34.682
Next slide. As you know, the interim risk

02:05:34.746 --> 02:05:38.497
management framework study is underway and a more fulsome

02:05:38.529 --> 02:05:42.413
update will come forward the next month. So this is a continuous process

02:05:42.445 --> 02:05:45.931
with continuous updates. We are having an ongoing

02:05:46.431 --> 02:05:50.573
meeting with MECP to work together with them on moving infrastructure solutions forward.

02:05:51.119 --> 02:05:54.958
We've got an excellent rapport with them, and meetings are critical to

02:05:54.991 --> 02:05:58.573
ensure that we have a streamlined process for approvals on our infrastructure plans.

02:05:58.942 --> 02:06:02.155
I'm pleased to report that the Pathway water system is back

02:06:02.203 --> 02:06:06.363
online. Extensive upgrades to the Parkway system have restored 60

02:06:06.460 --> 02:06:09.959
liters per second of capacity. Helping to stabilize the water supply

02:06:10.569 --> 02:06:14.499
in the Mannheim service area. Work on the Green Book Water

02:06:14.999 --> 02:06:19.072
Treatment Plant is ongoing and is expected to be completed in the fall of 2026,

02:06:19.714 --> 02:06:23.083
and that is— will return 80 liters per second of capacity.

02:06:25.152 --> 02:06:28.377
Short-term infrastructure work continues to advance, including the side

02:06:28.877 --> 02:06:32.372
stream container at the Mannheim Water Treatment Plant, and this work includes

02:06:32.872 --> 02:06:36.303
a review of the test findings, uh, to inform the

02:06:36.803 --> 02:06:41.709
upcoming concrete and foundational work and design of the piping system that will connect

02:06:42.110 --> 02:06:46.186
the container to the treatment plant. We will keep you apprised

02:06:46.686 --> 02:06:50.421
of these things moving forward. Things are happening rapidly, and, uh, we'll certainly keep

02:06:50.921 --> 02:06:53.550
you apprised of that. So I'd like to turn the floor over to Commissioner,

02:06:53.710 --> 02:06:56.935
uh, Kanopka for a staffing update. Thank you.

02:06:58.523 --> 02:07:02.067
Oh, next slide please. So hiring to support this work

02:07:02.147 --> 02:07:05.609
is underway, and I'll give a quick overview. Uh, the 4

02:07:05.754 --> 02:07:08.960
senior engineers that we're recruiting for are in the interview

02:07:09.460 --> 02:07:12.855
stages, with some expected to move into the reference stage shortly.

02:07:13.993 --> 02:07:17.969
The water resources technologist has taken a bit longer,

02:07:18.081 --> 02:07:20.774
and we are conducting active outreach to candidates.

02:07:21.688 --> 02:07:25.519
The instrument and control technician roles started interviews this

02:07:25.631 --> 02:07:29.850
week. And while not noted on the slide, recruitment for the financial

02:07:29.931 --> 02:07:33.995
analyst position is ongoing, and the communications role to support

02:07:34.495 --> 02:07:38.253
