02:25:06.265 --> 02:25:09.042
Thank you, Chair Redmond. The deck is up.

02:25:12.366 --> 02:25:16.204
Council, as you know, our dedicated water staff have been working tirelessly

02:25:16.220 --> 02:25:19.993
towards solutions to address the water capacity constraint in

02:25:20.493 --> 02:25:24.280
the Mannheim service area. Staff identified an immediate options

02:25:24.585 --> 02:25:26.769
to reduce the water supply capacity constraint.

02:25:27.941 --> 02:25:31.939
One of those options brought forward by staff is to rescind the 1980

02:25:32.035 --> 02:25:35.166
Wilmont water taking to permanently transfer the

02:25:35.666 --> 02:25:39.694
30 liters per second capacity to the Mannheim service area where

02:25:40.194 --> 02:25:42.103
there is current water capacity constraint.

02:25:43.596 --> 02:25:47.113
While many of the staff were not involved in the 1980 policy decisions

02:25:48.092 --> 02:25:52.186
around the long-term testing, they were reviewed They

02:25:52.686 --> 02:25:55.775
have reviewed all of the relevant historical information as detailed in

02:25:55.823 --> 02:25:57.442
the report before you today.

02:26:00.198 --> 02:26:03.467
Staff have been and are committed to bring forward new information

02:26:03.660 --> 02:26:07.634
in a transparent manner to both Council and the general public as

02:26:07.730 --> 02:26:10.214
it becomes available and verified by staff.

02:26:11.752 --> 02:26:15.630
So I'll call upon Director Shaw to give you an outline

02:26:15.678 --> 02:26:18.483
on the presentation and the Participants,

02:26:19.752 --> 02:26:23.381
thank you. Uh, thank you, Regional Council. Today we

02:26:23.445 --> 02:26:26.961
bring forward a report outlining key studies completed in Wilmot

02:26:26.977 --> 02:26:30.638
Centre, as well as an up-to-date detailed hydrogeological

02:26:30.670 --> 02:26:34.251
assessment of aquifer conditions by SLR Consulting.

02:26:34.941 --> 02:26:39.004
In this report, we confirm that the Wilmot Centre wells have sufficient capacity

02:26:39.116 --> 02:26:42.488
to meet the forecasted growth needs of Baden and New Hamburg through

02:26:42.988 --> 02:26:46.711
to 2051. Current water taking from the Wilmot Centre wellfield is

02:26:46.759 --> 02:26:50.886
sustainable and follows the recommendations for staged long-term pumping

02:26:51.399 --> 02:26:54.980
increases to confirm water supply available for growth in Wilmot

02:26:54.996 --> 02:26:58.850
Township. I will now turn it over to Jeff Morose, the manager,

02:26:58.866 --> 02:27:02.494
hydrogeology and water programs, who will walk you through his

02:27:02.543 --> 02:27:06.011
presentation and the evidence-based rationale behind

02:27:06.511 --> 02:27:08.901
the recommendation to permanently rescind the policy.

02:27:10.266 --> 02:27:14.344
Thank you. And thank you, Council. At the

02:27:14.844 --> 02:27:18.952
request of Council, this report provides additional information about the Wilmot Centre wellfield

02:27:19.017 --> 02:27:22.838
within the context of staff's recommendation to allow the transfer of

02:27:22.870 --> 02:27:26.836
30 litres per second of surplus water to the Mannheim service area while

02:27:26.868 --> 02:27:30.112
continuing to protect long-term water supply for growth

02:27:30.612 --> 02:27:34.190
in Wilmot Township, as well as, as well as private wells and the natural environment.

02:27:35.057 --> 02:27:38.766
The 1980 policy applies to the combined pumping from Erb Street,

02:27:39.087 --> 02:27:41.463
Mannheim West, and Wilmot Centre well fields.

02:27:42.459 --> 02:27:45.574
Despite increasing pumping from Wilmot Centre beginning in 2019,

02:27:46.216 --> 02:27:49.507
the region has not violated the total allowable flows that

02:27:50.007 --> 02:27:52.510
can be taken from Wilmot Township under the 1980 policy.

02:27:54.179 --> 02:27:57.728
The 2011 Baden-New Hamburg Master Plan recommended staged increases

02:27:58.228 --> 02:28:02.095
to pumping at Wilmot Centre to provide long-term testing to confirm sustainability

02:28:02.593 --> 02:28:06.302
of proposed water takings to support growth in the Baden-New Hamburg

02:28:06.802 --> 02:28:10.252
service area out to 2041. At the time the pumping test

02:28:10.300 --> 02:28:13.640
was initiated, a constraint in the Mannheim service area had not

02:28:13.688 --> 02:28:17.300
yet been identified, and the intent of this testing was for

02:28:17.800 --> 02:28:21.957
the benefit of long-term planning for Baden and New Hamburg. As with

02:28:22.021 --> 02:28:25.698
all pumping, any amount of pumping lowers groundwater levels in

02:28:25.714 --> 02:28:27.856
an aquifer. Aquifer, even from private well pumping.

02:28:28.911 --> 02:28:32.288
Lowering of groundwater levels does not mean that water taking is unsustainable.

02:28:33.520 --> 02:28:36.928
There is an acceptable amount of groundwater lowering that can occur in an

02:28:37.428 --> 02:28:41.451
aquifer while still maintaining sustainability of the aquifer systems and

02:28:41.951 --> 02:28:45.261
the natural environment. Long-term operational testing is not

02:28:45.309 --> 02:28:49.213
unique to Wilmot Centre. When any new, uh, supply well comes online,

02:28:49.519 --> 02:28:52.844
it takes several years to understand long-term environmental responses

02:28:52.876 --> 02:28:56.089
to pumping. This is a long-term operational test,

02:28:56.394 --> 02:28:59.510
which basically happens with every new supply well that comes online.

02:29:01.052 --> 02:29:04.345
Historical aquifer tests support that surface water features within the Wilmot

02:29:04.845 --> 02:29:06.963
Centre well field are not being impacted by pumping.

02:29:08.296 --> 02:29:11.926
Given the 53 years of pumping at the well field and consistent range

02:29:12.426 --> 02:29:15.749
of water levels, long-term monitoring data supports the conclusion of a

02:29:15.765 --> 02:29:19.826
long-term sustainable pumping rate for the well field of at least 158

02:29:20.326 --> 02:29:23.772
litres per second, which is higher than what is currently being pumped

02:29:24.048 --> 02:29:27.333
from the well field. If the 1980 policy is not

02:29:27.365 --> 02:29:30.656
rescinded, this will contradict the approach that had been established for

02:29:30.704 --> 02:29:34.234
the water supply strategy update as presented in staff report

02:29:34.299 --> 02:29:37.223
EES-WAS-24008.

02:29:38.476 --> 02:29:42.075
There are no alternative solutions to immediately provide 30 litres

02:29:42.107 --> 02:29:45.814
per second of water to the Mannheim service area. If the Region

02:29:45.830 --> 02:29:48.988
can't permanently transfer this water to the Mannheim

02:29:49.488 --> 02:29:52.194
service area, it will delay growth in Kitchener, Waterloo,

02:29:52.579 --> 02:29:54.791
parts of Cambridge, and in the township of Woolwich.

02:29:56.491 --> 02:30:00.001
Uh, with that, um, I'm going to jump into my presentation.

02:30:00.659 --> 02:30:05.131
Uh, accompanying me today are some of the industry leaders in water supply hydrogeology in

02:30:05.228 --> 02:30:09.059
Southern Ontario. Craig Johnston, uh, from SLR Consulting,

02:30:09.492 --> 02:30:12.944
was the lead hydrogeologist on the 2026 Wilmot Centre Aquifer

02:30:13.444 --> 02:30:17.328
Assessment. Craig has extensive experience in many well fields

02:30:17.424 --> 02:30:21.246
throughout the region, including Wilmot Centre and the Mannheim well

02:30:21.294 --> 02:30:24.650
fields. Craig obtained his Bachelor of Science from

02:30:24.826 --> 02:30:29.145
McMaster and Master's in Hydrogeology from the University of Waterloo and

02:30:29.645 --> 02:30:33.545
has 34 years of municipal water supply experience, including work for various municipalities,

02:30:33.979 --> 02:30:37.126
including the Region of Waterloo, City of Guelph, Region of Peel,

02:30:37.591 --> 02:30:41.027
City of Hamilton, Township of Amaranth, and the City of Winnipeg.

02:30:42.633 --> 02:30:46.182
Leslie Veal with Stantec Consulting was the lead hydrogeologist

02:30:46.682 --> 02:30:50.035
on the 2018 short-term operational testing program in Wilmot Centre.

02:30:50.918 --> 02:30:55.719
Leslie also has extensive experience in the Mannheim well field and

02:30:55.735 --> 02:30:59.814
is a senior hydrogeologist at Stantec with 23 years of consulting experience.

02:31:00.360 --> 02:31:04.166
She completed her bachelor's in environmental science at the University of Guelph and her master's

02:31:04.666 --> 02:31:08.712
in earth science at the University of Waterloo in 2002. Leslie focuses on planning,

02:31:08.808 --> 02:31:11.844
exploration, development, and monitoring of groundwater supplies.

02:31:13.144 --> 02:31:16.598
And Dr. Dave Rudolph, who was here for the

02:31:17.098 --> 02:31:20.758
first 2 hours, unfortunately had to leave. He's meeting with Finnish authorities

02:31:20.790 --> 02:31:23.568
to discuss water supply and transfer of knowledge.

02:31:24.580 --> 02:31:27.921
So unfortunately he had to leave for meetings with the Finnish authorities, so he can't

02:31:28.421 --> 02:31:32.223
join us anymore. Dr. Rudolph completed his Bachelor of Science

02:31:32.272 --> 02:31:35.788
in Geological Engineering at the University of Manitoba and completed his master's

02:31:36.288 --> 02:31:39.432
and PhD thesis in hydrogeology at the University of Waterloo.

02:31:40.123 --> 02:31:43.751
Dr. Rudolph completed a peer review of the 2026 SLR report.

02:31:44.955 --> 02:31:48.439
Dr. Rudolph has been a professor of hydrogeology at the University of Waterloo for

02:31:48.939 --> 02:31:52.903
the past 35 years, with his research focused on teaching— research

02:31:53.288 --> 02:31:57.227
and teaching focused on regional groundwater resource Resources Management internationally,

02:31:58.288 --> 02:32:02.161
conducting a significant amount of research on the Waterloo Moraine for the

02:32:02.661 --> 02:32:05.058
past 30 years with a focus on sustainable development,

02:32:05.447 --> 02:32:09.157
management, and protection of groundwater resources in Waterloo Region.

02:32:11.192 --> 02:32:14.427
And with that, I will move into my presentation.

02:32:14.927 --> 02:32:21.932
[FOREIGN LANGUAGE] So

02:32:21.948 --> 02:32:25.335
I'd like to start by reminding everybody that, as stated in the staff

02:32:25.835 --> 02:32:29.106
report, population increases in Wilmot Township will amount

02:32:29.606 --> 02:32:32.798
to approximately 11,500 or 73% increase

02:32:33.298 --> 02:32:37.083
by the year 2051 over and above the 2025 population today.

02:32:38.014 --> 02:32:41.416
So even with the reallocation of 30 liters per second of water from

02:32:41.464 --> 02:32:45.364
the Wilmot Center well field, we'll be able to meet the growth

02:32:45.717 --> 02:32:48.414
needs of Wilmot Township out to the year 2051.

02:32:49.811 --> 02:32:50.341
Next slide, please.

02:32:53.889 --> 02:32:57.277
There are strong regulatory frameworks to protect water in Ontario,

02:32:58.160 --> 02:33:01.179
which are governed by the Ministry of the Environment, Conservation and Parks.

02:33:02.078 --> 02:33:05.835
This includes approval authority over municipal and private water takings

02:33:05.948 --> 02:33:10.155
through the Ontario Water Resources Act and Ontario Regulation 387/04,

02:33:11.006 --> 02:33:13.221
the water taking and transfer regulation.

02:33:14.038 --> 02:33:17.918
Further, private wells are governed by the province under Ontario Regulation 903,

02:33:18.062 --> 02:33:21.332
the Wells Regulation. Next slide,

02:33:21.348 --> 02:33:21.557
please.

02:33:25.837 --> 02:33:29.540
The 1980 policy was established under a very different legislative

02:33:30.040 --> 02:33:33.516
framework in Ontario that was much less protective of water resources at the

02:33:34.016 --> 02:33:37.219
time. The policy was adopted prior to the existence

02:33:37.719 --> 02:33:41.196
of the Ontario Water Resources Act or the Water Taking and Transfer Regulation.

02:33:42.190 --> 02:33:45.557
Today's provincial legislative framework provides robust protection

02:33:46.057 --> 02:33:49.743
and regulation of sustainable water taking in the province of Ontario above

02:33:50.243 --> 02:33:53.928
and beyond that of the 1980s. In addition, the Region

02:33:54.041 --> 02:33:57.136
has exercised the precautionary principle by completing

02:33:57.184 --> 02:34:01.065
a significant number of technical investigations, environmental monitoring

02:34:01.113 --> 02:34:04.929
installations, and ongoing monitoring well above and beyond

02:34:05.042 --> 02:34:08.924
what is required on our permit to take water. Despite this,

02:34:09.534 --> 02:34:13.047
the Region has not exceeded the total allowable flows that can be taken from

02:34:13.063 --> 02:34:16.705
Wilmot Township under the 1980 agreement. Next slide,

02:34:16.753 --> 02:34:20.090
please. On this slide,

02:34:20.203 --> 02:34:22.738
I provide you with an overall summary of the 1980 policy.

02:34:23.893 --> 02:34:27.262
Water is supplied from 3 separate well fields to the IUS from the

02:34:27.762 --> 02:34:31.868
Township of Wilmot. These 3 well fields are the Wilmot Center Well Field, Manheim West

02:34:31.916 --> 02:34:35.564
Wellfield and Erb Street Wellfield. The limits

02:34:35.692 --> 02:34:39.034
are based on recorded water takings in the year 1978 from

02:34:39.066 --> 02:34:41.396
these wellfields, as outlined in this table.

02:34:43.067 --> 02:34:47.679
The 1980 policy allows the Region to take up to 434.6

02:34:47.888 --> 02:34:51.085
litres per second cumulatively from the Township of Wilmot every

02:34:51.117 --> 02:34:55.070
year to send to the IUS. Since 2019, the Region

02:34:55.118 --> 02:34:57.062
has stayed consistently below this total.

02:34:58.347 --> 02:35:02.217
Under the 1980 policy, the region could take an additional 65

02:35:02.345 --> 02:35:05.894
to 75 meters per second more water from Lil'wat Township than

02:35:06.394 --> 02:35:10.486
what is currently being pumped. But that doesn't mean we should do that.

02:35:11.658 --> 02:35:15.287
All of that pumping, that additional pumping would need to be focused in the Mannheim

02:35:15.303 --> 02:35:18.707
area where we currently have localized declines in water levels

02:35:19.205 --> 02:35:21.790
within the aquifer in that specific area.

02:35:22.592 --> 02:35:26.149
Increasing pumping rates would make those water level conditions worse in the Mannheim

02:35:26.197 --> 02:35:30.331
area and potentially cause us to breach MECP-imposed

02:35:30.524 --> 02:35:34.337
trigger levels. And it creates a higher risk of causing well interferences

02:35:34.674 --> 02:35:38.311
in the Mannheim area. So by taking an adaptive management

02:35:38.811 --> 02:35:42.510
approach, we can effectively manage our water supply by spreading

02:35:42.606 --> 02:35:45.586
out pumping more evenly across a larger area.

02:35:46.468 --> 02:35:50.123
This reduces the likelihood of causing adverse impacts and allow staff

02:35:50.155 --> 02:35:54.121
to make adjustments as we refine our understanding of our aquifers through

02:35:54.169 --> 02:35:56.755
long-term monitoring. Next slide, please.

02:36:03.322 --> 02:36:06.934
I think back one slide, sorry. Oh,

02:36:07.320 --> 02:36:10.595
nope, next slide.

02:36:11.430 --> 02:36:15.605
This figure depicts annual takings from the Wilmot

02:36:16.105 --> 02:36:19.555
Township well fields that, uh, with water taking transfer that happens to

02:36:19.619 --> 02:36:23.553
the Mannheim service area. So as you can see, uh, with increased

02:36:24.053 --> 02:36:26.411
pumping rates in the year 2019 from Wilmot Centre,

02:36:27.069 --> 02:36:30.184
despite that increase, we have remained consistently

02:36:30.682 --> 02:36:34.279
and well below the total overall takings allowable

02:36:34.793 --> 02:36:38.454
by the 1980 policy. Next slide,

02:36:38.486 --> 02:36:38.694
please.

02:36:43.681 --> 02:36:47.415
The Baden-New Hamburg Master Plan recommended a staged approach to increase pumping

02:36:47.543 --> 02:36:51.116
over time from the Wilmot Centre wellfield. Following this recommendation,

02:36:51.789 --> 02:36:55.283
the Wilmot Centre long-term monitoring program was developed in order to

02:36:55.299 --> 02:36:59.962
identify potential effects on local water resources from

02:37:00.462 --> 02:37:03.856
staged increases of pumping at Wilmot Centre. This staged

02:37:03.888 --> 02:37:07.878
testing was meant to confirm that future growth could be accommodated for,

02:37:08.199 --> 02:37:11.853
for Baden-New Hamburg. Rather than jumping straight into

02:37:12.078 --> 02:37:16.550
staged long-term testing, the Region initiated a short-term operational

02:37:17.050 --> 02:37:21.246
testing program. This is a demonstration of the Region following the precautionary

02:37:21.278 --> 02:37:25.173
principle. This testing was not initiated for the purpose

02:37:25.590 --> 02:37:29.260
of benefiting the IUS. To execute

02:37:29.372 --> 02:37:32.626
the short-term test, excess supply above and beyond the

02:37:32.642 --> 02:37:36.092
water demands of Baden and New Hamburg were diverted to the IUS.

02:37:38.581 --> 02:37:42.034
Alternatively, we could have pumped the water to waste in a

02:37:42.534 --> 02:37:46.002
ditch along Bleems Road. However, this could have caused erosion of

02:37:46.050 --> 02:37:49.359
the ditch, erosion of downstream stormwater infrastructure,

02:37:49.840 --> 02:37:53.374
and erosion of creeks. It could have also presented

02:37:53.874 --> 02:37:57.791
potential flooding concerns during precipitation periods. Letters were

02:37:58.291 --> 02:38:02.417
mailed to residents in May 2018 in advance of

02:38:02.917 --> 02:38:06.771
this testing program. Further, the Wilmot Centre Public Liaison Committee was notified in 2017 and

02:38:06.787 --> 02:38:10.370
advised of the decision to send water to the IOS rather than discharging

02:38:10.402 --> 02:38:13.825
to waste. And as an aside, we confirmed

02:38:13.937 --> 02:38:17.199
the attendance list and Mr. Nkurun was invited to the meeting.

02:38:22.453 --> 02:38:25.827
This was also communicated in the letter mailed to residents where increased

02:38:26.327 --> 02:38:30.951
flow within the water mains would occur. No well interference— interference

02:38:31.451 --> 02:38:34.147
complaints were received throughout the duration of the short-term testing.

02:38:36.187 --> 02:38:39.929
The report concluded that water can be sustainably taken from Wilmot Centre

02:38:40.427 --> 02:38:44.025
up to 158 litres per second or more, which is above

02:38:44.137 --> 02:38:46.482
what we are currently pumping from the wellfield today.

02:38:48.056 --> 02:38:51.975
Further, it was concluded that the region could proceed immediately

02:38:52.088 --> 02:38:56.819
to a long-term test at 158 liters per second.

02:38:57.220 --> 02:38:57.829
Next slide, please.

02:39:02.045 --> 02:39:05.925
So again, going back to the Beethan New Hanover Master Plan, long-term stepped testing

02:39:05.957 --> 02:39:09.644
was recommended to confirm the water supply for the communities in Wilmot

02:39:10.144 --> 02:39:14.084
Township for future growth. The purpose of the long-term operational testing

02:39:14.213 --> 02:39:17.948
is to evaluate long-term responses to pumping in the natural environment over the course

02:39:18.044 --> 02:39:21.400
of several years. As we saw in Mannheim, it took a long

02:39:21.512 --> 02:39:25.175
data set to tease out the long-term responses to pumping while considering long-term

02:39:25.207 --> 02:39:28.548
climate trends. Rather than proceeding to a long-term

02:39:28.580 --> 02:39:32.178
test immediately to the full permitted rate of 158

02:39:32.678 --> 02:39:36.386
liters per second, the Region followed the precautionary principle and initiated

02:39:36.886 --> 02:39:40.354
a stepped increase with the first step at 135 liters

02:39:40.386 --> 02:39:44.080
per second. Long-term testing provides robust data

02:39:44.580 --> 02:39:47.925
sets that that allow for the assessment of sustainability over the long term while considering

02:39:48.037 --> 02:39:52.103
pumping, climate change, and recharge. Operational testing

02:39:52.119 --> 02:39:55.726
like this is not unique to Wilmot Centre. This is standard practice

02:39:55.888 --> 02:39:59.613
for any wellfield to evaluate long-term data and trends over time.

02:40:00.032 --> 02:40:04.144
This is not experimental pumping. Testing commenced

02:40:04.644 --> 02:40:08.596
in 2019 with excess supply above and beyond the demands of Baden-New Hamburg.

02:40:09.916 --> 02:40:13.620
This excess supply was sent to the US for the same reasons I mentioned previously.

02:40:16.891 --> 02:40:20.676
Next slide, please. Along with assessing sustainable

02:40:21.176 --> 02:40:25.118
water supply capacity to meet future growth in Wilmont Township, the long-term operational

02:40:25.150 --> 02:40:28.935
testing program and transfer of water to the US has provided

02:40:29.435 --> 02:40:33.553
the co-benefit of giving staff surplus water supply capacity

02:40:33.810 --> 02:40:37.805
to complete important asset renewal work. Such as the Strain Street,

02:40:38.238 --> 02:40:42.381
Williams Street, Parkway Reservoir upgrades, and emergency

02:40:42.477 --> 02:40:45.191
renewal work at the Greenbrook Water Treatment Facility.

02:40:46.427 --> 02:40:49.446
Without these additional flows from Wilmot Centre, it would have been very

02:40:49.478 --> 02:40:53.235
challenging or impossible to complete this critical asset renewal

02:40:53.735 --> 02:40:57.185
work. This work was required to maintain a fit state of

02:40:57.685 --> 02:41:01.055
repair. The Safe Drinking Water Act states that every owner of a municipal drinking

02:41:01.103 --> 02:41:04.651
water system shall ensure that at all times in which it is in service,

02:41:05.197 --> 02:41:07.943
the drinking water system is maintained in a fit state of repair.

02:41:09.211 --> 02:41:10.945
There are other co-benefits as well.

02:41:12.358 --> 02:41:16.196
This allows pumping rates to be held consistently at 135 liters per

02:41:16.696 --> 02:41:19.873
second as part of a long-term pumping test, rather than fluctuating with

02:41:19.889 --> 02:41:23.373
demands from Baden and New Hamburg. This allows for a

02:41:23.421 --> 02:41:26.601
cleaner data set that's easier to analyze and is more

02:41:26.649 --> 02:41:29.842
robust. It also provides practical

02:41:29.858 --> 02:41:33.788
and responsible use of ratepayer funds and natural

02:41:34.288 --> 02:41:37.719
resources by using this water for the benefit of the community rather

02:41:38.219 --> 02:41:41.745
than sending excess water to waste and risking environmental damage.

02:41:43.879 --> 02:41:44.424
Next slide, please.

02:41:49.093 --> 02:41:53.039
Given the 53 years of pumping at the Wilmot Centre well field and relatively consistent

02:41:53.539 --> 02:41:57.194
range of water levels, Long-term monitoring data supports the conclusion that

02:41:57.694 --> 02:42:01.510
the long-term sustainable pumping rate for the wellfield is at least 158

02:42:01.622 --> 02:42:05.392
liters per second. Surface water features have been demonstrated

02:42:05.892 --> 02:42:08.569
to not be impacted by pumping from the Wilmot Center wellfield,

02:42:08.922 --> 02:42:11.794
including the Shingletown Wetland as part of this assessment.

02:42:14.120 --> 02:42:17.666
Water level declines become less pronounced the further away from the well you go.

02:42:18.468 --> 02:42:21.403
Despite the level of pumping, Water level declines with,

02:42:21.916 --> 02:42:25.812
with pumping in Wilmot Centre have been quite small within the 2 to 2.5

02:42:25.908 --> 02:42:29.756
metre range in the immediate vicinity of the supply wells and

02:42:29.917 --> 02:42:32.707
less as you go further away from the wells with distance.

02:42:34.342 --> 02:42:36.907
The next few slides will dig into the key issues that have been raised by

02:42:37.407 --> 02:42:39.441
council and delegates in Wilmot Centre. Next, please.

02:42:45.085 --> 02:42:48.339
The SLR 2026 report concluded that the Shingletown Wetland

02:42:48.420 --> 02:42:52.164
appears to be located beyond the zone of influence of the Wilmot Centre wellfield,

02:42:52.372 --> 02:42:56.229
with no responses to pumping evident in the water level data from

02:42:56.245 --> 02:42:59.619
the 2026 Hardin Report at the Asbaugh Sand and Gravel

02:43:00.119 --> 02:43:03.283
Pit. Water level responses and surface water features,

02:43:03.476 --> 02:43:07.670
including the Shingletown Wetland, are interpreted to be affected by longer-term periods

02:43:07.879 --> 02:43:11.559
of below-average precipitation and recharge than by changes in pumping

02:43:11.655 --> 02:43:15.125
from the wellfield. In fact, the normal winter water

02:43:15.625 --> 02:43:18.304
level drop that we would see from 2019 to 2020.

02:43:19.203 --> 02:43:22.447
After that, we saw increasing water levels once again in the wetland,

02:43:22.591 --> 02:43:25.883
despite the fact that the Region had increased its pumping at Wilmot Centre.

02:43:27.713 --> 02:43:31.230
In addition, the water levels in the fall of 2020 in the wetland

02:43:31.728 --> 02:43:34.778
were higher than the water levels in the fall of 2019,

02:43:35.196 --> 02:43:38.536
despite the fact the Region had initiated an increased pumping

02:43:38.568 --> 02:43:41.504
rate for that time. Next slide, please.

02:43:46.348 --> 02:43:50.181
Water level triggers were assigned in the Wilmot Centre wellfield based

02:43:50.681 --> 02:43:54.158
on monitoring conducted from 1998 to present and have been adjusted over time.

02:43:55.313 --> 02:43:58.922
The report states that these trigger levels may not be representative of

02:43:59.002 --> 02:44:02.899
historical conditions in the wellfield. Although trigger levels have

02:44:02.947 --> 02:44:06.813
been crossed in recent years, a review of the long-term hydrographs

02:44:06.957 --> 02:44:10.471
indicated that the observed water levels over the past 6 years are

02:44:10.971 --> 02:44:15.047
consistent with historical water level trends. Crossing trigger

02:44:15.547 --> 02:44:19.142
levels at Wilmot Centre does not mean that adverse impacts

02:44:19.642 --> 02:44:23.333
are anticipated at nearby private wells or in the natural environment.

02:44:24.216 --> 02:44:27.828
This is a fundamental difference from the trigger levels that we have on

02:44:28.328 --> 02:44:30.173
our permit to take water for the Mannheim well field.

02:44:31.666 --> 02:44:34.909
Historically, there have been much lower water levels in Wilmot

02:44:35.409 --> 02:44:39.357
Centre despite lower pumping rates historically.

02:44:40.577 --> 02:44:44.238
This also is a demonstration of how significant precipitation patterns are

02:44:44.608 --> 02:44:47.883
in the aquifers in Wilmot Centre in comparison to municipal

02:44:48.383 --> 02:44:52.315
pumping. The fact the water levels rebound following periods

02:44:52.815 --> 02:44:56.216
of drought speaks to the resilience of the aquifer and the sustainability

02:44:56.345 --> 02:44:59.348
of water takings in Wilmot Centre. Next slide, please.

02:45:02.979 --> 02:45:06.947
Prior to February 2026, the Region had not received a well interference complaint

02:45:06.996 --> 02:45:09.550
from the Wilmot Centre area since 2008.

02:45:10.852 --> 02:45:14.773
No two private wells are the same. They are evaluated individually

02:45:14.965 --> 02:45:18.115
based on their own unique characteristics. Water loss

02:45:18.163 --> 02:45:22.244
from private wells could be for many number of reasons, including mechanical issues

02:45:22.501 --> 02:45:25.699
such as a pump reaching its end of life, electrical malfunction,

02:45:25.956 --> 02:45:29.472
failing pressure tanks, lack of proper well maintenance through

02:45:29.729 --> 02:45:32.443
routine well rehabilitation, periods of drought,

02:45:33.342 --> 02:45:36.088
plumbing issues, regional pumping interference,

02:45:36.650 --> 02:45:40.118
or private water taking interference. But there are mitigation options.

02:45:41.145 --> 02:45:44.565
These include lowering pumps in wells, deepening private wells,

02:45:45.240 --> 02:45:48.740
or outright replacing private wells, following the Council endorsed policy

02:45:48.756 --> 02:45:52.127
and procedures for responding to groundwater interference complaints in

02:45:52.627 --> 02:45:55.540
the unlikely event that the Region's pumping has impacted a private well.

02:45:56.068 --> 02:45:57.078
Next slide, please.

02:46:00.796 --> 02:46:03.856
This figure may be a little bit difficult to see, so I apologize for the

02:46:04.356 --> 02:46:08.295
scale, but it depicts the areas of pumping in Wilmot Centre

02:46:08.407 --> 02:46:12.060
based on measured water level responses during the 2018

02:46:12.573 --> 02:46:16.291
operational testing program. The figure you see in front of you represents

02:46:16.499 --> 02:46:20.301
shallow aquifer conditions in aquifer AFB1.

02:46:21.136 --> 02:46:24.668
This is shallower than the municipal supply aquifer, AFB2.

02:46:25.471 --> 02:46:29.421
We would expect to find more of the shallow dug wells in this particular aquifer.

02:46:30.786 --> 02:46:34.993
The outer blue line that you see represents the 10-centimeter level

02:46:35.041 --> 02:46:37.899
of change from pumping during this testing.

02:46:38.975 --> 02:46:42.973
Anything outside of the blue line is not expected to have any measurable response

02:46:43.473 --> 02:46:46.997
from pumping. These lines were based

02:46:47.497 --> 02:46:51.531
on observed water levels during the test at permitted—

02:46:51.788 --> 02:46:55.009
at the full permitted pumping rate of 158 litres per

02:46:55.509 --> 02:46:58.662
second. So as you can see, the area of influence in the shallow aquifer is

02:46:58.758 --> 02:47:01.867
fairly limited to within the closest proximity

02:47:01.899 --> 02:47:04.191
to the supply wells. Next slide, please.

02:47:08.870 --> 02:47:11.036
Previous slide, please. Yes. Thank you.

02:47:12.627 --> 02:47:16.418
This figure shows conditions in AFB2. This is the municipal

02:47:16.918 --> 02:47:19.969
supply aquifer, and this is also the aquifer where you would expect to

02:47:20.017 --> 02:47:23.954
find more of the deeper drilled private wells installed in the Wilmot Centre

02:47:24.034 --> 02:47:27.488
area. Once again, the outer edge, the last blue ring that

02:47:27.988 --> 02:47:32.084
you see, the largest area, represents 10 centimeters of drawdown based on measured response

02:47:32.584 --> 02:47:36.695
data from the 2018 test. The next circle in represents

02:47:37.195 --> 02:47:39.906
the 50-centimeter decline in water levels related to pumping.

02:47:41.383 --> 02:47:44.900
And the smallest one in the immediate vicinity of

02:47:45.253 --> 02:47:48.014
the supply wells is the 1-meter drawdown contour.

02:47:49.973 --> 02:47:53.779
Even in very close proximity to the supply wells, only approximately

02:47:53.843 --> 02:47:57.022
1 meter of measured response was observed

02:47:58.740 --> 02:48:01.711
inside of that blue— at that blue line on the very inside.

02:48:02.578 --> 02:48:06.272
This is remarkable considering this is in the supply aquifer right beside

02:48:06.772 --> 02:48:09.838
the wells. This again was under pumping rates at the full permitted

02:48:10.338 --> 02:48:13.934
amount of 158 liters per second, which is less than we're currently producing today.

02:48:17.613 --> 02:48:21.339
And these lines are bigger than the one we saw in the previous

02:48:21.371 --> 02:48:24.038
slide. That's expected. This is response data,

02:48:25.082 --> 02:48:28.149
water level response data from in the municipal supply supply aquifer.

02:48:28.999 --> 02:48:33.154
As I mentioned earlier, when you pump a well, you will lower groundwater elevations.

02:48:33.410 --> 02:48:36.939
You can't get around that. That's just the physics of it. But there is

02:48:37.439 --> 02:48:40.902
an acceptable amount of lowering that can happen to ensure that water taking is

02:48:41.402 --> 02:48:43.901
still sustainable. Next slide, please.

02:48:47.783 --> 02:48:50.109
This is the part you've all been waiting for— the data.

02:48:52.178 --> 02:48:54.937
The last time I brought information to you, it was for the Mannheim well field.

02:48:55.708 --> 02:48:58.791
This is a very similar plot, but with over 50 years of data.

02:48:59.353 --> 02:49:03.126
I'm going to explain what you're seeing on this graph

02:49:03.335 --> 02:49:06.402
before I move through some of the slide transitions. The blue

02:49:06.902 --> 02:49:10.946
and black dots represent water levels measured from monitoring wells in close proximity

02:49:11.235 --> 02:49:14.398
to the region's municipal supply wells, very close to our supply wells

02:49:15.634 --> 02:49:19.086
in the municipal aquifer. This is the same type of situation I

02:49:19.586 --> 02:49:23.164
showed for the Mannheim area. The black line that you

02:49:23.664 --> 02:49:26.999
see along the bottom below the red dashed line is our annualized

02:49:27.032 --> 02:49:30.932
pumping from Wilmot Centre over time. The red

02:49:30.964 --> 02:49:34.382
dashed line represents the reliable operating flow in

02:49:34.399 --> 02:49:38.283
Wilmot Centre, which is the same as our permit to take water maximum

02:49:38.427 --> 02:49:42.327
of 158 litres per second and what has been confirmed to

02:49:42.343 --> 02:49:46.179
be at the very least a sustainable pumping rate in Wilmot Centre.

02:49:47.737 --> 02:49:51.317
I will note a data gap in this data set. I don't know why monitoring

02:49:51.349 --> 02:49:55.059
didn't happen between the year 1990 and 1999.

02:49:56.070 --> 02:49:59.346
Um, I don't know the decision-making around that, but we still have a very long

02:49:59.843 --> 02:50:01.722
data set to compare other periods to.

02:50:03.777 --> 02:50:07.727
In addition, on this particular figure, we have flagged notable

02:50:08.227 --> 02:50:11.790
drought periods during this time scale. So you can see 4 different drought periods

02:50:11.806 --> 02:50:15.405
listed here. Now I'm going to start adding

02:50:15.421 --> 02:50:17.735
the lines, so if I can get one click forward, please.

02:50:20.804 --> 02:50:24.837
The green trend line that we just added represents select periods of pumping where

02:50:25.337 --> 02:50:28.838
there is a specific trend that's been identified. From 1972 to

02:50:29.338 --> 02:50:32.277
1984, we see a fairly stable annualized pumping trend.

02:50:33.434 --> 02:50:36.535
From 1984 to 1990, we see a period

02:50:36.648 --> 02:50:40.311
of increased pumping From 1990 to 2000,

02:50:40.504 --> 02:50:43.943
we see a period of decreasing pumping. From 2000,

02:50:43.959 --> 02:50:48.024
2010, we see a stable period of pumping. And finally, from 2010

02:50:48.056 --> 02:50:50.755
to 2025, we see an increasing trend of pumping.

02:50:51.848 --> 02:50:55.527
Now I'm going to get one more click forward. Thank you.

02:50:56.315 --> 02:50:59.962
The purple line shows the general trends that have been observed in water level

02:51:00.462 --> 02:51:04.556
responses in the monitoring wells in very close proximity to our supply flywells in

02:51:04.604 --> 02:51:07.750
the supply aquifer. During that

02:51:08.250 --> 02:51:11.649
first period of stable pumping, we see a notable decrease in water levels by about

02:51:12.149 --> 02:51:15.373
2.5 meters. This is expected. As we pump water, we know

02:51:15.873 --> 02:51:19.497
that groundwater levels will decline. Towards the end of the stable pumping

02:51:19.529 --> 02:51:23.172
period, we actually see water levels begin to increase following the drought

02:51:23.204 --> 02:51:26.719
condition that ended in approximately 1982. This shows

02:51:26.735 --> 02:51:30.185
that precipitation and recharge is an important factor influencing water

02:51:30.685 --> 02:51:33.823
level responses. Despite consistent pumping, we see

02:51:33.951 --> 02:51:37.460
water levels going up towards the end of this period. Then we move

02:51:37.492 --> 02:51:40.665
to the second pumping period where we have an increasing trend in water taking.

02:51:41.658 --> 02:51:45.087
It's noted that this increasing trend starts in 1984 and

02:51:45.103 --> 02:51:48.484
water levels continued to go up in the aquifer until

02:51:48.984 --> 02:51:52.522
1986, despite the fact we started increasing our pumping 2 years

02:51:52.570 --> 02:51:55.455
earlier. As we move through Drought Period B,

02:51:55.679 --> 02:51:59.029
we do see a steep decline in water levels. This water level decline

02:51:59.045 --> 02:52:02.972
likely reflects drought conditions and may also be reflected by increased

02:52:03.472 --> 02:52:06.387
pumping over that time period. It's likely a combination of both.

02:52:08.006 --> 02:52:11.548
Moving to Drought Period D, we see water levels decline from 1999

02:52:12.048 --> 02:52:15.604
to 2001, despite the, the region's pumping decreasing.

02:52:16.838 --> 02:52:20.397
From 2000 to 2010, we have very stable

02:52:20.445 --> 02:52:23.619
pumping, and we also see a significant increase in water levels.

02:52:23.700 --> 02:52:27.071
Essentially all the way back to our pre-pumping levels.

02:52:27.987 --> 02:52:31.615
This is remarkable resiliency in this aquifer. Gold star

02:52:31.664 --> 02:52:35.228
for Wilmot Center. It further cements the critical role

02:52:35.728 --> 02:52:38.825
that precipitation and recharge play on water levels in Wilmot Center.

02:52:39.869 --> 02:52:43.481
And then finally, moving through the increasing water taking trend from 2010 to

02:52:43.981 --> 02:52:47.560
2025, as pumping gradually increased, there is a decrease

02:52:48.060 --> 02:52:51.269
in water levels until 2012. Despite continuing to

02:52:51.769 --> 02:52:55.925
increase pumping between 2012 and 2019, there is no noticeable responses

02:52:56.425 --> 02:52:59.698
in water levels based on the trend. The drought period from 2020

02:52:59.779 --> 02:53:03.472
to 2023 coincides with increased pumping rates, which can

02:53:03.972 --> 02:53:07.406
make interpretation difficult. However, based on the full suite of response data,

02:53:07.871 --> 02:53:11.243
we know that precipitation and drought play a significant effect on water

02:53:11.743 --> 02:53:15.354
levels in the aquifer. Water levels appear to have stabilized over the past couple of

02:53:15.854 --> 02:53:19.352
years. So do we have concerns about water levels in Wilmot

02:53:19.852 --> 02:53:23.061
Centre when it comes to our municipal supply wells? Now I'm going to direct you

02:53:23.561 --> 02:53:24.329
to the side of the screen.

02:53:26.674 --> 02:53:29.660
It's a little bit difficult to see, but this is basically what it would look

02:53:29.708 --> 02:53:32.775
like if you were looking at our supply well from the side, if you could

02:53:33.275 --> 02:53:36.773
see through the earth. The light blue line at the top represents the

02:53:37.273 --> 02:53:40.706
maximum observed water level at this monitoring location. The dark

02:53:40.722 --> 02:53:44.312
blue dashed line below, below it shows you the minimum water

02:53:44.812 --> 02:53:48.526
level measured at this location. The black

02:53:48.638 --> 02:53:51.843
line at the very bottom represents the top of the well screen where

02:53:52.343 --> 02:53:55.336
we can't draw water levels below. And the 2— the red,

02:53:55.625 --> 02:53:58.958
the red line just above that is 2 meters above the top of the screen.

02:53:59.310 --> 02:54:02.563
That's ideally where we don't want to pull water levels down below because

02:54:02.579 --> 02:54:05.175
that's roughly where the top of our pump would be. So we call that the

02:54:05.675 --> 02:54:09.053
safe groundwater level elevation. And as you can see, even when

02:54:09.165 --> 02:54:12.578
water levels were at their worst in the early 1990s, there is—

02:54:12.771 --> 02:54:16.088
there was still at least 18 metres of drawdown available at the supply

02:54:16.136 --> 02:54:19.389
wells. Contrast this with the example from Mannheim,

02:54:19.741 --> 02:54:22.754
where we only had 90 centimetres of available drawdown.

02:54:23.427 --> 02:54:27.240
This is a very stark difference, and there is a lot of additional drawdown

02:54:27.288 --> 02:54:30.718
available at the Wilmot Centre well field.

02:54:32.000 --> 02:54:35.015
This is very important from a mitigation perspective.

02:54:35.915 --> 02:54:39.463
In the unlikely event that the Region's pumping does impact private wells in the area,

02:54:39.945 --> 02:54:43.606
the problem can be remedied because of how much water is in

02:54:43.638 --> 02:54:47.026
the aquifer, how much saturated thickness we have in the aquifer.

02:54:47.668 --> 02:54:51.168
If a private well is not drilled deep enough, we can deepen it and

02:54:51.249 --> 02:54:54.492
tap into the more saturated portion of the aquifer closer to

02:54:54.992 --> 02:54:55.488
the base of the aquifer.

02:55:00.899 --> 02:55:02.231
And I'll move you to the next slide, please.

02:55:05.539 --> 02:55:08.831
One more. Thank you.

02:55:10.950 --> 02:55:14.049
So what I've done is I've just taken the trend lines that

02:55:14.549 --> 02:55:17.084
we see in Wilmont Center and I've laid them on top of the trend lines

02:55:17.100 --> 02:55:18.850
that we saw in the Mannheim wellfield.

02:55:20.969 --> 02:55:23.828
The pumping responses are very different between these two wellfields.

02:55:24.902 --> 02:55:28.219
The pumping in Mannheim shows a direct inverse

02:55:28.252 --> 02:55:32.130
relationship to water levels in the aquifer in the Mannheim

02:55:32.630 --> 02:55:35.751
area based on periods of increased pumping, decreased pumping,

02:55:36.665 --> 02:55:40.367
uh, and, and the highest rates of pumping. It's a

02:55:40.431 --> 02:55:43.892
lot messier in Wilmot Centre, which suggests that the influence of

02:55:44.392 --> 02:55:48.251
pumping is not as strong based on the water taking we, we have today compared

02:55:48.299 --> 02:55:51.716
to the Mannheim area. There's a much more significant

02:55:51.812 --> 02:55:55.312
response related to precipitation patterns than what we see

02:55:55.361 --> 02:55:57.416
in the plot below at Mannheim.

02:55:59.776 --> 02:56:03.598
Given the 53 years of pumping at the Wilmot Centre wellfield and relatively

02:56:03.630 --> 02:56:07.323
consistent range of water levels, long-term monitoring and data

02:56:07.823 --> 02:56:12.461
supports the conclusion of a long-term sustainable rate of at least 158

02:56:12.961 --> 02:56:15.399
liters per second, which is higher than what we're currently producing.

02:56:20.401 --> 02:56:22.806
The aquifer is in good health in Wilmot Center.

02:56:24.313 --> 02:56:27.904
Current pumping rates are sustainable. Previous studies

02:56:28.096 --> 02:56:32.280
have confirmed that surface water features in the study area did not respond or

02:56:32.780 --> 02:56:36.400
are impacted by regional pumping, and the wetland does not show responses

02:56:36.432 --> 02:56:39.126
to pumping. Next slide, please.

02:56:43.972 --> 02:56:48.114
The 1980 policy is not aligned with the water supply strategy update,

02:56:49.350 --> 02:56:53.124
with the approach for the water supply strategy update where we look for water.

02:56:53.525 --> 02:56:57.058
We advised council we would be looking for water outside of the IUS

02:56:57.558 --> 02:57:01.008
boundary areas in the townships. The region has found a lot

02:57:01.508 --> 02:57:04.749
of the easy water. We're getting into more difficult options that are located further away

02:57:04.909 --> 02:57:05.519
from the IUS.

02:57:08.521 --> 02:57:11.425
If the Region were bound to individual well field limits as set out by the

02:57:11.441 --> 02:57:15.259
1980 policy, we would need to increase our taking in the Mannheim area and

02:57:15.339 --> 02:57:19.077
further exacerbate the water level issues that we're already having in that

02:57:19.126 --> 02:57:22.062
location. Next slide, please.

02:57:26.185 --> 02:57:30.196
The well field, the Wilmot Centre well field, has been rigorously studied for

02:57:30.696 --> 02:57:34.126
over 50 years. It's drawn The same conclusions time after

02:57:34.626 --> 02:57:38.216
time by independent consultants in different technical reports has been verified

02:57:38.716 --> 02:57:42.387
through third-party peer review by MECP and by Dr.

02:57:42.499 --> 02:57:44.215
Dave Rudolph from the University of Waterloo.

02:57:47.103 --> 02:57:51.289
When wells are pumped, groundwater levels will decline, but there is an acceptable

02:57:51.789 --> 02:57:54.898
amount of decline while sustainable water takings can still occur.

02:57:57.609 --> 02:58:01.604
If the Region is bound to these individual well field limits, it will severely hamper

02:58:02.022 --> 02:58:03.884
our ability to flexibly operate the system.

02:58:05.811 --> 02:58:09.087
And in the unlikely event that the Region shows— that

02:58:09.587 --> 02:58:13.663
the Region does impact a private well, the Region implements the Council-endorsed groundwater interference

02:58:13.679 --> 02:58:17.260
policy and procedure. Staff have provided evidence, data,

02:58:17.276 --> 02:58:21.675
and analysis to support the conclusion that our water taking in Wilmot Township is

02:58:21.707 --> 02:58:25.208
sustainable. If the 1980 policy is rescinded,

02:58:25.898 --> 02:58:29.793
an additional 30 liters per second can be used and sent

02:58:30.293 --> 02:58:33.721
to the US permanently to help reduce the current Mannheim service area supply

02:58:34.221 --> 02:58:37.152
deficit. If the 1980 policy is not rescinded,

02:58:37.809 --> 02:58:41.336
it has significant implications for the region's ability to

02:58:41.352 --> 02:58:42.554
grow and manage the system.

02:58:44.638 --> 02:58:48.229
And I'll open it up to questions. And we

02:58:48.729 --> 02:58:52.278
have a few. So thank you very much for a very thorough presentation. And for

02:58:52.778 --> 02:58:56.087
all the people in the audience that contributed. Colleagues, I'm going to ask for

02:58:56.135 --> 02:59:00.024
one question, one follow-up. Again, looking that we're just before 12

02:59:00.524 --> 02:59:03.660
o'clock and we have some colleagues that will have to leave us, we will continue

02:59:04.160 --> 02:59:07.558
to have quorum so we can continue the meeting, but I would ask you to

02:59:07.769 --> 02:59:11.134
have some respect if you can be brief for other

02:59:11.247 --> 02:59:14.289
colleagues that may want to get a question in before they have to leave.

02:59:14.354 --> 02:59:17.849
So, Councillor Hunnicke, you're first. Thank you for the report

02:59:18.349 --> 02:59:21.526
and the presentation. The regional data and the consultative

02:59:21.558 --> 02:59:25.973
data indicates that more water

02:59:26.473 --> 02:59:30.228
pumping is sustainable in Wilmot. If that is the case, what are we

02:59:30.293 --> 02:59:34.403
to make of the individuals who delegated

02:59:34.903 --> 02:59:38.851
today talking about water shortages in Shingletown and others

02:59:38.899 --> 02:59:40.986
as well who have delegated previously?

02:59:42.592 --> 02:59:46.489
Thank you for the question. And through the Chair, we evaluate well

02:59:46.681 --> 02:59:50.434
interferences individually based on the information available.

02:59:50.690 --> 02:59:53.978
No two private well interferences are the same.

02:59:54.683 --> 02:59:58.709
We have to look at conditions such as the geographic location and proximity

02:59:58.773 --> 03:00:01.788
to our pumping, the depth of the private well,

03:00:02.541 --> 03:00:05.893
the condition of the well, maintenance that's been done over the years.

03:00:06.711 --> 03:00:10.723
The most common well interference complaints that we have dealt with historically have

03:00:11.223 --> 03:00:14.544
largely been mechanical failures. There are

03:00:15.636 --> 03:00:18.703
instances where once those are fixed,

03:00:19.088 --> 03:00:21.754
then water supply is fully restored.

03:00:22.861 --> 03:00:26.105
If proper well maintenance isn't undertaken on a private well,

03:00:27.036 --> 03:00:30.199
you can get well plugging over time as well. So you may have a well

03:00:30.247 --> 03:00:33.619
that starts as being very productive, and if you don't maintain it over time and

03:00:34.119 --> 03:00:37.620
rehabilitate the well, as a standard practice in both private and municipal

03:00:38.120 --> 03:00:41.460
wells, they can plug up over time. They can lose well efficiency,

03:00:42.039 --> 03:00:45.510
and you can see water levels decline much further under

03:00:46.010 --> 03:00:49.350
the same pumping conditions because the well has to work much harder to pull the

03:00:49.850 --> 03:00:53.930
water in from the aquifer. Thank you for that insight. It still seems peculiar

03:00:54.430 --> 03:00:57.626
to me that it's partly like within the time period of the

03:00:58.126 --> 03:01:01.963
experimental pumping. But my second question My question is with respect

03:01:02.463 --> 03:01:06.137
to if the 1980 water strategy permits

03:01:06.637 --> 03:01:10.296
us to pump more water than we're currently pumping,

03:01:11.677 --> 03:01:15.402
like to get to the maximum, then why do we need to change it?

03:01:16.574 --> 03:01:19.721
So the 1980 policy does lay out

03:01:20.221 --> 03:01:23.864
individual water takings that happen at individual

03:01:23.976 --> 03:01:27.204
water level, well fields. This,

03:01:29.180 --> 03:01:33.019
you know, can create some issues with the flexibility of

03:01:33.067 --> 03:01:36.264
staff to actually operating the system. So where we could

03:01:36.764 --> 03:01:40.199
take additional water as allowed, you know, following the guidance

03:01:40.699 --> 03:01:44.215
of the policy and is allowed under our permits, it's probably not the best idea

03:01:44.715 --> 03:01:47.973
to do that and focus it in the Mannheim area, which is where we're

03:01:48.054 --> 03:01:50.977
already seeing really serious water level declines. So by rescinding the policy,

03:01:51.090 --> 03:01:55.131
policy, it frees up the flexibility to spread

03:01:55.631 --> 03:01:58.980
out our pumping more evenly and not over-rely on pumping in one really

03:01:59.480 --> 03:02:03.438
focused area. Okay. I know other

03:02:03.470 --> 03:02:06.838
people have to ask questions, so I'll just say where I'm getting stuck is it

03:02:07.338 --> 03:02:10.783
doesn't make sense to me to draw water from an

03:02:10.832 --> 03:02:14.376
area that's struggling in order to support development that doesn't

03:02:14.876 --> 03:02:18.275
yet exist. But I'll leave it there. Thank you.

03:02:21.600 --> 03:02:24.893
I don't know, Jeff, if you want to let that liar respond

03:02:25.393 --> 03:02:29.391
to the fact an area that's struggling, that, that's a characterization

03:02:29.891 --> 03:02:33.133
of, of Wilmot. Councillor Huinink? Uh, well,

03:02:33.792 --> 03:02:37.181
at least Singletown. Okay, Jeff. Yeah,

03:02:37.261 --> 03:02:40.956
thanks for the opportunity to respond through the chair. Um, so the

03:02:41.456 --> 03:02:44.329
health of the aquifer in Wilmot Center water is, is excellent. We still have a

03:02:44.829 --> 03:02:47.685
lot of water available for drawdown in the municipal supply aquifer.

03:02:48.119 --> 03:02:51.781
So the aquifer is in good shape. It may be related to the individual

03:02:52.281 --> 03:02:56.615
characteristics of private wells. So it could be well maintenance, it could be mechanical

03:02:57.115 --> 03:03:00.100
issues, or it could be that private wells were only drilled into the very top

03:03:00.148 --> 03:03:03.360
of the aquifer. So through the council-endorsed policy and

03:03:03.860 --> 03:03:08.419
procedures for groundwater interference complaints, we have the ability to remedy those situations

03:03:08.919 --> 03:03:12.508
and mitigate it through lowering somebody's pump in their well, deepening somebody's

03:03:13.008 --> 03:03:16.821
private well, or outright replacing their private well to ensure that everybody in our

03:03:17.321 --> 03:03:20.556
community maintains access, reliable access to drinking water.

03:03:21.710 --> 03:03:24.451
Great. Councillor McCabe. Thanks very much.

03:03:24.756 --> 03:03:28.315
And through you, Chair, thanks for the presentation. It's been

03:03:28.379 --> 03:03:32.114
really clear and really helpful. So thank you very much for that. I just,

03:03:32.130 --> 03:03:35.465
you know, in having listened to various delegates talk about

03:03:35.514 --> 03:03:39.319
their concerns in their immediate area. I'm just—

03:03:39.447 --> 03:03:43.140
in some of the questions around data and the information in the staff

03:03:43.640 --> 03:03:46.850
report, and I think someone characterized it that you

03:03:46.994 --> 03:03:49.884
cherry-picked some data.

03:03:50.671 --> 03:03:54.236
I'm not saying that. I'm just repeating what some of the delegates

03:03:54.736 --> 03:03:58.683
said. I'm just wondering, like, are you open to

03:03:59.197 --> 03:04:02.617
conducting more field work that some of the residents mentioned

03:04:03.773 --> 03:04:07.787
to, you know, in a— because they showed some

03:04:08.287 --> 03:04:11.448
pictures from various points in time, and I'm just wondering

03:04:11.625 --> 03:04:15.189
if that's work that would kind of help them and better understand

03:04:15.238 --> 03:04:19.477
the information that you provided. Is that something that staff are open to

03:04:19.509 --> 03:04:22.351
doing? Through you, Chair, yes,

03:04:22.624 --> 03:04:25.819
absolutely. We would be open to accessing

03:04:26.397 --> 03:04:30.283
the wetland. It is located on private property, so we would obviously need

03:04:30.783 --> 03:04:34.345
agreement from the property owners to maintain regular access to the wetland

03:04:34.845 --> 03:04:38.600
on an ongoing basis. But yes, we would be happy to

03:04:38.680 --> 03:04:42.775
add instrumentation, region-owned instrumentation to that feature.

03:04:43.032 --> 03:04:46.211
Okay. Thanks very much. That's helpful. Councillor James.

03:04:47.271 --> 03:04:51.510
Yes, thank you through you, Chair, and thanks, Jeff, for your presentation and

03:04:51.686 --> 03:04:55.572
and the thoroughness of all of this. I just have a couple

03:04:56.072 --> 03:05:00.020
of questions. I guess the first is around

03:05:00.774 --> 03:05:04.435
the 1980 policy and your thoughts,

03:05:04.772 --> 03:05:09.268
you know, is it relevant

03:05:09.768 --> 03:05:13.009
to today, time and place? I just, when I looked at the notes,

03:05:13.057 --> 03:05:16.895
I looked at what I was seeing, and I'm seeing documents going back to 1978

03:05:17.072 --> 03:05:20.759
in 1980 and I look at where we are in advancement,

03:05:20.903 --> 03:05:24.911
is it relevant? So, and with this, I guess the caveat is because if

03:05:25.411 --> 03:05:29.175
you're called to do a review, does that make

03:05:29.675 --> 03:05:33.071
sense given how things have advanced? So just your thoughts on

03:05:33.087 --> 03:05:36.694
that with this 1980 policy. [Speaker] Yeah, that's a great

03:05:37.194 --> 03:05:41.102
question. Through you, Chair. The 1980 agreement was established

03:05:41.118 --> 03:05:44.309
under a very different regulatory regime. Regulatory framework in the province of

03:05:44.809 --> 03:05:48.781
Ontario. It was much less protective of sustainable water takings

03:05:48.974 --> 03:05:52.004
in aquifers. It was much less protective of the natural environment.

03:05:52.517 --> 03:05:56.172
So since the 1980 agreement was reached and the 1980— and

03:05:56.204 --> 03:05:59.763
subsequently amended in 1984, the province introduced

03:05:59.875 --> 03:06:03.274
much stronger, much more robust environmental

03:06:03.386 --> 03:06:06.592
protection framework within the province of Ontario through

03:06:07.092 --> 03:06:10.903
the creation of the Ontario Water Resources Act and the Water Taking and Transfer Regulation.

03:06:11.496 --> 03:06:15.102
So that really, you know, the 1980 policy was established during

03:06:15.602 --> 03:06:19.284
a time when there may not have been as much provincial oversight

03:06:19.669 --> 03:06:22.762
and sustainable water taking really wouldn't have been something that was,

03:06:23.034 --> 03:06:26.528
was top of mind for, for previous governments during

03:06:26.560 --> 03:06:30.165
those decades. So with the current legislative framework,

03:06:30.310 --> 03:06:34.107
the ministry controls water rights, they review, they approve,

03:06:34.557 --> 03:06:38.507
they assign water rights, water taking rights to private entities, to municipal

03:06:39.007 --> 03:06:42.328
entities, and it's a very robust framework. There's an enormous amount of

03:06:42.360 --> 03:06:46.550
technical work that goes into those approvals. So I would say the

03:06:47.050 --> 03:06:50.452
1980 policy, the purpose and intent of it

03:06:52.154 --> 03:06:54.948
is fulfilled through the legislative environment that we have today.

03:06:55.750 --> 03:06:59.186
Okay. So— Councillor James, another question? Yes. Just one follow-up question,

03:06:59.202 --> 03:07:02.269
and that's around the private wells? Because I think there's some

03:07:02.769 --> 03:07:06.267
confusion between, you know, a regional system well and

03:07:06.299 --> 03:07:09.382
a private well. Can you just, I mean, in very,

03:07:09.719 --> 03:07:11.534
you know, layman's terms here,

03:07:13.252 --> 03:07:16.078
what would be described as a private well? Am I going in my backyard,

03:07:16.094 --> 03:07:19.433
I'm digging it myself, does it have to meet a certain, you know,

03:07:19.514 --> 03:07:23.913
depth? And again, I think they're under the provincial

03:07:24.010 --> 03:07:27.542
regulation for these private wells. But I guess the other

03:07:28.042 --> 03:07:31.298
question, just to add on to that, Chair Redmond, is just, is there ability

03:07:31.330 --> 03:07:34.829
for those residents in these areas to attach to our

03:07:34.845 --> 03:07:38.553
well? Like, I guess there's a process there. And if you could—

03:07:39.053 --> 03:07:40.817
it sounded like a two-for-one question, Jeff.

03:07:43.032 --> 03:07:46.178
Thank you for the question. Through you, Chair. Yes, private wells are

03:07:46.242 --> 03:07:49.116
also under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of the Environment.

03:07:50.496 --> 03:07:54.492
Well construction is governed by Ontario Regulation 903. OHSRI, the Wells Regulation.

03:07:54.989 --> 03:07:58.501
They have to be completed by licensed well drillers.

03:07:59.030 --> 03:08:03.248
So it's quite a robust, stringent framework around construction

03:08:03.748 --> 03:08:08.187
requirements and standards for private wells. So private well would be anything

03:08:08.236 --> 03:08:12.405
that's, you know, owned by a private resident, a private corporation, not municipally

03:08:12.905 --> 03:08:16.655
owned. So that's kind of the differentiation there. In terms

03:08:17.155 --> 03:08:20.521
of private wells kind of connecting the system, they are completely isolated entities.

03:08:20.601 --> 03:08:24.344
So as the province receives permit to take water

03:08:24.424 --> 03:08:28.247
applications from either private or municipal entities, part of their,

03:08:28.520 --> 03:08:32.279
uh, course of their review is to look at potential impacts to private wells.

03:08:32.391 --> 03:08:36.166
So that's something that has to be thoroughly investigated prior to

03:08:36.666 --> 03:08:39.218
establishing a permit. The province has to feel comfortable with it,

03:08:40.343 --> 03:08:44.230
and then they require monitoring on our permits to make sure that all the information,

03:08:44.278 --> 03:08:48.132
the technical information, is still supported by monitoring to provide

03:08:48.632 --> 03:08:52.787
that additional layer of information in case something is different than what's identified

03:08:53.287 --> 03:08:57.251
during short-term testing. Councillor Salonen.

03:08:57.925 --> 03:09:01.762
Thank you. Through you, Chair, thank you very much, both Jeff and to

03:09:02.262 --> 03:09:05.872
your team and staff for this report. I have multiple

03:09:06.372 --> 03:09:10.304
questions. I guess the first one I will start with on page 2

03:09:10.384 --> 03:09:13.178
of the report or page 4 43 of the agenda.

03:09:14.221 --> 03:09:17.962
It identifies that Wilmot's current population as of 2025 is

03:09:17.995 --> 03:09:20.612
15,755 people.

03:09:21.350 --> 03:09:25.140
I cannot find that number anywhere online. Stats Canada

03:09:25.640 --> 03:09:30.021
2021 census is 21,429 people. The township

03:09:30.521 --> 03:09:33.152
of Wilmot website says it's 22,500 people.

03:09:33.634 --> 03:09:37.448
And the most recent regional report I could find, which was a report forward

03:09:37.948 --> 03:09:42.677
in 2025, PDL GDS 25-005,

03:09:43.030 --> 03:09:46.261
actually said it was 23,140

03:09:46.277 --> 03:09:49.379
people. So the entire assumptions that

03:09:49.879 --> 03:09:53.647
you're basing Wilmot's growth on, I have concerns. It's not

03:09:53.792 --> 03:09:57.038
the 73% growth that you're talking about. Also,

03:09:57.070 --> 03:10:00.958
this report is silent on the real constraints

03:10:01.458 --> 03:10:05.297
in Wilmot, which is our wastewater capacity is capped at about 10,000

03:10:05.797 --> 03:10:09.135
new people. So that's more so looking in the

03:10:09.635 --> 03:10:13.069
40% range of growth. Curious if you can speak

03:10:13.569 --> 03:10:16.954
to where your population size comes from and all

03:10:17.083 --> 03:10:20.358
of those underlying assumptions. Through the

03:10:20.858 --> 03:10:22.944
Chair, I think I'll direct this question to Commissioner Regier.

03:10:26.781 --> 03:10:30.684
Over to you, Rod. Hello, thank you for the question.

03:10:32.823 --> 03:10:39.354
The numbers that I have for Baden and New Hamburg are 5,965

03:10:40.035 --> 03:10:45.416
for Baden in 2025 and 9,790

03:10:45.465 --> 03:10:48.674
for New Hamburg in 2025 for

03:10:49.174 --> 03:10:53.114
total population of 15,755

03:10:53.614 --> 03:10:56.061
in, uh, last year.

03:10:58.619 --> 03:11:02.099
I also, so I, the information

03:11:02.599 --> 03:11:06.779
that I have is that we have room for growth of

03:11:07.459 --> 03:11:09.515
population.

03:11:10.600 --> 03:11:13.553
Hang on a second. Just interpreting the numbers here.

03:11:17.346 --> 03:11:19.897
Population growth to 34,900.

03:11:22.415 --> 03:11:25.690
Across Wilmot in for a 66% growth rate.

03:11:25.786 --> 03:11:29.382
So it's slightly different than what Jeff has got

03:11:29.882 --> 03:11:33.652
here, but I think it aligns with the

03:11:34.037 --> 03:11:38.291
understanding that very significant levels of growth are expected and

03:11:38.791 --> 03:11:42.271
are planned for within the existing allocation of water for

03:11:42.480 --> 03:11:45.289
both Baden and New Hamburg. Rod,

03:11:45.931 --> 03:11:49.029
what about the water and the wastewater? That was the other sort of— Yes.

03:11:49.222 --> 03:11:52.734
Natasha's doing two-parters too. Yeah, no, that's a

03:11:53.234 --> 03:11:58.026
legitimate point. The NIF has limited

03:11:58.526 --> 03:12:03.334
assimilative capacity, and that figure

03:12:04.521 --> 03:12:08.754
is about 10,000 population growth between now and 2051.

03:12:09.268 --> 03:12:12.972
So I guess the calculation that we have is that the amount of

03:12:13.036 --> 03:12:17.529
water available actually exceeds the

03:12:17.770 --> 03:12:21.383
amount that the NIF can receive in terms of

03:12:21.883 --> 03:12:25.413
wastewater. Councillor Saladin, do you have a second question? Yes,

03:12:25.542 --> 03:12:27.549
I do, and I'll be queuing back up after.

03:12:29.074 --> 03:12:32.863
In reviewing this report, I also actually found myself

03:12:33.363 --> 03:12:37.520
far more confused as to why staff

03:12:38.020 --> 03:12:41.853
are even asking for the rescinding of the 1980 policy. I appreciate

03:12:42.559 --> 03:12:46.326
Councillor Hunnicke asked for clarification. I'm still not satisfied with

03:12:46.826 --> 03:12:50.526
that answer on the premise that your justification provided

03:12:51.472 --> 03:12:55.447
is that you didn't violate with the increase of the 2019

03:12:55.704 --> 03:12:57.371
pumping, you didn't violate the policy.

03:12:58.926 --> 03:13:02.757
Which is fine. I'll accept that assumption based off of the statistics

03:13:03.257 --> 03:13:06.877
you gave on page 4 and 5 of the report.

03:13:06.990 --> 03:13:10.917
Report, but then it also identifies

03:13:10.949 --> 03:13:14.315
that there's 77 extra liters available.

03:13:14.540 --> 03:13:17.874
I get that in your presentation you noted you're not suggesting that we pump all

03:13:17.938 --> 03:13:21.865
of that, but I'm really confused why you suddenly

03:13:22.365 --> 03:13:25.985
need the rescinding of a policy to do 30

03:13:26.097 --> 03:13:30.426
extra liters per second when there's 77 available

03:13:30.926 --> 03:13:34.611
in your justification for not having violated the policy itself?

03:13:35.237 --> 03:13:37.614
Jeff, I don't know if you want to call a friend, but one is coming

03:13:37.646 --> 03:13:40.054
in the wings. I'm going to phone a friend. Thank you.

03:13:44.952 --> 03:13:48.693
Through the chair, that's a really good question. And I think one of the reasons

03:13:48.709 --> 03:13:52.177
why we've talked about it at a staffing level is that the way that the

03:13:52.257 --> 03:13:55.436
policy was written, it can create some confusion, right?

03:13:55.709 --> 03:13:58.969
There's the total amount of water taken from all the well fields combined.

03:13:59.306 --> 03:14:03.251
And there's the individual amounts for each of the well fields. And so the

03:14:03.556 --> 03:14:07.324
motion brought forward in terms of rescinding the policy was brought forward in an effort

03:14:07.824 --> 03:14:11.301
of transparency and to ensure that everyone was aware of the path moving

03:14:11.349 --> 03:14:15.246
forward versus possible misinterpretations. And again, it is aligned

03:14:15.278 --> 03:14:19.399
with the overall approach for the water supply strategy update in terms of looking

03:14:19.448 --> 03:14:23.264
for new water supply sources throughout Waterloo Region, including those

03:14:23.360 --> 03:14:27.226
outside of the geographic boundary. Of the integrated urban system.

03:14:29.713 --> 03:14:33.276
Councillor Gowing. Uh, thanks, Chair.

03:14:35.956 --> 03:14:38.651
I'm a little confused as well with, with that,

03:14:40.063 --> 03:14:44.701
in the way that if we are looking— if the agreement isn't

03:14:45.985 --> 03:14:49.290
clarified, or, or it could be interpreted in another way,

03:14:50.076 --> 03:14:53.270
uh, and I, and I respect the fact that you said that

03:14:53.446 --> 03:14:57.380
it was communicated to Wilmont itself,

03:14:58.263 --> 03:15:01.892
why don't we work on a new agreement instead of rescinding the

03:15:09.680 --> 03:15:13.533
Through the chair, there are no other areas where we have a similar

03:15:14.033 --> 03:15:17.756
agreement or policy in place, but there are many examples of where we're currently transferring

03:15:17.852 --> 03:15:21.447
water across municipal boundaries. So a really good example of

03:15:21.479 --> 03:15:25.441
that would be Woolwich, where we have water that's transferred from

03:15:25.473 --> 03:15:29.484
the integrated urban system through connections from the City of Waterloo and

03:15:29.660 --> 03:15:33.077
also through the City of Kitchener, and we don't have these types of agreements in

03:15:33.254 --> 03:15:36.687
place. One of the benefits of having it

03:15:36.719 --> 03:15:40.858
set up where you're not just setting agreements through municipal boundaries is

03:15:40.954 --> 03:15:44.564
that you can manage the water based on the monitoring for the aquifer,

03:15:44.820 --> 03:15:48.624
based on sustainable practices. It allows us to adapt

03:15:48.672 --> 03:15:52.331
and be flexible in our operation, to look at things holistically

03:15:52.845 --> 03:15:55.301
to best meet the needs of the residents throughout Waterloo Region.

03:15:56.954 --> 03:16:00.710
Okay, and then just— Councillor Cowing, second question. Going to go to my questions now.

03:16:00.790 --> 03:16:04.401
That was one that just came to me. Um, in the report,

03:16:04.626 --> 03:16:08.109
uh, it tells us that, uh, several 20— uh,

03:16:08.157 --> 03:16:11.511
2026 well interference complaints were still

03:16:11.687 --> 03:16:15.746
being addressed. Given that, can you explain how

03:16:16.901 --> 03:16:20.575
you're able to conclude that the report interference— excuse me,

03:16:20.591 --> 03:16:23.526
the reported interferences are not related to pumping?

03:16:24.617 --> 03:16:27.649
Has the full engineering and monitoring analysis been

03:16:27.729 --> 03:16:31.291
completed for all of those complaints, or is some of

03:16:31.791 --> 03:16:34.772
the analysis still outstanding? Thank you for the question.

03:16:35.205 --> 03:16:37.579
Through the Chair, so the analysis is still ongoing.

03:16:38.333 --> 03:16:41.975
Initial complaints were received, and following

03:16:42.023 --> 03:16:45.489
the procedure, we are now in an appeal process with a few

03:16:45.989 --> 03:16:49.355
of them. I don't want to speak too much to the

03:16:49.564 --> 03:16:53.703
individual specifics, specifics of each one to maintain privacy and confidentiality

03:16:54.457 --> 03:16:58.276
for each individual case. So as

03:16:58.372 --> 03:17:02.768
I mentioned, well interference complaints can be received for any variety of reasons that

03:17:02.784 --> 03:17:06.954
could be related to mechanical failures, they could be related to lack

03:17:07.454 --> 03:17:10.771
of rehabilitation or maintenance over the years, they could be related to municipal pumping,

03:17:10.883 --> 03:17:14.379
they could be related to any number of things. So we, we do address

03:17:14.411 --> 03:17:18.902
it systematically through the policy and procedure for groundwater interference

03:17:19.402 --> 03:17:23.328
complaints. So I don't believe that the report specifically

03:17:23.828 --> 03:17:27.305
states that private wells are not being impacted by municipal pumping

03:17:28.139 --> 03:17:31.510
because each one needs to be individually assessed based on its

03:17:32.010 --> 03:17:35.621
own merit, location, depth of the well, all— there's

03:17:36.121 --> 03:17:39.683
many different factors that go into individual assessments. And due to the sensitive

03:17:39.699 --> 03:17:43.135
nature with it being private, private infrastructure as well,

03:17:43.151 --> 03:17:46.507
that's not information that's really, you know,

03:17:46.523 --> 03:17:48.851
appropriate to be presented in a public-facing report.

03:17:50.312 --> 03:17:53.636
Not sure if that totally answered the question. Okay. Councillor Foxton.

03:17:56.782 --> 03:17:58.307
Thank you, Chair.

03:18:01.131 --> 03:18:04.581
Good information, but from listening to my

03:18:05.081 --> 03:18:07.679
colleagues, there's a lot of questions still going around.

03:18:08.497 --> 03:18:11.835
I agreed with what Councillor Gowing was

03:18:12.335 --> 03:18:15.751
stating about rather than rescinding, revising the motion.

03:18:16.441 --> 03:18:19.908
I have concerns still about the

03:18:20.408 --> 03:18:24.044
drying of the wells. Because water is water. Water goes

03:18:24.076 --> 03:18:28.130
through the system as we aquifer. And it also makes

03:18:28.194 --> 03:18:31.832
sense for wells. So I'm sure there's some type of connection

03:18:32.332 --> 03:18:36.095
there. Not 100% positive because I'm not a water expert.

03:18:36.559 --> 03:18:40.165
Also, you said that Shingletown was out of the— beyond

03:18:40.665 --> 03:18:43.883
the zone, so they're not collecting from the water for now. They're on municipal water.

03:18:44.828 --> 03:18:48.582
So that's an issue to My biggest concern

03:18:49.082 --> 03:18:54.202
is there's a problem. I can't tell you what it is for

03:18:54.218 --> 03:18:56.305
the wetlands to dry up and everything else.

03:18:58.055 --> 03:19:01.604
You showed your seasons of droughts, but there's also

03:19:02.104 --> 03:19:05.827
been seasons of plenty of rain and snow. The last couple years

03:19:05.859 --> 03:19:09.937
of snow and rain have been phenomenal, which you think would replenish

03:19:10.437 --> 03:19:13.761
our water. I just have real

03:19:14.261 --> 03:19:17.918
concerns about where we're going with this. I also wonder if

03:19:17.983 --> 03:19:22.030
we can pull in somebody from the GRCA to deal with the wetlands issues

03:19:22.046 --> 03:19:25.363
and what's going on there. Maybe resources that we have that we

03:19:25.863 --> 03:19:28.713
don't have to hire, that we can just ask them to be involved to look

03:19:29.213 --> 03:19:32.736
into these things. Because there is an issue, I think. There really is an issue

03:19:33.236 --> 03:19:35.923
in Roma, and I think we need to resolve it. I don't think I have

03:19:36.020 --> 03:19:39.642
clarity yet on that, and that's my concern. Um,

03:19:40.934 --> 03:19:44.874
so I'd like to hear what

03:19:45.374 --> 03:19:49.129
we plan to do about Wilma. Jeff, do you want,

03:19:49.145 --> 03:19:52.135
do you want to weigh in? Uh,

03:19:52.442 --> 03:19:56.240
through the chair, so there's a lot there, so my apologies

03:19:56.740 --> 03:19:59.859
if I don't, uh, fully address, um, all of the comments there.

03:20:00.478 --> 03:20:03.650
So we're regularly engaged with the GRCA,

03:20:04.040 --> 03:20:07.416
um, We're happy to involve them in discussions related to the

03:20:07.916 --> 03:20:11.446
Shingletown Wetland. I know there's a lot of concerns about that, and,

03:20:13.116 --> 03:20:16.953
you know, we had a good snowpack this year, we've had a good wet spring,

03:20:17.483 --> 03:20:21.224
so hopefully that contributes to seeing water levels rebound

03:20:22.027 --> 03:20:25.671
in that wetland. When you go through significant drought periods,

03:20:26.217 --> 03:20:30.360
one good precipitation year might not fully rebound

03:20:30.376 --> 03:20:34.047
water levels in a natural surface water feature that's very sensitive

03:20:34.159 --> 03:20:37.589
to precipitation patterns. You could see after an extended drought

03:20:38.089 --> 03:20:41.469
period, it may take several years for water levels to recover naturally based

03:20:42.719 --> 03:20:46.871
on longer periods of time with average or above average precipitation.

03:20:47.000 --> 03:20:50.286
So we've seen that demonstrated in the water levels through

03:20:50.786 --> 03:20:54.582
our monitoring wells, where despite increasing pumping, we actually see rising

03:20:54.598 --> 03:20:58.658
levels. So we know that the these longer-term precipitation

03:20:58.690 --> 03:21:02.063
and recharge periods following drought periods

03:21:02.207 --> 03:21:05.676
allow water levels to recover in aquifers, can allow water levels to recover

03:21:06.176 --> 03:21:10.349
in natural features. So you may

03:21:10.849 --> 03:21:13.769
not see it over a short term. It could be a longer-term natural response that

03:21:14.269 --> 03:21:18.651
you see in the environment. Now, with respect to the

03:21:18.667 --> 03:21:21.814
wetland itself, again, we're happy

03:21:22.314 --> 03:21:24.945
to get in there and add our own monitoring network There is a good data

03:21:25.445 --> 03:21:29.549
set there that dates back to 2017, but we're happy to add more

03:21:29.678 --> 03:21:33.528
data and monitoring locations in that area. As we learn

03:21:34.028 --> 03:21:37.924
new things, we make adjustments. That's part of the adaptive management approach that staff

03:21:38.424 --> 03:21:42.416
take to ensure that we're following the Ontario Water Resources Act, which speaks to sustainable

03:21:42.916 --> 03:21:46.540
water taking, and it's what, you know, it's one

03:21:47.040 --> 03:21:51.289
of the principles that's important to our community and that we use

03:21:51.789 --> 03:21:54.465
that we hold ourselves to. Councillor Foxton, do you have a second question? Yeah,

03:21:54.609 --> 03:21:57.208
one quick thing is, so the motion actually, if I,

03:21:57.416 --> 03:22:01.170
if I read it right, said not to revoke the 1980

03:22:02.389 --> 03:22:04.010
agreement. Is that correct?

03:22:06.400 --> 03:22:09.993
Uh, through the chair, so I believe the motion recommends the temporary suspension

03:22:10.571 --> 03:22:14.309
of the 1980 agreement, right? Not a revoking of it. Yes,

03:22:14.437 --> 03:22:17.695
and not, and not the full revoking So staff recommendation

03:22:17.871 --> 03:22:22.046
is the permanent rescindment, not a temporary rescindment, the permanent rescindment of

03:22:22.546 --> 03:22:24.840
the 1980 agreement. And part of it is,

03:22:25.819 --> 03:22:29.383
you know, while the region maintains that we have not violated

03:22:29.883 --> 03:22:32.337
the totals under the individual— or under the agreement,

03:22:32.819 --> 03:22:36.400
we've been challenged on that. So it's definitely creating an element of confusion.

03:22:37.058 --> 03:22:41.088
And by rescinding the policy, it establishes very clearly what

03:22:41.184 --> 03:22:44.508
the regulatory framework is that falls under the provincial

03:22:44.540 --> 03:22:48.426
jurisdiction. Right now, I think without a full permanent rescindment,

03:22:48.474 --> 03:22:52.006
because of that uncertainty, we couldn't confidently say that

03:22:52.506 --> 03:22:55.378
we will always be able to permanently transfer that 30 litres per second of water.

03:22:55.860 --> 03:22:59.376
So without that confidence that it's a permanent additional source of water to the

03:22:59.876 --> 03:23:03.647
Mannheim service area, we can't trim our deficit and

03:23:04.193 --> 03:23:07.067
use that for, for operational resiliency or growth.

03:23:08.303 --> 03:23:11.783
And what, with your indulgence, Chair? Well,

03:23:12.232 --> 03:23:14.637
uh, we have other people that are going to have to leave by 12:30,

03:23:14.653 --> 03:23:17.748
so if you're really quick— Okay, really, really quick.

03:23:17.845 --> 03:23:20.940
Is this a legal document and do you need approval from Wilmot to rescind?

03:23:23.153 --> 03:23:27.050
Sorry, I did not quite hear that question. Does the region require

03:23:27.550 --> 03:23:29.760
Wilmot to agree to rescinding it?

03:23:31.797 --> 03:23:35.938
Does the region require Wilmot to agree To rescind the 1980

03:23:35.970 --> 03:23:39.841
agreement. Uh, I refer

03:23:40.002 --> 03:23:42.042
to— Fiona is going to weigh in.

03:23:44.886 --> 03:23:48.339
Thank you. And through you, Chair, uh, it is a regional

03:23:48.839 --> 03:23:52.484
policy that is before you to be rescinded. It is—

03:23:52.757 --> 03:23:56.050
there's no, uh, contractual arrangement

03:23:56.550 --> 03:23:59.793
or agreement. Thank you. Um, I'm going

03:23:59.809 --> 03:24:03.291
to go on to Councillor Brivanovic. Thank you

03:24:03.791 --> 03:24:07.938
very much, Chair Redman, and thank you to staff for

03:24:09.043 --> 03:24:13.691
the presentations and lots of good questions being asked. I guess

03:24:14.191 --> 03:24:17.232
I want to dig a little deeper into this issue

03:24:17.457 --> 03:24:20.918
of if staff are saying that

03:24:22.104 --> 03:24:24.780
there were no issues with taking the water,

03:24:25.693 --> 03:24:28.516
the region was well within within their rights to do it,

03:24:29.560 --> 03:24:33.253
notwithstanding the agreement and, you know, how some people may

03:24:33.753 --> 03:24:35.597
be interpreting it.

03:24:37.187 --> 03:24:40.736
I'm struggling to understand how continuing that

03:24:41.121 --> 03:24:44.830
methodology wouldn't allow us to

03:24:45.681 --> 03:24:49.310
add that 30 liters per second back into

03:24:49.374 --> 03:24:53.003
the process and in, you know, towards development

03:24:53.503 --> 03:24:56.744
or resiliency or some combination thereof,

03:24:59.265 --> 03:25:03.423
recognizing that, you know, in the future there

03:25:03.923 --> 03:25:07.550
would, A, be a more in-depth report that looks at the whole picture,

03:25:08.240 --> 03:25:12.801
and B, there would presumably be other

03:25:12.993 --> 03:25:16.510
sources that add to the capacity. So if that

03:25:17.010 --> 03:25:20.827
was ever revisited in the future, future, uh, it— those

03:25:20.924 --> 03:25:24.936
other sources could deal with the 30, but this would, at least on

03:25:24.968 --> 03:25:28.161
an interim basis, allow us to proceed with

03:25:28.661 --> 03:25:33.296
that 30. I'm just trying to understand what, what the, the logic,

03:25:33.762 --> 03:25:37.276
uh, is, uh, behind that, or the thinking.

03:25:37.469 --> 03:25:40.325
Jeff, do you want to speak to the permanency versus the, um,

03:25:41.160 --> 03:25:44.385
temporary capacity? So the, the current,

03:25:44.450 --> 03:25:48.865
um, system capacity in the Mannheim service area, I believe 1,455

03:25:49.365 --> 03:25:53.072
litres per second, does not include this 30 litres per second from Wilmot

03:25:53.120 --> 03:25:57.022
Centre. So with the rescindment of this agreement, we can add

03:25:57.150 --> 03:26:01.598
30 litres per second to that total. So that would bring it up to 1,485

03:26:01.774 --> 03:26:05.098
litres per second permanently. Without the rescindment,

03:26:05.628 --> 03:26:08.775
we can't add it to that total. So we can't trim the

03:26:09.275 --> 03:26:12.786
deficit that we currently have in the Mannheim time service area because we can't guarantee

03:26:13.286 --> 03:26:17.066
that that supply of water will be there permanently. As far

03:26:17.194 --> 03:26:21.009
as the amount of study that's completed, I do want to speak to that because

03:26:21.042 --> 03:26:24.616
Wilmot Centre, probably our most extensively

03:26:24.664 --> 03:26:28.704
studied well field in the entire region of Waterloo, an extensive

03:26:28.736 --> 03:26:32.103
monitoring network, an enormous amount of technical work. So I would actually like

03:26:32.603 --> 03:26:36.030
to call upon Craig Johnston from SLR, who's been a consultant

03:26:36.530 --> 03:26:39.899
on a lot of projects dating back to the 1990s in Wilmot Township to

03:26:40.399 --> 03:26:43.945
maybe provide a little bit more information based on

03:26:44.041 --> 03:26:47.124
his extensive career profile. Thanks. Welcome, Craig.

03:26:47.285 --> 03:26:51.026
Sorry, but before you pass the mic to him, can you, can you just— like,

03:26:51.267 --> 03:26:54.494
I didn't hear an answer about why we can't add that 30

03:26:54.994 --> 03:26:58.011
in, because if we've been taking it anyway and

03:26:58.511 --> 03:27:01.977
we're saying we were totally within our rights to do that, um,

03:27:03.245 --> 03:27:07.147
you know, what is precluding us from continuing

03:27:07.323 --> 03:27:11.081
to take it? And if there's a future decision

03:27:11.113 --> 03:27:15.416
that impacts that by a future council,

03:27:16.155 --> 03:27:18.916
it would obviously be done in the context of, you know,

03:27:18.964 --> 03:27:22.144
additional water supply being

03:27:22.256 --> 03:27:26.061
considered. I mean, this is not an overall problem that we're going

03:27:26.142 --> 03:27:29.511
to fix in the next 2 months. Months. This is going to

03:27:30.011 --> 03:27:33.326
be a multi-year solution that deals with both infrastructure and

03:27:33.826 --> 03:27:38.023
capacity. Through the chair, so the 1980 agreement

03:27:38.136 --> 03:27:41.550
as it is casts doubt on the permanent nature of that,

03:27:41.663 --> 03:27:44.692
our ability to transfer that water. So until we can confirm

03:27:44.885 --> 03:27:47.626
that this is a permanent transfer of water that can be allowed from the well

03:27:48.126 --> 03:27:50.993
field, we can't allocate it towards the,

03:27:51.281 --> 03:27:56.441
the Mannheim service area, and we can't add it to that 1,455

03:27:56.635 --> 03:28:00.615
litres per second. So it's the uncertainty that

03:28:00.761 --> 03:28:04.309
the policy casts over the entire— over

03:28:04.809 --> 03:28:08.647
the permanency of that water taking that causes us to not be able to include

03:28:09.147 --> 03:28:12.667
that as part of, you know, the firm capacity in the Mannheim service

03:28:13.167 --> 03:28:16.102
area. I hope that answered that question.

03:28:16.876 --> 03:28:19.391
So who— I guess who's telling us that we can't, like,

03:28:19.762 --> 03:28:23.103
What's the policy or legal

03:28:23.794 --> 03:28:25.978
framework or background that's telling us that?

03:28:29.335 --> 03:28:32.773
So, in terms of legal framework, I may

03:28:33.273 --> 03:28:37.832
need to call on Fiona to discuss

03:28:38.332 --> 03:28:41.977
the legal framework of it being a regional policy versus

03:28:42.475 --> 03:28:46.185
the legislative framework that's currently in existence. Assistance from the province.

03:28:56.365 --> 03:29:00.234
The policy creates a certain limit of what can

03:29:00.734 --> 03:29:04.136
be taken. So if— and

03:29:05.677 --> 03:29:08.664
if you need more than that at any point in time, that would limit the

03:29:09.164 --> 03:29:09.627
ability to do that.

03:29:12.098 --> 03:29:16.122
And I'd like to pass it to Craig Johnson to

03:29:16.622 --> 03:29:19.825
just provide his technical overview as well. Welcome, Craig.

03:29:21.989 --> 03:29:25.244
Thank you. And through the chair, I guess there's a few things that I've

03:29:25.744 --> 03:29:29.861
heard today. I started working on that well field in 1994,

03:29:30.149 --> 03:29:33.965
'95 as part of the original Baid New Humber work and was

03:29:34.301 --> 03:29:38.277
one of the founding members with the Wilbutte

03:29:38.777 --> 03:29:41.473
Centre Advisory Committee to set up the monitoring that was done that Mr. Ikern spoke

03:29:41.973 --> 03:29:45.437
about. So I've had the luxury of looking at this well field and just

03:29:45.550 --> 03:29:48.599
recently coming back after over 30 years and looking at long-term

03:29:49.099 --> 03:29:52.371
data, which is often not a time you get

03:29:52.871 --> 03:29:55.227
in your career to go back that far in history and look at some of

03:29:55.244 --> 03:29:59.256
the original work. So I think one of the things for me that

03:29:59.756 --> 03:30:02.851
is astounding to some degree about this well field is

03:30:03.750 --> 03:30:05.982
after 53 years of pumping,

03:30:07.138 --> 03:30:10.558
we still see periods of higher precipitation in the region

03:30:10.590 --> 03:30:14.332
when your water levels in that aquifer come back to pre-pumping levels.

03:30:14.845 --> 03:30:19.406
So back into the late '60s. So we reach a return to original

03:30:19.906 --> 03:30:22.842
levels. And obviously right around the well field, as Jeff said,

03:30:23.339 --> 03:30:26.776
you get about 2 to 2.5 meters of drawdown

03:30:27.113 --> 03:30:30.705
water level change within that municipal aquifer, so 8 feet roughly.

03:30:31.699 --> 03:30:35.226
And as you get out, that gets less. So a lot of the work that's

03:30:35.726 --> 03:30:39.234
been done has been looking at where does that effect happen, and we have

03:30:39.734 --> 03:30:42.600
a very good understanding of that and very long-term data to look at it.

03:30:43.225 --> 03:30:46.448
The other thing that makes this well field more challenging, and it probably

03:30:46.948 --> 03:30:49.734
ties back to some of the private well interference complaints,

03:30:50.600 --> 03:30:55.107
is that when you think about pumping is only, call it, 2 meters We

03:30:55.607 --> 03:30:58.960
have these long-term drought scenarios, and they're defined where you have 3 to

03:30:59.460 --> 03:31:03.456
4 years of below-average precipitation. And there's been 5 since

03:31:03.956 --> 03:31:07.277
1970 that occurred, the most recent in 2020

03:31:07.550 --> 03:31:11.002
to 2023. Those long-term drought

03:31:11.502 --> 03:31:15.097
periods can result in a meter to 2 meters of additional lowering of

03:31:15.597 --> 03:31:19.287
water levels due to climate. So when you get away from the well field,

03:31:19.320 --> 03:31:23.045
where responses are either very, very small or

03:31:23.545 --> 03:31:27.540
not at all, you could have a 2-meter, 1- to 2-meter change

03:31:28.040 --> 03:31:31.313
in water levels just purely based on that long-term climate effect,

03:31:31.378 --> 03:31:36.034
which we just came through in 2020 to 2023.

03:31:36.853 --> 03:31:40.048
So I think that's one of the complicating factors. You know,

03:31:40.064 --> 03:31:43.259
back to the private wells, we have been asked

03:31:43.759 --> 03:31:48.109
to look at 6 of those. From the Region. All 5

03:31:48.609 --> 03:31:51.562
of those wells are outside of any area of influence of pumping,

03:31:52.027 --> 03:31:55.625
so our conclusion is that it is not related to municipal

03:31:56.125 --> 03:32:00.170
pumping. One of those wells was in the area, and the question on

03:32:00.202 --> 03:32:03.896
that one was more, I use a dug well because I like the water

03:32:04.396 --> 03:32:07.686
quality from the dug well, I have a drilled well, if I used it,

03:32:07.750 --> 03:32:10.304
would it be within the influence of regional pumping?

03:32:10.801 --> 03:32:14.182
And in that case, the answer would be yes, but it's still certainly

03:32:14.697 --> 03:32:17.980
completed deep within the aquifer and ability to take

03:32:18.480 --> 03:32:21.620
that water. So from— Okay, thank you. Sorry.

03:32:24.552 --> 03:32:28.096
Go ahead. No, no, please finish. I thought you were done. Sorry. I think that's

03:32:28.596 --> 03:32:31.913
really it. So certainly from my perspective, this aquifer has been pumped

03:32:32.413 --> 03:32:35.961
sustainably for a long period of time. We have excellent data

03:32:36.461 --> 03:32:38.194
to support the 150 8 liters per second.

03:32:41.245 --> 03:32:44.071
Do you have a second question, Councillor Vrbanovic? Yeah, well, no,

03:32:44.440 --> 03:32:47.828
I think I'll just say, you know, thanks to everyone for the

03:32:48.328 --> 03:32:52.292
answers. I'm struggling a little bit because

03:32:52.388 --> 03:32:56.065
I do think that, you know, we've been taking this 30 liters,

03:32:56.434 --> 03:32:58.506
staff have determined that it's okay.

03:32:59.132 --> 03:33:03.323
I'm struggling with why we

03:33:03.403 --> 03:33:07.305
can't, short of rescinding the agreement, which has

03:33:07.337 --> 03:33:11.592
some protections, I'm struggling with why we can't have some wording that

03:33:12.092 --> 03:33:15.012
allows us to continue doing that and add it back to the capacity.

03:33:15.526 --> 03:33:19.267
And so I guess the question to that would be, to legal,

03:33:19.395 --> 03:33:22.639
is there any alternative wording short of rescinding

03:33:22.992 --> 03:33:26.155
that would allow us to do

03:33:26.187 --> 03:33:29.752
that, recognizing that that

03:33:29.880 --> 03:33:33.108
we were within our rights to do what we have done

03:33:33.608 --> 03:33:36.865
thus far from what has been reported. And then

03:33:37.365 --> 03:33:40.622
the last point I'll just make, Madam Chair, and I know these are difficult,

03:33:40.638 --> 03:33:44.107
complex issues. This is, I guess, the second special

03:33:44.607 --> 03:33:48.297
meeting that's going to be running significantly later,

03:33:48.442 --> 03:33:52.424
and I think what we need to do is probably have a discussion amongst council

03:33:52.600 --> 03:33:55.868
to, maybe this needs to be a

03:33:55.885 --> 03:33:59.824
9:00 to 2:00 or 9:00 to 3:00 day on this issue 'cause it's always

03:33:59.921 --> 03:34:03.872
easier to get time back than it is to add on when

03:34:04.386 --> 03:34:08.033
even like adding on, we're adding on extra time beyond the end time,

03:34:08.643 --> 03:34:12.387
but we all have other commitments that we're trying to balance as well. Thank you.

03:34:12.887 --> 03:34:15.515
[Speaker] I think it's an indication of how many people from the community want to

03:34:15.580 --> 03:34:19.466
come and delegate, so we'll take that under advisement and maybe

03:34:20.697 --> 03:34:24.175
mark off bigger chunks for these special council meetings, but I do think

03:34:24.575 --> 03:34:28.421
it's very important that we hear from everybody. And I understand, Councillor Vrbanovic,

03:34:28.469 --> 03:34:32.091
you're not suggesting that we don't hear from any— everybody. Craig,

03:34:32.155 --> 03:34:35.536
I just want to ask, um, and this is

03:34:36.036 --> 03:34:38.405
a little probably— and we've got two more speakers and then we're going to move

03:34:38.905 --> 03:34:42.074
on to the motion, but what Fiona said to us is

03:34:42.090 --> 03:34:45.199
this is not a legal undertaking, this is a policy.

03:34:45.872 --> 03:34:49.157
And I get that. And I also get that this, I think, is a very

03:34:49.221 --> 03:34:53.163
unique policy vis-à-vis the water system throughout the Region

03:34:53.663 --> 03:34:57.314
of Waterloo. So I guess I'm just— and maybe it's a Jeff

03:34:57.330 --> 03:35:01.144
question or you, but have you worked on other areas of our aquifer

03:35:01.480 --> 03:35:04.861
and our water system in the Region of Waterloo? It's interesting that

03:35:05.361 --> 03:35:09.140
this seems to be an anomaly. Yeah, I've worked

03:35:09.588 --> 03:35:13.596
throughout pretty much all of your well fields. Right into Cambridge, into some

03:35:13.612 --> 03:35:16.867
of the other townships. It is definitely unique. And if I go

03:35:17.367 --> 03:35:21.374
back to looking at some of the original sort of documentation

03:35:21.874 --> 03:35:25.110
around it, so the Wilmot Centre well field, when it started

03:35:25.142 --> 03:35:27.949
pumping in the late '60s,

03:35:28.286 --> 03:35:31.878
early '70s, there was an immediate effect that was

03:35:32.378 --> 03:35:36.176
observed. And that really resulted from drilled wells

03:35:36.929 --> 03:35:39.847
that were in the municipality Lakewi'fer that were artesian at the time,

03:35:40.840 --> 03:35:44.141
and they overflowed into a ditch and then into Huntsburger Creek.

03:35:44.862 --> 03:35:48.147
And so one of the things that happened in that situation is as soon

03:35:48.647 --> 03:35:51.368
as you pumped the water levels of the wells, the water levels dropped, the flow

03:35:51.868 --> 03:35:55.246
stopped, and that was partially tying back to the augmentation of flow in

03:35:55.342 --> 03:35:58.178
Huntsburger Creek and, and to some degree with Mr.

03:35:58.627 --> 03:36:02.441
Ankuren's pond. There was also obviously impacts

03:36:02.569 --> 03:36:06.082
for private wells right in the Shingletown area where we knew water

03:36:06.582 --> 03:36:10.321
levels would drop a few meters. And obviously that caused some of the concerns

03:36:10.369 --> 03:36:13.886
at the time. So if you really look at the agreement

03:36:14.386 --> 03:36:18.301
and try to find science or logic behind why

03:36:18.801 --> 03:36:22.123
those numbers were set, I wouldn't say professionally there are any

03:36:22.219 --> 03:36:25.848
sort of clear logic or science there. It was done to address concerns

03:36:26.348 --> 03:36:29.557
at the time, and the best data you had was 1979.

03:36:30.183 --> 03:36:32.512
To sort of put in place at that time.

03:36:33.491 --> 03:36:37.618
Since that time, obviously, the Region's been monitoring extensively and

03:36:37.714 --> 03:36:41.022
built up an extensive knowledge of the systems and how they behave.

03:36:41.921 --> 03:36:45.453
So I do personally think, you know, it was done

03:36:45.517 --> 03:36:48.889
for a purpose at the time, and that purpose may

03:36:49.389 --> 03:36:53.208
be different today. Thank you. Okay, we've got two speakers that are coming up for

03:36:53.708 --> 03:36:55.521
the second time, and then I'm going to suggest that we move We move to

03:36:55.553 --> 03:36:59.794
the motion. Sorry, I need a recess or just

03:37:00.294 --> 03:37:04.035
to hold the vote till I get back. Okay, we will continue talking.

03:37:04.468 --> 03:37:06.701
Councillor Salonen, thank you for flagging that.

03:37:07.681 --> 03:37:10.380
Or Councillor Hunnicutt, Councillor Salonen next.

03:37:11.440 --> 03:37:15.151
Thank you, through you, Chair. Going back to some of the

03:37:15.651 --> 03:37:18.412
questions that Councillor Verbanovich had,

03:37:19.569 --> 03:37:22.878
I guess I'm also struggling with the need for the water to

03:37:22.942 --> 03:37:26.603
be permanent. And I'm going back to on,

03:37:27.020 --> 03:37:30.778
I believe this was the January 28th council meeting,

03:37:30.890 --> 03:37:34.727
the possible solutions from immediate

03:37:34.888 --> 03:37:38.822
to medium and long-term. The one that

03:37:39.322 --> 03:37:42.547
council did actually proceed with and decided to allow up to $15

03:37:43.047 --> 03:37:46.882
million to pursue is the pilot of the Mannheim temporary side

03:37:47.382 --> 03:37:50.657
stream treatment which is to be used as a bridge

03:37:50.946 --> 03:37:54.224
until the DAF is completed. So we're already

03:37:54.481 --> 03:37:57.967
using water in a non-permanent way

03:37:58.096 --> 03:38:01.935
to bridge a gap to then a longer-term solution.

03:38:02.546 --> 03:38:06.466
So I don't understand why the Wilmot water

03:38:06.966 --> 03:38:10.579
that you're asking for, the 30 liters a second, can't also be used

03:38:11.109 --> 03:38:14.933
as a bridge, also can be tied to the DAF because

03:38:15.446 --> 03:38:19.709
The capacity is far beyond what—

03:38:20.830 --> 03:38:24.035
in the DAF being completed, what is currently going to be

03:38:24.535 --> 03:38:27.849
created by the Wilmot situation.

03:38:27.946 --> 03:38:31.872
I'm really confused at why one needs to be permanent but the other doesn't,

03:38:32.449 --> 03:38:36.823
especially when the 1980 policy talks about increasing

03:38:36.919 --> 03:38:40.381
our water taking from the river. So I would think

03:38:40.881 --> 03:38:43.720
that that should be more of a permanent solution under the current policy.

03:38:45.036 --> 03:38:48.521
Jeff, uh, through the chair, so I think I need to phone a friend again,

03:38:48.874 --> 03:38:52.535
uh, because now we're getting into engineering solutions. So Nicole,

03:38:52.663 --> 03:38:55.875
are you able to speak to that?

03:39:00.354 --> 03:39:02.426
Thanks, Nicole. I should have introduced you last time.

03:39:04.128 --> 03:39:06.070
No problem. Uh, through the chair,

03:39:06.616 --> 03:39:09.972
I'm going to start with the Mannheim side stream and talking

03:39:10.004 --> 03:39:13.392
about what that means in terms of a temporary solution, and then I'll talk about

03:39:14.083 --> 03:39:17.326
the potential temporary use of the 30 litres per second.

03:39:17.406 --> 03:39:20.762
So in the case of the Mannheim side stream, we did have

03:39:21.262 --> 03:39:24.760
a permanent solution that was already in progress. We were in detailed

03:39:25.260 --> 03:39:28.502
design for that upgrade. What was proposed through

03:39:28.550 --> 03:39:32.451
the side stream treatment was a way to expedite that solution. So we

03:39:32.548 --> 03:39:36.352
recognize that this constraint is causing a lot

03:39:36.852 --> 03:39:39.499
of hardship for the community. And that was one of the solutions that was brought

03:39:39.547 --> 03:39:43.432
forward of, can we do this faster? For the 30 liters per second

03:39:43.480 --> 03:39:47.237
to be temporarily allocated, I think one of the challenges there

03:39:47.737 --> 03:39:50.881
is that if we talk about a temporary allocation, the question becomes,

03:39:51.025 --> 03:39:54.637
until when? So in one case, we had a clear

03:39:55.137 --> 03:39:58.795
solution in terms of time and flow for when it would be in place.

03:39:59.132 --> 03:40:01.927
but to temporarily allocate that 30 litres per second.

03:40:02.585 --> 03:40:06.439
We don't have a specific time frame that we can say

03:40:06.664 --> 03:40:10.566
until this point in time. And if we were to allow development to progress,

03:40:11.079 --> 03:40:14.114
we can't then say, well, that flow is no longer available.

03:40:14.484 --> 03:40:17.856
So that gives staff the confidence and the decision-making to say, yes,

03:40:18.000 --> 03:40:21.774
this can be permanently allocated for growth for the integrated urban

03:40:22.274 --> 03:40:25.901
system. But [Speaker] in my question, I said why can't we tie

03:40:26.013 --> 03:40:29.690
this also to the DA— sorry, I'm really bad with acronyms—

03:40:29.722 --> 03:40:34.026
DAF, because that DAF creates way more new,

03:40:35.487 --> 03:40:38.778
net new people in the system than what the

03:40:39.278 --> 03:40:42.953
30 liters and the pilot of the side stream combined

03:40:43.453 --> 03:40:46.309
has. So why couldn't both of those be tied to that timeline?

03:40:47.320 --> 03:40:50.757
Through the Chair, so as a clarification there, you could

03:40:51.257 --> 03:40:53.993
say until Mannheim side stream stream, but that just means that at that point in

03:40:54.493 --> 03:40:57.860
time, you'd have 30 litres per second less than, than what was projected

03:40:58.360 --> 03:41:02.192
based on that preliminary list of projects. So your total amount that's available in

03:41:02.692 --> 03:41:06.296
that 5-year window has been effectively reduced by the 30 litres per

03:41:06.796 --> 03:41:10.158
second. Correct. But also my understanding is that

03:41:10.544 --> 03:41:13.573
these solutions are continually evolving and we are looking

03:41:13.685 --> 03:41:17.807
for more long-term and medium-term solutions.

03:41:18.367 --> 03:41:22.595
These all in the report. At least it says that these are all to

03:41:22.659 --> 03:41:26.048
be verified and to be

03:41:26.112 --> 03:41:29.273
researched. So I guess I'm just not understanding why we're

03:41:29.773 --> 03:41:33.416
now suddenly saying this is the set amount that we're allowed and

03:41:33.690 --> 03:41:37.151
that we're saying is going to be in the system. I don't understand

03:41:37.651 --> 03:41:42.764
why this one can't be as fluid as all of the other solutions

03:41:42.861 --> 03:41:46.080
that we're exploring. So Nicole's going to call a friend

03:41:46.112 --> 03:41:49.820
now. Jeff, are you going to respond? Actually, through the

03:41:50.320 --> 03:41:54.041
chair, if I— if you don't mind, um, I just wanted to point out with

03:41:54.057 --> 03:41:57.459
respect to the side stream, using that analogy,

03:41:57.604 --> 03:42:01.119
we're not allocating that up to 300

03:42:01.619 --> 03:42:05.468
liters per second now. It's only when it actually will be in place and

03:42:05.968 --> 03:42:09.497
available that we would be allocating that. So it's, it's not a

03:42:09.690 --> 03:42:13.264
similar argument in the sense that we're asking for the 30 liters per

03:42:13.764 --> 03:42:17.817
second to be able to allocate that now. So it's not a bridge

03:42:18.317 --> 03:42:21.953
like what Nicole is saying is it would be until the DAF comes

03:42:21.985 --> 03:42:25.495
online, which is 2029 or 2030. So that doesn't

03:42:25.512 --> 03:42:30.128
help us in this situation, just like the side stream is not adding capacity

03:42:30.240 --> 03:42:33.815
until that's complete. I think that was the missing piece.

03:42:33.911 --> 03:42:37.392
They're both, they're both going to

03:42:37.892 --> 03:42:41.631
be available when, when done. But that's the difference between the

03:42:42.131 --> 03:42:45.613
two, is one is under construction and we're not allocating that capacity

03:42:45.726 --> 03:42:49.226
until it's finished, whereas the 30 liters per second right now could

03:42:49.258 --> 03:42:50.671
be added to that total.

03:42:53.449 --> 03:42:56.853
So, Amy, just to underscore, we talk about capacity. So this is

03:42:56.949 --> 03:43:00.417
the 20% resilience that we are now eating into that

03:43:00.917 --> 03:43:04.560
we have to put back in system before we can think about adding capacity,

03:43:04.994 --> 03:43:08.269
which is exactly what the development community is asking us to

03:43:08.349 --> 03:43:10.774
allocate when we get to that point, correct?

03:43:11.240 --> 03:43:15.318
So through the Chair, there's two pieces. So we need to be operating sustainably,

03:43:15.511 --> 03:43:18.320
and then we need to have an adequate operational resiliency,

03:43:18.545 --> 03:43:23.202
which is the work that Nicole is working on with our area municipal counterparts

03:43:23.702 --> 03:43:26.895
on the interim risk management framework to determine what level of

03:43:27.395 --> 03:43:30.766
operational operational resiliency we would be comfortable with in proceeding. So that

03:43:30.830 --> 03:43:34.123
aspect is part of that framework that's under construction—

03:43:34.623 --> 03:43:38.315
or under development right now. So there's the two pieces. We need to

03:43:38.815 --> 03:43:42.427
be operating sustainably and also have adequate operational resiliency.

03:43:43.198 --> 03:43:45.029
Thank you. Councillor Salmond, do you have one more question?

03:43:47.005 --> 03:43:50.410
I have a lot more questions based off of that, but I

03:43:50.507 --> 03:43:54.040
guess I will switch track over to my bigger

03:43:54.056 --> 03:43:58.905
overarching question. None of the reports today nor

03:43:59.034 --> 03:44:02.807
anything that council's received still explains what

03:44:03.307 --> 03:44:05.826
the trigger was for needing to change our methodology.

03:44:06.725 --> 03:44:09.920
And I have yet to see anything even

03:44:09.952 --> 03:44:13.308
in the Agile report that says our old methodology was not

03:44:13.469 --> 03:44:16.857
valid. And then I get way more confused with the

03:44:17.357 --> 03:44:21.240
information then provided for today that relies on old methodology

03:44:21.674 --> 03:44:24.885
reports and information to verify that what we're

03:44:25.385 --> 03:44:28.754
doing now for the new methodology is okay, um,

03:44:29.605 --> 03:44:33.331
as well as that the new methodology is superior to the old

03:44:33.379 --> 03:44:37.136
methodology. The Agile report just verifies that the new methodology

03:44:37.264 --> 03:44:41.311
is logical sense, but it doesn't discredit the old methodology.

03:44:42.146 --> 03:44:43.960
Looking for that information as well. Please.

03:44:47.743 --> 03:44:48.048
Nicole,

03:44:53.274 --> 03:44:57.057
uh, through the chair, I think for the agile report, one of the,

03:44:57.923 --> 03:45:01.177
the reasons why that was implemented was the observations that staff

03:45:01.289 --> 03:45:04.383
were making in terms of the stressors for the natural environment and what we were

03:45:04.431 --> 03:45:07.958
seeing for the water system. Um, so we could potentially

03:45:08.458 --> 03:45:10.876
take that back at a staffing level and turn in terms of the data for

03:45:11.004 --> 03:45:14.659
what we're seeing. I think Jeff already presented in terms of what

03:45:14.691 --> 03:45:18.362
we're seeing for the aquifer levels, and I'm

03:45:18.862 --> 03:45:22.129
not sure if Ryan presented in terms of actual levels

03:45:22.629 --> 03:45:26.073
in terms of the reservoir levels, how that trended over time, but that information could

03:45:26.573 --> 03:45:30.048
be made available. Councillor Gowing.

03:45:32.485 --> 03:45:35.723
Thanks to you, Chair. So given that, um,

03:45:36.557 --> 03:45:40.808
Staff rely in part on the Region's complaint

03:45:41.308 --> 03:45:45.204
response process to interfere interference occurrences.

03:45:45.782 --> 03:45:49.488
Has the Region's 2025 groundwater interference complaints policy

03:45:49.552 --> 03:45:53.996
been reviewed against the latest hydrogeologic—

03:45:55.247 --> 03:45:58.921
yeah, hydrogeologic— hydrogeology.

03:45:59.113 --> 03:46:02.001
Oh my God, I can't say it. Geology. Yeah, see.

03:46:02.882 --> 03:46:07.028
Geological— there it is— evidence, including current modeling

03:46:07.606 --> 03:46:11.446
and observed drawdown from municipal pumping to

03:46:11.463 --> 03:46:15.369
confirm that the mapping response areas still reflect where impacts

03:46:15.869 --> 03:46:19.364
may actually occur. Thank you for the question.

03:46:19.445 --> 03:46:23.178
Through you, Chair, it's, it's a great question. So we're always looking at

03:46:23.194 --> 03:46:26.975
individual well interference complaints based on their site-specific

03:46:27.475 --> 03:46:31.062
characteristics, but also within our best and most up-to-date understanding of the wellfield.

03:46:31.592 --> 03:46:35.288
So the current reviews that we're completing are based on the most recent information

03:46:35.788 --> 03:46:39.305
that we have. While there are different policy areas that are outlined in the document,

03:46:39.385 --> 03:46:42.776
each one is assessed based on its own individual merit.

03:46:43.403 --> 03:46:47.436
And we can update mapping and

03:46:47.936 --> 03:46:50.650
policy through bringing forward, you know,

03:46:50.730 --> 03:46:54.280
revised procedures and protocols for council endorsement.

03:46:54.361 --> 03:46:58.647
So I believe the last, the last time it was updated was in 2015.

03:46:59.900 --> 03:47:03.191
So it's probably a good time to take a look at that again. And we'd

03:47:03.691 --> 03:47:07.108
certainly be open to doing that moving forward.

03:47:07.670 --> 03:47:10.801
Okay, perfect. Because that's one of the reasons I was going to— I

03:47:11.301 --> 03:47:13.980
brought that up is because it's now 11 years old. So I just want to

03:47:13.996 --> 03:47:16.950
make sure we were looking at that. And then my second question is,

03:47:17.705 --> 03:47:22.089
can you identify exactly what consultation occurred with

03:47:22.105 --> 03:47:26.746
Wilmot Township specifically on the rescinding of the 1980

03:47:26.762 --> 03:47:30.151
policy, separate from the broader regional water strategy

03:47:30.651 --> 03:47:34.520
discussions? And is— and if a resident's well

03:47:34.793 --> 03:47:38.728
is affected today but falls outside of the current

03:47:39.228 --> 03:47:42.984
policy boundary, can staff report based— sorry, respond based

03:47:43.484 --> 03:47:46.566
on the current— here's that word again— hydrogeological evidence,

03:47:47.160 --> 03:47:50.854
or does the existing map still determine who

03:47:51.354 --> 03:47:55.190
gets and who does not get? Through the Chair,

03:47:55.206 --> 03:47:58.531
so the existing policy lays out different areas where—

03:47:58.643 --> 03:48:02.241
so there's historical compensation areas, there's groundwater taking areas, and then there's areas

03:48:02.257 --> 03:48:04.650
that are outside of the Region's pumping influence.

03:48:05.244 --> 03:48:09.099
So we update that information. If a new supply came online or if

03:48:09.599 --> 03:48:13.227
we increased our pumping rates, you know, we would know that we're having impacts

03:48:13.355 --> 03:48:17.498
or had the potential to impact in different areas, potentially larger areas.

03:48:18.156 --> 03:48:21.640
So, yes, we review it within the context

03:48:22.140 --> 03:48:25.590
of our most up-to-date knowledge. Just because something

03:48:25.703 --> 03:48:29.636
is set in stone in a map from 2015 doesn't mean that it precludes

03:48:30.136 --> 03:48:33.426
our ability to look at the case, look at things on a

03:48:33.926 --> 03:48:37.408
case-by-case basis and apply the policy accordingly.

03:48:39.046 --> 03:48:42.321
And I am miss— I'm forgetting this first part of your

03:48:42.821 --> 03:48:46.431
question. You pretty well answered it. Oh, sorry, this— the

03:48:46.931 --> 03:48:50.156
first part was what, besides the water

03:48:50.526 --> 03:48:54.475
strategy, what can you say that you've

03:48:54.604 --> 03:48:58.907
actually went to Wilmot and had a good conversation about rescinding

03:48:59.019 --> 03:49:03.049
the 1980 policy? Yeah, through the chair, I took

03:49:03.549 --> 03:49:06.951
a look through historical documentation around that question, I'm not finding

03:49:06.983 --> 03:49:10.227
a lot. There is a touchpoint here, there was a touchpoint there.

03:49:12.105 --> 03:49:15.108
It probably could have been done better for sure.

03:49:16.055 --> 03:49:19.267
So that's— it's an important, you know,

03:49:19.347 --> 03:49:23.152
moment for us to understand that we need to bring regular updates

03:49:23.184 --> 03:49:26.653
back to council. We need to keep council informed of important decision-making like that,

03:49:27.118 --> 03:49:30.761
regardless of whether we make an interpretation of whether we're adhering to a policy or

03:49:31.261 --> 03:49:34.993
not because as we can see, it's created a lot of confusion and angst

03:49:35.493 --> 03:49:38.183
within the community and within council chambers here. So,

03:49:38.920 --> 03:49:42.479
yes, you know, an acknowledgment that notification

03:49:42.767 --> 03:49:45.124
in 2019 probably should have been better,

03:49:46.439 --> 03:49:49.981
but I can't speak to why those decisions were made. I don't

03:49:50.481 --> 03:49:53.716
have the historical context around that. Can I just— No.

03:49:53.989 --> 03:49:57.359
Quick statement. No. You can state when we go

03:49:57.859 --> 03:50:00.299
to the motion. It was, it was more of thanking him for that statement is

03:50:00.395 --> 03:50:02.901
what I was looking for. Well, you can say thank you then. Yes, thank you

03:50:03.401 --> 03:50:05.969
for that statement because that's exactly what I was looking for. Thank you.

03:50:07.318 --> 03:50:10.868
So colleagues, uh, we're now done with this report and I want to thank,

03:50:10.996 --> 03:50:14.626
um, staff very much. We're going to go to 6.3, which is deferred

03:50:15.126 --> 03:50:18.963
motion on the 1980 policy on water taking. It's already been moved

03:50:19.463 --> 03:50:21.981
by Councillor Salonen, seconded by Councillor Gowing.

03:50:22.815 --> 03:50:26.486
Councillor Salaam, I believe you have some updated wording that

03:50:26.986 --> 03:50:30.670
you wanted to provide. Yes, I do, and the clerks have that if

03:50:31.170 --> 03:50:35.783
it could be brought up. And I will note

03:50:36.283 --> 03:50:40.480
the changes while they're bringing it up. So I've

03:50:40.849 --> 03:50:44.905
removed that council temporarily suspend the water take policy.

03:50:45.498 --> 03:50:48.944
Again, although I do appreciate the ample work,

03:50:48.977 --> 03:50:51.917
and I know that our staff are working overtime on this problem,

03:50:53.726 --> 03:50:57.623
I personally do not agree with the interpretation that

03:50:58.092 --> 03:51:01.814
the 1980 council policy needs to be rescinded or

03:51:02.314 --> 03:51:05.917
removed in any capacity to allow for this flow of 30 litres

03:51:05.966 --> 03:51:08.735
per second. Again, some of that came up in my questioning.

03:51:09.652 --> 03:51:12.260
Some of it was further validated by Fiona's reply.

03:51:13.337 --> 03:51:17.255
That the policy isn't— well, that the policy

03:51:17.287 --> 03:51:20.499
would be a limitation if it limited the amount of water being pumped, but we

03:51:20.563 --> 03:51:25.701
aren't even near that amount. So, and Fiona,

03:51:25.717 --> 03:51:29.233
please, if that is not how I properly interpreted it, but that is what

03:51:29.733 --> 03:51:33.793
I took from your response from another councillor's question. So I did remove that part.

03:51:33.842 --> 03:51:37.358
So effectively we'd be keeping the 1980 water

03:51:37.858 --> 03:51:41.661
policy as is. Um, the rest of the motion remains

03:51:41.757 --> 03:51:46.205
relatively the same apart from I did add in a

03:51:46.705 --> 03:51:50.219
new language on that should staff choose to pump

03:51:50.719 --> 03:51:54.217
additional water from the Wilmot Centre wells into the integrated

03:51:54.717 --> 03:51:57.637
urban system, that the water be treated as follows. Um,

03:51:58.055 --> 03:52:02.214
the water be used as an immediate bridge solution until the dissolved

03:52:02.310 --> 03:52:05.875
air flotation is completed. It does note, like the

03:52:06.375 --> 03:52:10.180
Mannheim temporary side stream treatment,

03:52:10.196 --> 03:52:13.890
that the additional water pumping cannot exceed the

03:52:14.390 --> 03:52:18.436
staff's request of the 30 liters a second, that any observed or reported

03:52:18.533 --> 03:52:21.311
adverse impacts be immediately reported to council.

03:52:22.307 --> 03:52:25.793
That should say with, not will. Sorry, typo on my end.

03:52:26.500 --> 03:52:31.367
With expert data-backed opinion of the increase

03:52:31.867 --> 03:52:35.123
if the increased pumping can proceed. And that any decision to make the additional

03:52:35.171 --> 03:52:38.495
water pumping from the Wilmot Centre wells permanent to the

03:52:38.543 --> 03:52:42.156
Integrated Urban System must be tied to the completed water

03:52:42.656 --> 03:52:46.250
supply strategy for council decision at the time and any additional relevant data

03:52:46.588 --> 03:52:49.269
or information or informed decision-making,

03:52:49.301 --> 03:52:52.802
including the understanding of all well interference claims and their correlation

03:52:52.914 --> 03:52:55.644
to the pumping from this well. Field.

03:52:56.366 --> 03:52:59.963
So that's been added on to the other motion

03:53:00.463 --> 03:53:04.475
that you guys have now seen multiple times, and we've deferred multiple times. I did

03:53:04.539 --> 03:53:07.879
also add in, just in part 7, to make it really, really clear,

03:53:08.553 --> 03:53:12.310
um, that staff includes separate modeling and water allocation

03:53:12.326 --> 03:53:15.859
for the mega site, and that it's clearly independent from Wilmot's growth

03:53:15.875 --> 03:53:19.584
needs. Um, that's something that I think is paramount that

03:53:19.632 --> 03:53:23.276
we consider. Um, so with that, really what

03:53:23.776 --> 03:53:26.678
I see this motion being is a compromise between one,

03:53:27.738 --> 03:53:31.349
what we actually have the authority and ability to already do is verified

03:53:31.849 --> 03:53:35.217
through all the information we got for this council that we haven't

03:53:35.717 --> 03:53:38.941
violated the policy from 2019 taking more water,

03:53:39.535 --> 03:53:43.274
and so it's able to continue under the current policy, um,

03:53:43.563 --> 03:53:47.383
keeping the current policy in place until the water strategy comes

03:53:47.480 --> 03:53:51.076
back, which is really when we should be making permanent decisions

03:53:52.233 --> 03:53:55.974
about our water supply, nor does it prohibit

03:53:56.022 --> 03:53:59.330
the ability for staff to continue on

03:53:59.378 --> 03:54:02.878
the water supply strategy process because, again, it's at

03:54:02.974 --> 03:54:06.459
that point that we make decisions if policies need to be updated to reflect the

03:54:06.959 --> 03:54:10.794
new water supply strategy. And then it also balances and allows for

03:54:11.163 --> 03:54:14.838
growth to take place. Place, which our development community desperately needs.

03:54:15.495 --> 03:54:18.335
And certainly from my perspective, um,

03:54:18.544 --> 03:54:22.218
and that of my Wilmot Council, we don't want to

03:54:22.586 --> 03:54:25.747
stand in the way of allowing development to happen,

03:54:25.859 --> 03:54:29.437
but we want to ensure that we are, um, fully understanding

03:54:29.469 --> 03:54:32.405
and have the information for correct decision-making,

03:54:33.063 --> 03:54:36.095
um, behind what impacts on our water are having.

03:54:36.448 --> 03:54:39.559
So with that, I would I would look for everyone's

03:54:40.059 --> 03:54:43.552
support. So you're moving an amendment. Councillor Gowing,

03:54:43.648 --> 03:54:46.551
are you willing to second this amendment? I am, yes.

03:54:47.192 --> 03:54:49.918
Do you want to speak to it? Nope. Okay.

03:54:50.704 --> 03:54:53.767
Councillor Hunnicke. Councillor Salonen, thank you for bringing this

03:54:54.267 --> 03:54:57.343
forward. Do you know if you have the support of the Niitsitapi, the Neeth Valley

03:54:57.375 --> 03:55:01.624
EcoBoosters for this development? And also there

03:55:02.124 --> 03:55:04.751
is an organization led by Samantha Lernout. I forget the name of it.

03:55:05.859 --> 03:55:09.456
Water for Wilmot, I think. Thank you. That one.

03:55:10.597 --> 03:55:13.552
Um, are they in support of this that you know of?

03:55:15.126 --> 03:55:18.210
So I can't speak on behalf of those organizations.

03:55:18.467 --> 03:55:22.402
Um, I think anything that

03:55:22.498 --> 03:55:26.513
balances this growth and considers realistically what's

03:55:27.013 --> 03:55:28.987
happening in the community, um,

03:55:30.191 --> 03:55:32.970
would be something I would hope would be well received. I do know that I

03:55:33.259 --> 03:55:36.998
personally spoke to Samantha this morning and let her know of my intent.

03:55:37.656 --> 03:55:41.522
Um, but again, I wouldn't be willing to make claims on their behalf.

03:55:41.699 --> 03:55:45.437
Okay, thanks. Councillor James.

03:55:45.694 --> 03:55:48.871
Yeah, thank you. Through you, Chair, um, I just,

03:55:48.887 --> 03:55:52.015
uh, this is really complex, so,

03:55:52.176 --> 03:55:55.561
um, I have, uh, comments before I kind of state where I am with

03:55:56.061 --> 03:55:59.620
the position on this, but I, I feel like we're off— we're framing this

03:55:59.749 --> 03:56:03.474
is, you know, growth versus protection. I see it

03:56:03.827 --> 03:56:07.327
differently. I want to see us using the best tools available

03:56:07.424 --> 03:56:11.566
to protect our water system while also, well, responsibly supporting

03:56:12.160 --> 03:56:15.227
community needs. And based on what I've heard today,

03:56:16.094 --> 03:56:19.386
and be it from public, be it from staff,

03:56:19.402 --> 03:56:23.577
in particular the hydrologists, the, the consultants

03:56:24.077 --> 03:56:26.754
who've been working in this field for many, many, many decades.

03:56:27.652 --> 03:56:31.324
I think this policy, the 1984— 1980 Wilmot

03:56:31.356 --> 03:56:34.901
Water Take Policy, was created in a very different regulatory and

03:56:35.401 --> 03:56:38.974
scientific context. Today we're talking— we're taking— water

03:56:39.474 --> 03:56:42.374
taking is managed through provincial permits, technical standards,

03:56:42.743 --> 03:56:46.560
hydrogeological modeling, ongoing monitoring,

03:56:46.736 --> 03:56:50.152
and robust data. And this is where it lies.

03:56:50.200 --> 03:56:53.268
And I think If we look at what was decided last week at

03:56:53.574 --> 03:56:56.916
council when we've reinforced the oversight by calling on

03:56:56.948 --> 03:57:00.178
the province to pause water taking permits, we kind of

03:57:00.678 --> 03:57:04.500
highlighted that safeguard because a lot of what has changed

03:57:05.000 --> 03:57:07.521
since 1980, there's been provincial oversight.

03:57:07.955 --> 03:57:11.939
So I do want to acknowledge that concerns

03:57:11.955 --> 03:57:17.192
raised by residents about the groundwater and the private water wells and

03:57:17.692 --> 03:57:20.960
those impacts. I don't know if they're— I want— I,

03:57:21.184 --> 03:57:24.086
I just don't know if they're directly correlated, but I can see how people can

03:57:24.586 --> 03:57:27.837
make that assumption. And, um, but I think staff have

03:57:27.853 --> 03:57:31.508
kind of spoken to this, and, and especially some of those,

03:57:31.780 --> 03:57:35.708
those experts as well, and broader community. Um, so I

03:57:36.208 --> 03:57:39.267
think we're maintaining a policy that is completely outdated. And, um,

03:57:40.261 --> 03:57:44.704
this is the problem we get in as governments. We maintain these policies that

03:57:45.204 --> 03:57:48.205
are outdated. This is kind of how we have things like our housing crisis and

03:57:48.705 --> 03:57:52.443
all of that that we're in now, because there's policies that are being implemented

03:57:52.877 --> 03:57:56.554
that are moot. They're not current to where we are with regulatory

03:57:57.598 --> 03:58:01.660
and even science and data. So while

03:58:01.788 --> 03:58:05.883
I recognize that, you know, we need

03:58:06.383 --> 03:58:10.745
to do what we need to in terms of safeguards, I believe that this policy

03:58:10.777 --> 03:58:14.594
should be rescinded, and I think if there is anything, there needs to

03:58:14.626 --> 03:58:17.753
be work done. Um, so,

03:58:18.219 --> 03:58:20.432
you know, that's kind of, that's kind of where I am right now.

03:58:21.346 --> 03:58:24.714
Um, I think there are pieces of this motion that

03:58:25.034 --> 03:58:28.867
are, are necessary and conscious, and I think my question

03:58:28.899 --> 03:58:32.476
for staff is, you know, what is realistic with this motion

03:58:32.492 --> 03:58:36.436
in terms of the ability to maintain all of

03:58:36.936 --> 03:58:40.732
this within our scope as regional. So I'm

03:58:40.829 --> 03:58:44.291
leaning towards rescinding just because I think we're working with outdated

03:58:44.791 --> 03:58:47.385
policy here. And that outdated policy is,

03:58:47.578 --> 03:58:51.121
if it's not current to time and place, we're setting

03:58:51.621 --> 03:58:54.680
ourself up for future challenges with our systems.

03:58:57.116 --> 03:59:00.805
Councillor Harris. Oh, Amy, go ahead. So through

03:59:01.305 --> 03:59:04.402
the chair, yes, I just wanted to say I agree with that.

03:59:04.964 --> 03:59:08.400
As Jeff mentioned, we have much stronger legislative

03:59:08.416 --> 03:59:11.354
requirements now than this policy protected for.

03:59:12.029 --> 03:59:15.818
I'm just seeing this for the first time on the screen, so I'm reviewing it.

03:59:16.605 --> 03:59:20.105
We do have the existing well interference protocol,

03:59:20.250 --> 03:59:24.232
so that last highlighted bullet there, it may not

03:59:24.732 --> 03:59:27.520
follow that process. So I just want to flag— sorry, not the last bullet,

03:59:28.722 --> 03:59:33.467
the larger highlighted portion up above there, that last bullet around

03:59:33.708 --> 03:59:37.764
the technical piece on the well interference. So we do follow that policy.

03:59:38.277 --> 03:59:41.579
Whether we look at revising that policy is something that

03:59:41.643 --> 03:59:44.994
we could consider bringing back to council, but we are following that policy,

03:59:45.330 --> 03:59:49.065
and I'm not sure that bullet follows that policy as written

03:59:49.098 --> 03:59:52.898
right now. I would— I do also say that these are

03:59:53.587 --> 03:59:57.388
outdated— this is an outdated policy. It does fly

03:59:57.580 --> 04:00:00.948
in kind of the face of the way the water supply strategy update is carried

04:00:01.448 --> 04:00:04.717
out, as we are looking for water supply for all residents

04:00:04.797 --> 04:00:08.390
and not these individual protectionist type policies.

04:00:08.839 --> 04:00:12.495
So I do have concerns about extending it until 2029 or

04:00:12.995 --> 04:00:16.857
2030 temporarily. So with that,

04:00:16.890 --> 04:00:19.394
But I yield back.

04:00:21.112 --> 04:00:24.597
Sorry, Councillor James, are you done? I am, I'm, I'm done

04:00:24.629 --> 04:00:27.519
for now. Okay, and thank you, Amy. Councillor Harris.

04:00:27.744 --> 04:00:31.453
Yeah, no, well, Councillor James spoke to, to some of

04:00:31.953 --> 04:00:34.985
the similar items I had, and I want to thank, you know,

04:00:35.210 --> 04:00:38.502
Amy Shaw for your response. Sometimes we don't get a candid

04:00:39.002 --> 04:00:42.147
answer. I get that that's your professional opinion,

04:00:42.211 --> 04:00:45.438
and I share that with you. I mean, this is a regional— this is,

04:00:45.567 --> 04:00:49.773
uh, you know, I understand, um, you know, the, the councillor's,

04:00:50.062 --> 04:00:53.611
uh, perspective coming from her constituency, but again, you know,

04:00:53.916 --> 04:00:57.384
almost or over 40-year-old policy. We have provincial

04:00:57.884 --> 04:01:01.045
backstops, uh, it conflicts with our existing policies.

04:01:01.720 --> 04:01:05.156
I guess one quick question for Nicole is it— I believe

04:01:05.656 --> 04:01:09.106
you had mentioned that there is no other policy within the region that is similar

04:01:09.606 --> 04:01:11.354
to this. Is that just— is that correct in what I heard?

04:01:14.003 --> 04:01:16.732
Okay, so yes, so, so, uh, good answer.

04:01:16.877 --> 04:01:20.393
Um, you know, for that, for that very reason, with everything else we have in

04:01:20.425 --> 04:01:23.139
place, I mean, there's, there's just no way I'm going to be able to support

04:01:23.639 --> 04:01:27.009
this, and I would, uh, support the staff recommendation. I hope we can get there

04:01:27.509 --> 04:01:30.381
relatively soon, um, as, uh, we have to advance,

04:01:30.846 --> 04:01:34.426
you know, this significant, uh, water issue as as it is a regional

04:01:34.474 --> 04:01:36.992
issue, um, and, uh, go from there.

04:01:38.323 --> 04:01:38.964
Councillor Erb.

04:01:42.155 --> 04:01:44.096
Um, thank you, um, Madam Chair.

04:01:45.603 --> 04:01:49.260
I, um, I would like to— I'm not going to support,

04:01:49.484 --> 04:01:52.627
support this, um, um, amendment. I,

04:01:52.756 --> 04:01:56.107
I understand the concerns of, um, Councillor Salonen

04:01:56.607 --> 04:01:59.892
and representing the interests of Wilmot, but I, I do think I

04:02:00.392 --> 04:02:04.998
think our staff and the consultants we have hired have been very

04:02:05.175 --> 04:02:09.061
diligent in bringing the information forward that we require

04:02:09.125 --> 04:02:12.625
to make a decision. This is an outdated policy.

04:02:13.460 --> 04:02:16.591
We're not doing— making decisions today

04:02:16.896 --> 04:02:20.059
based on policy from 1980 for childcare,

04:02:20.910 --> 04:02:24.748
for construction, for the placement of underground services.

04:02:25.262 --> 04:02:28.634
And I don't know why we would be wanting to adhere to

04:02:29.134 --> 04:02:32.166
a policy from 1980 for water.

04:02:33.113 --> 04:02:36.710
I don't think this sends a solid message

04:02:36.742 --> 04:02:40.837
to the development community. It's another put-off

04:02:41.337 --> 04:02:44.610
of another couple of years until we get a final answer. And so

04:02:45.110 --> 04:02:48.768
for many of the reasons that Councillor James has highlighted and Councillor

04:02:49.268 --> 04:02:53.007
Harris, I I want to eventually support the staff,

04:02:53.023 --> 04:02:56.636
the decision that we rescind the 1980 policy

04:02:56.652 --> 04:03:00.426
and move forward and give some stability to this whole system. And I can't say

04:03:00.490 --> 04:03:04.038
enough about the professional advice we've

04:03:04.538 --> 04:03:08.261
been getting from our staff. Very, very convincing

04:03:08.293 --> 04:03:12.035
today on the presentation on the graphs and the monitoring

04:03:12.131 --> 04:03:15.968
that's been done and so extensively in Wilmut compared to other

04:03:15.984 --> 04:03:19.512
water systems that we have in the region. And I think there's just,

04:03:20.314 --> 04:03:23.537
um, no, no question in my mind that the, um, the data

04:03:23.601 --> 04:03:27.033
we've been given verifies that we can rescind this policy and move

04:03:27.049 --> 04:03:31.042
ahead safely. Thank you, Councillor Erb. Councillor Salonen.

04:03:32.340 --> 04:03:35.547
Thank you, through you, Chair. Um, I think

04:03:36.028 --> 04:03:39.845
the reliance on the province having all these new safeguards,

04:03:39.893 --> 04:03:43.228
one, goes against how we as a region tend

04:03:43.324 --> 04:03:47.482
to put our own better best practices

04:03:47.514 --> 04:03:50.934
in place. And I would say the countryside line is one of those things.

04:03:52.475 --> 04:03:56.329
I would say in speaking to people who were around when this motion was actually

04:03:56.829 --> 04:03:58.544
put in place, that was also the spirit of this.

04:04:00.343 --> 04:04:03.490
And then I also really struggle with the fact of we're saying, well,

04:04:03.506 --> 04:04:07.070
the province has protections in place, but then our methodology

04:04:07.102 --> 04:04:10.794
that was based off of the province isn't valid So we need to

04:04:11.294 --> 04:04:15.268
create our own Waterloo Region-specific methodology. That is just not

04:04:16.647 --> 04:04:20.608
a square that is circling for me. But I

04:04:20.640 --> 04:04:24.023
can appreciate the comments that have been made.

04:04:25.467 --> 04:04:28.802
One thing I will correct is the language on

04:04:29.636 --> 04:04:33.452
the interference. All it's saying is that

04:04:34.270 --> 04:04:37.864
additional relevant information be brought forward. I'm not saying

04:04:37.960 --> 04:04:40.481
to not do our interference, well interference, um,

04:04:41.268 --> 04:04:45.089
policy or follow how those claims are supposed to be done. I'm simply saying

04:04:45.185 --> 04:04:48.830
that data be brought back to council, uh, with the water supply

04:04:49.330 --> 04:04:53.149
strategy, either verifying, yep, those claims were

04:04:53.649 --> 04:04:57.051
related to pumping, or no, they weren't. I'm not trying to predetermine outcomes.

04:04:57.870 --> 04:05:01.450
Um, I would also say too, the water supply strategy is

04:05:01.562 --> 04:05:05.143
really where these decisions and updating policies is supposed to come.

04:05:05.834 --> 04:05:09.767
Right now there's a tension of staff trying to get us to

04:05:10.267 --> 04:05:13.669
rescind policies based off of work that they're currently doing

04:05:13.765 --> 04:05:17.169
on a water supply strategy that council has not seen. And I

04:05:17.314 --> 04:05:21.424
still have outstanding questions as to why that strategy

04:05:21.489 --> 04:05:25.519
wasn't even brought forward when it was supposed to be done in fall of 2025,

04:05:25.567 --> 04:05:29.324
which is still up on the regional website. As the timeline

04:05:29.356 --> 04:05:33.017
for completion. And the only time that we heard it wasn't

04:05:33.049 --> 04:05:36.742
going to be proceeding on that timeline was when we found out about this water

04:05:37.242 --> 04:05:41.126
constraint, which was already past the date that we should have gotten the supply strategy

04:05:41.238 --> 04:05:45.060
back. So I'm not saying that

04:05:46.473 --> 04:05:50.053
staff haven't continued to provide relevant information.

04:05:50.294 --> 04:05:53.425
I'm not saying that this policy doesn't need to be reviewed.

04:05:53.522 --> 04:05:57.759
Nor was I saying that this policy needs to be extended to 2030

04:05:57.775 --> 04:06:00.809
or 2031. I was saying the water can be pumped with

04:06:01.309 --> 04:06:05.063
this policy still being in place because that is how you justified pumping water

04:06:05.563 --> 04:06:07.455
in 2019 and continuing on.

04:06:08.032 --> 04:06:12.190
I'm really struggling with this when this

04:06:12.366 --> 04:06:15.946
really is a compromise on allowing development to proceed while

04:06:16.074 --> 04:06:20.087
also acknowledging that there are some legitimate concerns

04:06:20.587 --> 04:06:23.552
and still outstanding questions related to this ongoing issue.

04:06:25.220 --> 04:06:28.572
But I can appreciate if people don't

04:06:29.072 --> 04:06:33.319
see it that way. I certainly think that

04:06:33.544 --> 04:06:36.125
we really need to, especially on groundwater,

04:06:37.489 --> 04:06:40.616
be proceeding in a cautionary way,

04:06:40.921 --> 04:06:44.417
but a way that also allows for development, and this motion does allow for that.

04:06:45.090 --> 04:06:48.716
And again, this policy does

04:06:48.780 --> 04:06:52.329
not need to be revoked in order for those 30 litres a second to be

04:06:52.829 --> 04:06:56.568
transferred into the system, as they already have been for the last 6

04:06:57.068 --> 04:07:00.389
years almost now. Councillor Gowing.

04:07:01.626 --> 04:07:05.576
Uh, thanks, Chair. I was actually going to mimic most

04:07:06.076 --> 04:07:09.831
of what Councillor Salonen was saying there, but I will ask

04:07:10.331 --> 04:07:13.812
if we can have a suggestion based on what Councillor James,

04:07:14.470 --> 04:07:17.567
uh, was saying that she can look at some of it but not the

04:07:17.615 --> 04:07:21.739
overall. So maybe we can vote on each individual piece of this,

04:07:22.654 --> 04:07:26.008
uh, so that we can get some things approved instead of just saying

04:07:26.120 --> 04:07:29.731
no to the whole thing. So are you asking for each

04:07:30.231 --> 04:07:33.887
of the sections to be taken separately? I'm not seeing a lot of support

04:07:34.387 --> 04:07:37.771
for— okay, so we have

04:07:38.271 --> 04:07:40.498
to vote on the amendment first and then we will come back And, uh,

04:07:41.844 --> 04:07:41.956
okay,

04:07:47.773 --> 04:07:50.817
okay, so we have to vote on the amendment first. Okay.

04:07:51.522 --> 04:07:52.708
Uh, Councillor Vrbanovic.

04:07:54.262 --> 04:07:57.467
Thank you very much, um, Madam Chair, and,

04:07:57.611 --> 04:08:01.280
um, you know, thank you for— to, to both staff and,

04:08:01.392 --> 04:08:05.382
and colleagues for the, uh, healthy and, and important

04:08:05.882 --> 04:08:09.293
discussion on, on a very difficult and challenging issue. I really,

04:08:09.790 --> 04:08:12.949
um, need to get a clear answer

04:08:13.510 --> 04:08:17.311
from, from legal and staff because

04:08:17.407 --> 04:08:20.887
I, I think this is, this is paramount. You know,

04:08:20.935 --> 04:08:22.667
we're hearing that, um,

04:08:25.040 --> 04:08:28.664
the motion that's before us would potentially

04:08:29.164 --> 04:08:33.780
not allow staff to continue to draw the 30 liters per

04:08:34.280 --> 04:08:37.483
second. And the— but we're also here

04:08:37.547 --> 04:08:40.977
hearing that it's okay, we've been doing this this

04:08:41.477 --> 04:08:45.656
whole time, and we can continue doing this. And so

04:08:46.346 --> 04:08:49.583
what I want to know, and because it wasn't answered when I asked it earlier,

04:08:50.449 --> 04:08:54.616
is there wording that would allow us to

04:08:55.116 --> 04:08:58.735
not cancel the policy but allow the

04:08:59.969 --> 04:09:03.719
30 leaders to, you know, be added into the system

04:09:04.313 --> 04:09:08.215
and give staff the direction they're looking

04:09:08.311 --> 04:09:10.591
for so that they have the comfort they need.

04:09:11.378 --> 04:09:15.087
Because if, if there is wording, that's the way we should

04:09:15.151 --> 04:09:19.470
go, in my view. If there isn't wording and

04:09:19.970 --> 04:09:23.340
there is no legal or policy direction that can

04:09:23.840 --> 04:09:27.804
be provided by council short of taking the decision of rescinding the policy,

04:09:28.221 --> 04:09:29.361
that's a different story.

04:09:31.850 --> 04:09:35.318
So I'm going to look to Fiona and Amy because my sense is that this

04:09:35.447 --> 04:09:38.080
is an intersection of, um,

04:09:39.557 --> 04:09:43.218
professional, um, protocols for people

04:09:43.250 --> 04:09:46.638
who have to sign forms and, um, whether or

04:09:47.138 --> 04:09:49.576
not this is a legal document. So I don't know who wants to go first,

04:09:49.657 --> 04:09:53.414
but to— from my understanding of this, this is a conversion of

04:09:53.462 --> 04:09:57.185
these two tensions. So through

04:09:57.201 --> 04:10:00.071
the chair, I might actually call up Nicole just to be on,

04:10:01.611 --> 04:10:04.930
so she's here if I need her. Um, with— so I think

04:10:04.979 --> 04:10:08.426
the difference is what we were saying is it's making it available for

04:10:08.926 --> 04:10:12.275
development, and I know that's difficult to understand, but I, I do feel like

04:10:12.775 --> 04:10:16.285
staff tried to answer that a few times, and in, in the

04:10:16.785 --> 04:10:20.198
report I think we've tried to make that clear that this goes against this,

04:10:20.534 --> 04:10:23.921
the water strategy update in how we look for

04:10:24.421 --> 04:10:28.061
water. By moving this down the road, we are going

04:10:28.078 --> 04:10:31.031
to be looking for additional water sources, and realistically,

04:10:31.079 --> 04:10:35.236
some of those will be in Wilmot Township moving forward. So being limited

04:10:35.736 --> 04:10:38.895
by a 40-year-old policy on specific limits and

04:10:39.008 --> 04:10:42.988
total limits is going to cause problems for us operationally

04:10:43.245 --> 04:10:48.541
and long-term for growth. So I don't know, Nicole, if you want

04:10:49.041 --> 04:10:53.359
Nicole, I think you covered

04:10:53.859 --> 04:10:57.181
it. But is there— is, is there a— I

04:10:57.213 --> 04:11:00.344
understand the long-term issue and

04:11:00.844 --> 04:11:04.085
we would need to deal with it, but in terms of providing, um,

04:11:04.438 --> 04:11:07.714
adding in the 30 liters now so that we can continue the

04:11:07.826 --> 04:11:11.262
work, is that, is that something that

04:11:11.762 --> 04:11:15.657
staff can do based on the existing policy being

04:11:15.770 --> 04:11:18.897
in place, or is there alternative wording that

04:11:19.397 --> 04:11:23.051
would— that could give you the, I guess, cover that

04:11:23.551 --> 04:11:26.659
you're looking for from a policy point of view in

04:11:27.159 --> 04:11:30.588
order to, to allow that to proceed? That's what I'm, what I'm

04:11:31.088 --> 04:11:35.175
trying to, to understand. So my understanding, it would be to rescind the

04:11:35.675 --> 04:11:38.976
policy. That would be clear, and that would eliminate the uncertainty.

04:11:39.024 --> 04:11:42.734
But Nicole, go ahead. Through the chair,

04:11:43.103 --> 04:11:46.459
yes, you summarized that well, Tarisa. The policy provides that clarity,

04:11:46.812 --> 04:11:49.445
and I think one of the things that staff would struggle with is that if

04:11:49.461 --> 04:11:53.058
we did this on a temporary basis, again, if we don't

04:11:53.138 --> 04:11:56.735
have that certainty in terms of where is that next 30 litres

04:11:56.767 --> 04:12:00.364
per second coming from, then that creates challenges for how much you can

04:12:00.444 --> 04:12:04.233
release for development. So we could say that the decision is deferred

04:12:04.733 --> 04:12:07.958
to the water supply strategy update, but the preliminary list of projects

04:12:08.247 --> 04:12:11.346
in the next 5 years, that's very unlikely to change because

04:12:11.379 --> 04:12:14.991
we have an extensive amount of technical experts and regional

04:12:15.491 --> 04:12:19.086
staff who are looking at doing everything that they can to expedite projects, and that

04:12:19.586 --> 04:12:23.228
preliminary projects list is a result of all of that work. So it's very,

04:12:23.325 --> 04:12:27.419
very unlikely that we're going to have, like, this— another 30 liters per second

04:12:27.919 --> 04:12:31.077
that's going to be as cost-effective and as available to support growth in the near

04:12:31.125 --> 04:12:35.181
term. So we would be looking at outside of that 5-year window until

04:12:35.213 --> 04:12:38.627
you'd have something that might be enough to be in a surplus

04:12:38.804 --> 04:12:42.523
state. So that's where I was trying to say we

04:12:42.811 --> 04:12:46.627
could say that it's allocated until Mannheim Side Stream, but if Mannheim

04:12:46.691 --> 04:12:50.490
Side Stream were to give us, let's say it's up to 300 litres

04:12:50.522 --> 04:12:53.905
per second. So if it's 300, then it's 270 of

04:12:53.953 --> 04:12:57.466
that that becomes available at that time. Time. So you're just— you're reducing it within

04:12:57.562 --> 04:12:58.381
that 5-year window.

04:13:00.451 --> 04:13:03.597
Fiona, so the long and the short of it— oh, sorry, Fiona.

04:13:04.159 --> 04:13:07.032
Fiona, do you want to comment on— sorry, it's okay,

04:13:07.465 --> 04:13:11.349
I'll just keep jumping in. Fiona, did you want to respond from a legal perspective?

04:13:13.628 --> 04:13:16.405
Uh, thank you. And through you, I think through the, um,

04:13:17.400 --> 04:13:21.011
through the comments made, if the additional amount

04:13:21.511 --> 04:13:25.151
of water is being used to support development and for approvals

04:13:25.183 --> 04:13:28.696
to be based on that, then it needs to be a permanent

04:13:29.196 --> 04:13:32.290
fixture. Councillor Uranovich.

04:13:33.429 --> 04:13:37.376
Thank you. Thank you. In light of, in light

04:13:37.876 --> 04:13:41.339
of all that, and again, I struggle because I,

04:13:42.093 --> 04:13:45.141
I, I'm not sure

04:13:45.641 --> 04:13:48.734
that I, I share those interpretations,

04:13:48.798 --> 04:13:52.213
but I have to rely on our professional staff

04:13:52.713 --> 04:13:56.398
in this regard. And at the end of the day,

04:13:57.088 --> 04:14:00.631
I do think that, you know,

04:14:01.032 --> 04:14:05.249
no matter what we're proposing, the 30 meters

04:14:05.842 --> 04:14:07.558
would be put into the mix.

04:14:08.151 --> 04:14:11.502
And the only question is,

04:14:12.015 --> 04:14:15.574
what are the safeguards going forward? And I

04:14:15.686 --> 04:14:19.484
think that's something that we're going to need to work on diligently

04:14:19.984 --> 04:14:23.441
through the, through the process. I think that's going to be key to

04:14:23.793 --> 04:14:27.719
restoring trust, particularly from the

04:14:29.065 --> 04:14:32.734
Wilmot community on this issue with the, with the region.

04:14:33.727 --> 04:14:37.380
But, you know, when everything comes in, this is what council needs

04:14:37.396 --> 04:14:39.975
to do is balance all sides of an argument.

04:14:41.161 --> 04:14:44.301
I think based on the information that I have,

04:14:44.622 --> 04:14:47.496
I would have to, uh, support the rescinding. Thank you.

04:14:48.620 --> 04:14:51.992
Thank you, Councillor Vanovich. And you know, I also agree with you that we need

04:14:52.492 --> 04:14:56.022
to extend these meetings. So, um, I'm going to do a little bit

04:14:56.522 --> 04:14:59.860
of, um, chair intervention here. I know that Councillor Salonen wants to get

04:15:00.360 --> 04:15:03.504
back on. I'll give her the last word. I do believe that we have to

04:15:03.569 --> 04:15:07.230
listen to our experts. I also would acknowledge that this is a very

04:15:07.406 --> 04:15:10.842
old, um, agreement, a policy that was

04:15:10.890 --> 04:15:13.732
made at a very different time in our history.

04:15:14.391 --> 04:15:17.715
There are no other policies like this

04:15:17.860 --> 04:15:21.682
in any area of the region. The water is in

04:15:21.778 --> 04:15:25.102
many ways an integrated system. It's not

04:15:25.602 --> 04:15:28.715
fully integrated, but there is some integration in the urban setting.

04:15:29.406 --> 04:15:33.661
So it's not like one area of water belongs to that area

04:15:34.161 --> 04:15:36.424
when the system really flows throughout.

04:15:37.853 --> 04:15:41.370
The motion that's before us, even with the amendment by Councillor

04:15:41.870 --> 04:15:46.204
Salonen and Councillor Gowing, continues to perpetuate that uncertainty,

04:15:46.846 --> 04:15:50.316
not only for the community but for developers who I would tell

04:15:50.380 --> 04:15:53.962
you have been all in, in trying to find solutions.

04:15:54.540 --> 04:15:57.897
When we get to a point where there is an allocation that

04:15:57.929 --> 04:16:01.591
can be assigned to growth, we have

04:16:01.623 --> 04:16:06.217
to recognize that that allocation may not line up exactly

04:16:06.506 --> 04:16:10.518
with an area municipal boundary. So we continue

04:16:11.018 --> 04:16:14.812
to go forward hand in hand, in lockstep, because it's

04:16:14.876 --> 04:16:18.706
not compartmentalized. That's not the way water works. That's not

04:16:19.206 --> 04:16:22.440
the way our system has developed. So on a go-forward basis,

04:16:22.632 --> 04:16:26.846
I think continuing to perpetuate this uncertainty is

04:16:27.103 --> 04:16:30.243
a disservice to our constituents,

04:16:31.189 --> 04:16:34.618
to the development and the growth that we know everybody wants and that

04:16:34.634 --> 04:16:39.032
has been approved by the planning authority that is now in area municipalities.

04:16:39.465 --> 04:16:43.078
So I think the only option we have that will make it

04:16:43.578 --> 04:16:48.264
a clear path forward is rescinding the 1980 policy

04:16:48.377 --> 04:16:50.528
with Wilmot and moving forward.

04:16:51.443 --> 04:16:54.719
So having said that, Councillor Salonen, you want

04:16:55.219 --> 04:16:57.561
the last word before we take a vote on the amendment?

04:16:59.199 --> 04:17:02.683
Yes, I do, Chair. I would say

04:17:02.747 --> 04:17:06.232
you completely missed the point of what I'm even talking about.

04:17:06.986 --> 04:17:10.342
I am not talking about protecting water in

04:17:10.454 --> 04:17:14.099
Wilmot, and I completely accept and have said from day one,

04:17:15.015 --> 04:17:18.675
water is a provincially managed Crown

04:17:18.868 --> 04:17:21.550
resource. Fully understand that.

04:17:22.015 --> 04:17:25.708
What I am asking is that clearly this is in place historically

04:17:26.208 --> 04:17:29.817
for a reason. and it should be tied to decision-making around water

04:17:30.317 --> 04:17:33.989
supply strategies. That is when we change our policies. That is when we

04:17:34.054 --> 04:17:37.375
learn and find that we have outdated policies. And that,

04:17:37.488 --> 04:17:40.842
frankly, is a document that has not been provided at the time it was

04:17:40.874 --> 04:17:44.773
supposed to, but now we're being asked to make decisions on findings

04:17:44.790 --> 04:17:48.577
that staff have on it, but not council in front

04:17:49.077 --> 04:17:52.075
of us. Um, further, again,

04:17:52.268 --> 04:17:56.340
this is a compromise to allow for the

04:17:56.840 --> 04:18:00.554
growth that we do need. And frankly, which I didn't mention before

04:18:00.763 --> 04:18:04.208
but will now, um, as far as I see it, staff could have

04:18:04.224 --> 04:18:07.557
done this solution before we even announced there was a water

04:18:08.057 --> 04:18:11.692
capacity constraint because they clearly identified they've been doing it within

04:18:11.804 --> 04:18:15.794
the policy bounds without violating it since 2019.

04:18:16.371 --> 04:18:19.978
And further, that we actually have the permit to to take water from

04:18:20.058 --> 04:18:23.831
the province. So I see no legal bounds, and I

04:18:24.331 --> 04:18:27.701
have spoken to other lawyers on this to get their perspective.

04:18:27.781 --> 04:18:31.185
I see no legal bounds that has actually stopped this. Um,

04:18:31.635 --> 04:18:35.569
so me trying to advocate that we just don't

04:18:35.633 --> 04:18:39.342
blatantly start taking precedent of rescinding policies without having bigger

04:18:39.842 --> 04:18:43.164
framework around them, um, is something that I'm trying to do

04:18:43.196 --> 04:18:47.065
on a good governance standard. While also maintaining the ability for

04:18:47.113 --> 04:18:50.726
us to be able to proceed with development. I do continue to have

04:18:51.226 --> 04:18:55.126
the questions of why this was not acted on before, as we've already been taking

04:18:55.158 --> 04:18:58.690
this water and was an immediate solution that could

04:18:59.190 --> 04:19:03.074
be done. And suddenly a policy from 1980 becomes

04:19:03.186 --> 04:19:06.478
the barrier that it hasn't been able to be proceeded with.

04:19:07.586 --> 04:19:11.102
I still will be supporting my amendment. And,

04:19:11.679 --> 04:19:15.563
um, can appreciate some of you clearly aren't there. Okay,

04:19:15.772 --> 04:19:18.949
I'm going to, um— it's been moved and seconded. We are now moving,

04:19:19.543 --> 04:19:22.640
um, voting on the amendment. Is everyone clear

04:19:22.849 --> 04:19:25.112
what the amendment is? Oh,

04:19:25.497 --> 04:19:29.413
it's— okay,

04:19:30.231 --> 04:19:33.409
Councillor Harris. I just want to make sure you

04:19:33.425 --> 04:19:36.795
give— like, we've had some people transition online

04:19:37.295 --> 04:19:41.979
and I'm just wondering if we can make sure we kind of— clerks can let

04:19:42.479 --> 04:19:45.687
them know that we're going to be voting, etc., etc., give them a heads up.

04:19:45.783 --> 04:19:47.332
So then I'll, I'll go back to you.

04:19:56.125 --> 04:19:58.154
So currently we have Councillors McCabe,

04:19:59.096 --> 04:20:01.677
Rabanovich, and Fountain. I'm online.

04:20:04.171 --> 04:20:07.607
This is Dorothy. I'm online. Sandy's online

04:20:07.639 --> 04:20:09.245
too? No.

04:20:11.188 --> 04:20:12.681
So it's Dorothy, Barry, and Sue.

04:20:20.645 --> 04:20:24.049
So, Councillor Huinink, you'd like us to read the amendment? Uh,

04:20:24.162 --> 04:20:27.164
what was just changed? Sure.

04:20:28.352 --> 04:20:31.964
You were not in the room when it was posted. Do you want just the

04:20:32.464 --> 04:20:33.297
changes or do you want the whole thing?

04:20:41.500 --> 04:20:45.048
You're okay? Okay, thank you. So we're voting on the

04:20:45.193 --> 04:20:49.222
amendment to the motion. Please use eScribe

04:20:49.286 --> 04:20:52.930
to vote. Here,

04:20:52.979 --> 04:20:55.965
Redmond, I can't use eScribe, but, uh, I'm, uh,

04:20:56.190 --> 04:21:00.782
opposed to the— thank

04:21:01.282 --> 04:21:01.681
you, Councillor Vovanovic.

04:21:26.858 --> 04:21:30.230
I'm waiting for it to pop up online, but I don't— on eScribe,

04:21:30.246 --> 04:21:33.473
but I don't see it. Councilman— okay,

04:21:34.501 --> 04:21:38.242
do you want to just verbally tell us? Yeah, I'm, I'm opposed.

04:21:38.499 --> 04:21:39.655
Thank you. Thank you.

04:21:56.739 --> 04:21:58.200
Colleagues, that is lost.

04:22:00.416 --> 04:22:03.611
Councillor Harris. I'd like to move the staff recommendation.

04:22:11.832 --> 04:22:15.267
Ah, I see. Okay, so we're now back to the deferred

04:22:15.363 --> 04:22:18.990
motion that was on the agenda. Given the result of the amendment, does the

04:22:19.490 --> 04:22:21.911
move— do the mover and seconder still want us to vote on that one?

04:22:22.826 --> 04:22:26.437
Okay, so it will now be on the motion that

04:22:26.937 --> 04:22:28.090
has been referred twice,

04:22:30.289 --> 04:22:30.819
unamended.

04:22:36.147 --> 04:22:39.518
Madame Chair, I'm opposed to it. Thank you, Councillor Vrbanovic.

04:22:43.918 --> 04:22:48.205
Councillor McCabe, we're voting

04:22:48.253 --> 04:22:51.770
on the staff recommendation? No, we're now voting on the unamended

04:22:52.107 --> 04:22:55.639
motion by Councillor Salonen and Gowing as it was

04:22:56.139 --> 04:22:58.802
originally. I'm opposed. Thank you.

04:23:00.938 --> 04:23:01.275
There we go.

04:23:06.653 --> 04:23:09.399
Colleagues, that is defeated. Uh, now Councillor Harris.

04:23:10.651 --> 04:23:14.054
Yeah, move this, uh, staff recommendation, please. It's been moved by

04:23:14.554 --> 04:23:18.453
staff, seconded— or it's been moved by Councilor Harris, seconded by Councilor

04:23:18.953 --> 04:23:22.900
Erb. Please use your eScribe to vote, and we are voting on rescinding

04:23:23.590 --> 04:23:27.025
the 1980 agreement on water taking from Wilmot.

04:23:28.486 --> 04:23:31.778
We are just— um, Sorry, I'm in favor.

04:23:32.323 --> 04:23:35.679
Thank you. So we're not allowed to speak to the motion?

04:24:09.816 --> 04:24:10.699
Councilor McCabe.

04:24:13.509 --> 04:24:16.174
I'm in favor of the staff recommendation. Thank you.

04:24:39.050 --> 04:24:41.185
Sorry, technology is just catching up, guys.

04:24:45.390 --> 04:24:48.200
Colleagues, that, that motion is carried.

04:24:50.672 --> 04:24:52.278
E-Scribe is just not cooperating.

04:24:54.478 --> 04:24:57.064
So colleagues, oh,

04:24:57.819 --> 04:25:00.789
I'm going to look for consensus here. We can, um,

04:25:00.886 --> 04:25:03.648
forego our closed portion of this meeting,

04:25:04.644 --> 04:25:07.936
um, if I have agreement that everybody— and we will, uh,

04:25:07.952 --> 04:25:11.645
deal with those issues at a future meeting. They're not time sensitive. Everybody's good with

04:25:12.145 --> 04:25:15.917
that? Okay, I am seeing consensus

04:25:16.045 --> 04:25:19.480
for for, uh, not holding our closed meeting of

04:25:19.512 --> 04:25:20.315
today's date.

04:25:30.490 --> 04:25:36.589
Okay, so we

04:25:37.089 --> 04:25:40.313
have correspondence that we've received, 7.1, 7.2.

04:25:41.164 --> 04:25:44.871
Um, I now need a mover and a seconder to enact bylaws for second

04:25:45.371 --> 04:25:48.901
and third readings. Moved by Councilor Wolf,

04:25:48.949 --> 04:25:52.418
second by Councilor Nowak. I'll please you, you scribe, to vote.

04:25:53.204 --> 04:25:56.512
And you're voting that the bylaw to confirm the proceedings of the council meeting of

04:25:57.012 --> 04:25:59.996
Regional Municipality of Waterloo on April 8th,

04:26:00.045 --> 04:26:03.336
2026, be read first, second, and third time, finally passed,

04:26:03.352 --> 04:26:06.869
and numbered 26-015, and be signed by

04:26:07.369 --> 04:26:10.192
the regional chair and the regional clerk and sealed with the regional seal.

04:26:26.042 --> 04:26:29.054
So colleagues, that is carried. Um,

04:26:29.151 --> 04:26:32.772
if I can have a mover and a seconder to adjourn. Moved by Councillor

04:26:32.901 --> 04:26:37.932
Wolf, seconded by Councillor Nowak. Do I have consen—

04:26:38.432 --> 04:26:40.746
consensus to adjourn. That is unanimous, colleagues.

04:26:41.822 --> 04:26:44.872
Good debate, great information. Thank you, Madam Chair.

04:26:45.097 --> 04:26:48.325
Just a quick note, uh, Ron Eddy passed away

04:26:48.825 --> 04:26:52.403
